= Cosmology
{wiki}

= Cosmological particle freeze-out
{parent=Cosmology}

Decoupling of a particle abundance from chemical equilibrium when interactions become too slow compared with cosmic expansion. Pair annihilation can continue after equilibrium production is negligible, leaving a nonzero relic abundance.

= Post-freeze-out relic abundance
{parent=Cosmological particle freeze-out}
{title2=$Y_\infty$}

For $dY/dx=-\lambda Y^2/x^2$ after freeze-out, the exact limit is $Y_\infty=[Y_f^{-1}+\lambda/x_f]^{-1}$. The frequently used estimate $x_f/\lambda$ additionally neglects the initial inverse abundance.

= Spatial homogeneity
{parent=Cosmology}

Relative physical laws and large-scale statistical properties do not depend on the chosen spatial origin. For a continuous deterministic galaxy velocity field, using the same relative-velocity rule from each origin forces additivity and hence a position-independent linear velocity gradient.

= Cosmological shear
{parent=Spatial homogeneity}

The traceless symmetric part of the galaxy velocity-gradient <matrix> changes shape without isotropic expansion. An antisymmetric part instead represents rigid rotation. A <matrix> proportional to the identity plus an antisymmetric <matrix> has no shear.

= Nonlinear cosmological perturbation theory
{parent=Cosmology}

Nonlinear cosmological perturbations allow finite fluctuations of the spatial scale, density and other fields. Expanding in spatial gradients can remain useful even when a linear expansion in their amplitudes is invalid. The <long-wavelength approximation in cosmology> is one such organization of the equations.

= Long-wavelength approximation in cosmology
{parent=Nonlinear cosmological perturbation theory}
{title2=$k/(aH)\ll1$}

= Cosmological long-wavelength approximation
{synonym}

When physical wavelengths greatly exceed the <Hubble radius>, spatial derivatives are small compared with temporal expansion scales. A cosmological gradient expansion then approximates neighbouring regions as locally homogeneous universes, while retaining finite perturbation amplitudes. This does not alone discard frozen <tensor cosmological perturbations>.

= Nonlinear curvature covector
{parent=Long-wavelength approximation in cosmology}
{title2=$\zeta_i=-\partial_i\log a+(H/\Pi)\partial_i\phi$}

= Separate-universe curvature covector
{synonym}

With negligible shear, a <minimally coupled scalar field> serving as a clock, and $\Pi\ne0$, the <Hamiltonian constraint> and <momentum constraint> imply $\dot H=-4\pi GN\Pi^2$ and $\partial_iH=-4\pi G\Pi\partial_i\phi$. Differentiating the local Friedmann relation yields $\dot\Pi=-N(3H\Pi+V_{,\phi})$ and $\partial_i\Pi=-(3H+V_{,\phi}/\Pi)\partial_i\phi$. Inserting these identities into $\dot\zeta_i$ cancels all terms, including gradients of the <lapse function>. The conserved covector becomes the gradient of a <uniform-density curvature perturbation> at linear adiabatic order. Dropping the shear constraint is essential; this is not a proof of conservation in an arbitrary non-attractor phase.

= Nonlinear scalar metric in a cosmological gradient expansion
{parent=Long-wavelength approximation in cosmology}
{title2=$h_{ij}=a_{\mathrm{bg}}^2e^{2\zeta}\delta_{ij}$}

With vectors and independent tensors neglected, finite <scalar cosmological perturbations> of the local spatial scale can be encoded by $h_{ij}=a_{\mathrm{bg}}^2e^{2\zeta}\delta_{ij}$. Scalar lapse and shift potentials complete its <3+1 decomposition of spacetime>. The exact shift-square term is $h^{ij}N_iN_j$, independently of the parametrization of the scalar shift.

= Inflationary shear damping
{parent=Long-wavelength approximation in cosmology}
{title2=$\widetilde K^i{}_j\propto a_{\mathrm{loc}}^{-3}$}

The traceless mixed <extrinsic curvature of a spatial hypersurface> can have leading solution $\widetilde K^i{}_j=C^i{}_j(\mathbf x)a_{\mathrm{loc}}^{-3}$. <Cosmic inflation> suppresses this anisotropic expansion. An anisotropic spatial shape can nevertheless freeze to a nonzero constant, so shear damping does not prove the absence of <tensor cosmological perturbations>.

= Baryogenesis
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki}

Baryogenesis is the dynamical production of the cosmic excess of baryons over antibaryons. It requires baryon-number violation, C and CP violation, and departure from thermal equilibrium, or an appropriate generalization of these Sakharov conditions.

= Sakharov conditions
{parent=Baryogenesis}
{c}

The standard conditions for <baryogenesis> from a symmetric initial ensemble are <baryon number> violation, both <C violation> and <CP violation>, and departure from <thermal equilibrium>. Conserved <baryon number> cannot develop a net value from zero; an exact conjugation symmetry pairs production with equal antiproduction. In <thermal equilibrium>, <CPT symmetry> makes the equilibrium expectation of net <baryon number> vanish for zero imposed <chemical potentials> for charges odd under <CPT symmetry>. These conditions assume the usual local quantum-field framework and do not alone guarantee a large asymmetry.

= CP rate cancellation in baryogenesis
{c}
{parent=Sakharov conditions}

In a symmetric initial population of particles and antiparticles, exact <CP symmetry> equates conjugate decay rates after summing final momenta and helicities. Since <parity> does not reverse baryon number, this equality prevents a total baryon asymmetry even if <charge conjugation> alone is violated. Different same-helicity rates can cancel against the opposite-helicity rates.

= Cosmological perfect-fluid continuity equation
{parent=Cosmology}

For a homogeneous fluid of energy density $\rho$, pressure $P$, and scale factor $a(t)$, adiabatic work in a comoving volume gives
$$
\dot\rho+3\frac{\dot a}{a}(\rho+P)=0.
$$

= Separately conserved cosmological fluids
{parent=Cosmological perfect-fluid continuity equation}

A noninteracting <perfect fluid in general relativity> has $\nabla_\mu T_i^{\mu\nu}=0$, so $\dot\rho_i+3H(\rho_i+P_i)=0$. If energy is transferred between species, source terms $Q_i$ appear and only their sum vanishes. The <Friedmann equation> still involves the sum of all component stress tensors because all components share one spacetime metric.

= Barotropic equation of state
{parent=Cosmological perfect-fluid continuity equation}

A constant barotropic equation of state has $P=w\rho$. The cases $w=0$ and $w=1/3$ describe pressureless matter and radiation respectively.

= Coasting fluid
{parent=Barotropic equation of state}
{title2=$w=-\tfrac13,\quad\rho\propto a^{-2}$}

A <coasting fluid> has pressure $p=-\rho/3$. The <cosmological perfect-fluid continuity equation> makes its density scale as $a^{-2}$. If it alone fills a flat expanding universe, the <scale factor> grows linearly in physical time. Its background density has the same scale-factor dependence as a spatial-curvature term, although the fluid does not itself create spatial curvature.

= Cosmological domain wall network
{parent=Barotropic equation of state}

An isotropic, noninteracting network of <domain walls> with energy density proportional to $a^{-1}$ has effective <equation-of-state parameter> $w=-2/3$. This follows from <constant-equation-of-state density scaling> $\rho\propto a^{-3(1+w)}$.

= Constant-equation-of-state density scaling
{parent=Barotropic equation of state}

For constant $w\ne-1$, the cosmological continuity equation integrates to
$$
\rho(a)=\rho_0a^{-3(1+w)}
$$
when $a=1$ at the reference time and $\rho_0$ is the reference density.

= Flat constant-equation-of-state scale factor
{parent=Constant-equation-of-state density scaling}

For an expanding spatially flat universe with constant $w>-1$ and $a(t_0)=1$,
$$
a(t)=\left[1+\frac32(1+w)H_0(t-t_0)\right]^{2/[3(1+w)]}.
$$
It vanishes at the finite past time $t_*=t_0-2/[3(1+w)H_0]$.

= Pressureless matter
{parent=Barotropic equation of state}
{wiki}

Pressureless matter has equation-of-state parameter $w=0$, energy density $\rho\propto a^{-3}$ and negligible stress compared with its rest-mass density.

= Cosmic string network
{parent=Barotropic equation of state}
{wiki=Cosmic_string}

An isotropic network of long cosmic strings has average equation-of-state parameter $w=-1/3$ and energy density proportional to $a^{-2}$.

= Coasting cosmic-string universe
{parent=Cosmic string network}
{title2=$a(t)=B(t-t_B)$}

An isotropic <cosmic string network> with $\rho=C/a^2$ has <equation-of-state parameter> $w=-1/3$. The <Friedmann equation> gives $\dot a^2=8\pi GC/3-K$. On the expanding branch with positive right side, $a=B(t-t_B)$, the <comoving Hubble radius> is constant, and the <cosmological density parameter> is constant. There is no growing <flatness problem>, but flatness is not an attractor. If this exact coasting solution extends all the way to $t_B$, then $\int_{t_B}^t dt'/a(t')$ diverges and there is no finite <particle horizon>. A finite starting time changes that global conclusion.

= Static closed cosmic-string universe
{title2=$\rho_s a^2=3K/(8\pi G)$}
{parent=Cosmic string network}

A closed universe containing only a <cosmic string network> can remain static. Its <Friedmann acceleration equation> vanishes identically because $P_s=-\rho_s/3$, and its <Friedmann equation> requires $\rho_s a^2=3K/(8\pi G)$. Once this conserved normalization is tuned, every positive radius gives a static solution. The usual Hubble-normalized <cosmological density parameter> is undefined at a static epoch.

= Homogeneous stability of a static cosmic-string universe
{parent=Static closed cosmic-string universe}

At fixed string normalization $C=\rho_sa^2$, scale-factor shifts move between static solutions and are neutral, with no restoring force. The acceleration equation gives $\ddot a=0$, but the Friedmann constraint also imposes $\dot a^2=8\pi GC/3-K$. At the static normalization it excludes a nonzero velocity. A slightly larger $C$ admits linearly expanding or contracting branches; a smaller $C$ admits no real closed solution. This distinguishes permitted perturbations from constraint violations.

= Radiation--cosmic-string Friedmann solution
{parent=Cosmic string network}

Consider a spatially flat universe containing <radiation in cosmology>[radiation] and a <cosmic string network>. If their densities are both $\rho_{\rm eq}$ at scale factor $a_{\rm eq}$, then
$$
\rho_r=\rho_{\rm eq}\left(\frac{a_{\rm eq}}a\right)^4,
\qquad
\rho_s=\rho_{\rm eq}\left(\frac{a_{\rm eq}}a\right)^2.
$$
In <conformal time> $d\tau=dt/a$, the flat <Friedmann equation> becomes
$$
\left(\frac{da}{d\tau}\right)^2
=\frac{8\pi G a_{\rm eq}^2}{3c^2}\rho_{\rm eq}
(a^2+a_{\rm eq}^2).
$$
Choosing the Big Bang at $\tau=0$, its expanding solution is
$$
a(\tau)=a_{\rm eq}\sinh\!\left(
a_{\rm eq}\sqrt{\frac{8\pi G\rho_{\rm eq}}{3c^2}}\,\tau
\right).
$$

= Critical density
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki=Critical_density}

At Hubble parameter $H$, the critical energy density is
$$
\rho_{\rm crit}=\frac{3c^2H^2}{8\pi G}.
$$

= Cosmological density parameter
{title2=$\Omega$}
{parent=Critical density}
{wiki=Density_parameter}

The density parameter is $\Omega=\rho/\rho_{\rm crit}$. In a Friedmann universe, its departure from one measures the spatial-curvature term relative to the density or expansion term.

= Cosmic baryon fraction
{parent=Cosmological density parameter}
{title2=$f_b=\Omega_b/\Omega_m$}

The <cosmic baryon fraction> is the ratio of mean baryonic matter density to total matter density. Assigning this fraction to a halo is a simplifying assumption; cooling, ejection and other baryonic processes can change the halo's retained gas and stellar fractions. A baryon conversion efficiency becomes a stellar-to-total-halo mass ratio only after multiplying by the applicable baryon fraction.

= Cosmological density parameter flow
{parent=Cosmological density parameter}
{title2=$d\Omega/d\log a$}

For an expanding <Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric> with positive <energy density>, put $\Omega=8\pi G\rho/(3H^2)$ and $w=P/\rho$, in units $c=1$. The <Friedmann equation>, <Friedmann acceleration equation> and <cosmological perfect-fluid continuity equation> give
$$
\frac{\dot H}{H^2}=-1-\frac12(1+3w)\Omega,\qquad
\frac{d\Omega}{d\log a}=(1+3w)\Omega(\Omega-1).
$$
For constant $w$, linearizing at $\Omega=1$ gives $\Omega-1\propto a^{1+3w}$. For variable $w$, replace the power by $\exp\int(1+3w(a))\,d\log a$. This describes the <flatness problem> for decelerating expansion, away from epochs at which $H=0$.

= Flatness problem
{c}
{parent=Cosmological density parameter}
{wiki=Flatness_problem}

In a decelerating expanding universe, $|\Omega-1|$ grows, so its small present value requires extremely fine-tuned early initial data unless an earlier mechanism drives $\Omega$ toward one.

= Inflationary solution of the flatness problem
{parent=Flatness problem}

The continuity equation gives
$$
\frac d{dt}(\rho a^2)=-Ha^2(\rho+3P).
$$
During expansion with $\rho+3P<0$, the product $\rho a^2$ grows and $|\Omega^{-1}-1|\propto(\rho a^2)^{-1}$ decreases.

= Horizon problem
{c}
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki=Horizon_problem}

In a decelerating hot Big Bang model, widely separated regions of the observable universe have nearly equal conditions despite having no shared past light cone. Inflation supplies an earlier era with a shrinking comoving Hubble radius, allowing a formerly causal patch to grow beyond the later horizon.

= Particle horizon
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki}

The particle horizon at cosmic time $t$ is the greatest comoving distance from which a light signal could have reached an observer since the initial time:
$$
\chi_{\rm ph}(t)=\int_{t_{\rm initial}}^t\frac{c\,dt'}{a(t')}.
$$
Regions separated by more than twice this distance cannot have exchanged signals by time $t$.

= Open radiation universe particle horizon
{parent=Particle horizon}
{title2=$d_{h0}=\frac{\operatorname{arsinh}\sqrt{(1-\Omega_0)/\Omega_0}}{H_0\sqrt{1-\Omega_0}}$}

For a radiation-filled expanding universe with $0<\Omega_0<1$ and no <cosmological constant>, set $x=a/a_0$. The <particle horizon> radial proper distance is $H_0^{-1}\int_0^1[\Omega_0+(1-\Omega_0)x^2]^{-1/2}\,dx$. An inverse-hyperbolic-sine antiderivative gives the formula. The spatially flat limit is $d_{h0}=H_0^{-1}$; radial proper distance is distinct from the area radius of the corresponding horizon sphere.

= Horizon angle at matter-radiation equality
{parent=Particle horizon}
{title2=$\theta_{\rm eq}\simeq\tau_{\rm eq}/(\tau_0-\tau_{\rm eq})$}

A horizon-length patch on the <matter-radiation equality> surface has physical size $a_{\rm eq}\tau_{\rm eq}$ in a flat universe with <conformal time> measured from the <Big Bang>. Dividing by its <angular diameter distance> $a_{\rm eq}(\tau_0-\tau_{\rm eq})$ gives its small angular size. For the <radiation-matter scale factor in conformal time>, this is $(\sqrt2-1)\sqrt{\Omega_{r,0}}/[1-\sqrt{2\Omega_{r,0}}]$. A patch diameter is twice the angle associated with one horizon radius. The <particle horizon> is an integral over past evolution and must not be silently replaced by the instantaneous <Hubble radius>.

= Radiation in cosmology
{parent=Cosmology}

Cosmological radiation has equation of state $P_r=\rho_r/3$ and density scaling $\rho_r\propto a^{-4}$.

= Effective number of relativistic energy degrees of freedom
{title2=$g_*$}
{parent=Radiation in cosmology}

For relativistic species in thermal equilibrium at a common temperature $T$, the total energy density can be written
$$
\epsilon=\frac{4\pi^5}{15(hc)^3}g_*(k_BT)^4,
\qquad
g_*=\sum_{\rm bosons}g_i+\frac78\sum_{\rm fermions}g_i.
$$
The factor $7/8$ is the <fermion-to-boson thermal integral ratio> for energy density.

= Radiation mass density
{parent=Effective number of relativistic energy degrees of freedom}
{title2=$\rho_{\mathrm{rad}}$}

For relativistic species at a common temperature, the mass-equivalent energy density is $\rho=\pi^2g_*(k_BT)^4/(30\hbar^3c^5)$. Using mass rather than energy density accounts for the powers of $c$ in the <Friedmann equation>.

= Dark energy
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki}

Dark energy is a smooth component with sufficiently negative pressure to drive accelerated cosmic expansion.

= Dynamical dark energy
{parent=Dark energy}

Dynamical dark energy has an energy density or equation-of-state parameter that changes with cosmic time, unlike a cosmological constant.

= Variable dark-energy equation of state
{title2=$w(z)$}
{parent=Dynamical dark energy}

For $P_{\rm DE}=w(z)\rho_{\rm DE}$, the continuity equation gives
$$
\rho_{\rm DE}(z)=\rho_{{\rm DE},0}(1+z)^3
\exp\left(3\int_0^z\frac{w(z')}{1+z'}\,dz'\right).
$$

= Chevallier-Polarski-Linder parametrization
{c}
{title2=$w(a)=w_0+w_a(1-a)$}
{parent=Variable dark-energy equation of state}
{wiki}

The Chevallier-Polarski-Linder parametrization models dynamical dark energy by $w(a)=w_0+w_a(1-a)$, equivalently $w(z)=w_0+w_az/(1+z)$.

= Cosmological constant
{parent=Dark energy}
{title2=$\Lambda$}
{wiki}

= Cosmological constant energy
{synonym}

The cosmological constant $\Lambda$ is the constant term proportional to the metric in the <Einstein field equations>. Interpreted as vacuum energy, it has $P_\Lambda=-\rho_\Lambda$, so its energy density is constant during expansion.

= Matter density
{title2=$\rho_m$}
{parent=Cosmology}

The matter density $\rho_m$ is the <energy density> in nonrelativistic matter. In a homogeneous expanding universe, conservation of particle number and dilution of physical volume give $\rho_m\propto a^{-3}$.

= Big Crunch
{parent=Cosmology}
{c}
{wiki}

A Big Crunch is a future finite-time singularity in which a recollapsing universe reaches vanishing scale factor.

= Cosmic inflation
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki=Inflation_(cosmology)}

Cosmic inflation is an early period of accelerated expansion, often modeled by a scalar field whose potential energy dominates its kinetic energy.

= de Sitter approximation
{c}
{parent=Cosmic inflation}
{title2=$a(\tau)\simeq-1/(H\tau)$}

Approximately constant positive <Hubble parameter> gives a nearly <de Sitter spacetime> expansion in flat slicing. Its scale factor can be approximated by $a=-1/(H\tau)$ with negative <conformal time> increasing toward zero. Slow variation of the expansion rate limits the accuracy of this approximation.

= Reheating
{parent=Cosmic inflation}

Reheating transfers the <inflaton> energy into other particles after <cosmic inflation>, eventually establishing a hot thermal bath. It requires interactions in addition to a background <scalar potential>. The duration and averaged <equation-of-state parameter> of reheating affect the <number of e-folds> between a chosen scale's horizon exit and the end of inflation.

= Reheating temperature
{parent=Reheating}
{title2=$T_R$}

The reheating temperature characterizes the thermal radiation bath after energy from a nonthermal component has been transferred and thermalized. For <sudden decay of a dominant nonrelativistic relic>, $T_R\simeq[3M_{\rm Pl}^2\Gamma^2/(c_2g_\star)]^{1/4}$. It differs from the residual radiation temperature immediately before decay.

= Sudden decay of a dominant nonrelativistic relic
{parent=Reheating}
{title2=$\rho_\phi\simeq3M_{\rm Pl}^2\Gamma^2$}

The sudden-decay approximation converts a dominant cold relic's rest-mass density into radiation at $H\simeq\Gamma$, neglecting expansion during the conversion. Its pre-decay radiation temperature obeys $T_-^3\simeq3M_{\rm Pl}^2\Gamma^2/(c_1g_\star Ym)$, while the <reheating temperature> is set by the post-decay radiation energy density. Constant relativistic counts and prompt thermalization are additional assumptions.

= Entropy production by decay of a dominant relic
{parent=Sudden decay of a dominant nonrelativistic relic}
{title2=$s_+/s_-=(1+\rho_\phi/\rho_r)^{3/4}$}

At fixed <scale factor> and unchanged relativistic counts, instantaneous conversion of a relic into thermal radiation gives the displayed entropy-density enhancement. It exceeds one because the gas receives energy from outside its prior equilibrium sector. This does not contradict <cosmological entropy conservation>, whose source-free reversible assumptions fail during decay and thermalization.

= Number of e-folds
{title2=$N=\log(a/a_i)$}
{parent=Cosmic inflation}

The number of e-folds during an interval of expansion is the logarithmic growth of the <scale factor>:
$$
N=\log\frac{a_f}{a_i}=\int_{t_i}^{t_f}H\,dt.
$$

= Primordial perturbation
{parent=Cosmic inflation}

A primordial perturbation is an early-universe fluctuation that seeds later cosmic microwave background anisotropy and large-scale structure. Inflation generates scalar curvature perturbations and tensor perturbations from quantum fluctuations.

= Dimensionless cosmological power spectrum
{parent=Primordial perturbation}
{title2=$\Delta_X=k^3P_X/(2\pi^2)$}

= Dimensionless power spectrum
{synonym}

The dimensionless spectrum is the contribution to a statistically isotropic field variance per logarithmic <wavenumber> interval: $\langle X^2\rangle=\int d\log k\,\Delta_X(k)$, with any necessary smoothing or regulators. Some conventions denote this same quantity $\Delta_X^2$. The definition, not the superscript convention, fixes its meaning.

= Dimensional and dimensionless matter spectra across equality
{parent=Dimensionless cosmological power spectrum}
{title2=$\mathscr P(k)=k^3P(k)/(2\pi^2)$}

The dimensionless spectrum is $\mathscr P(k)=k^3P(k)/(2\pi^2)$. If it rises as $k^4$ below equality and as $\ln^2k$ above equality, then the dimensional spectrum rises as $k$ and falls as $k^{-3}\ln^2k$. Thus the dimensional spectrum has a turnover while the dimensionless spectrum only bends to a much slower rise. A schematic transition must smoothly join the two asymptotic regimes.

= Inflaton
{parent=Cosmic inflation}
{wiki}

A canonical homogeneous inflaton $\phi(t)$ has
$$
\rho=\frac12\dot\phi^2+V(\phi),
\qquad
P=\frac12\dot\phi^2-V(\phi),
$$
and obeys $\ddot\phi+3H\dot\phi+V'(\phi)=0$.

= Inflaton equation of motion
{parent=Inflaton}

For a homogeneous canonical inflaton in an expanding <FLRW metric>,
$$
\ddot\phi+3H\dot\phi+V'(\phi)=0.
$$
The expansion contributes the damping term called <Hubble friction>.

= Frozen scalar fields and the Friedmann constraint
{parent=Inflaton equation of motion}

For a homogeneous canonical <scalar field>, the <cosmological perfect-fluid continuity equation> reduces to $\dot\phi(\ddot\phi+3H\dot\phi+V')=0$. It implies the <inflaton equation of motion> wherever $\dot\phi\ne0$, and by continuity at isolated turning points. It cannot by itself exclude a frozen field on a time interval. Indeed, choose $\phi=\phi_0$ with $V(\phi_0)>0$ but $V'(\phi_0)\ne0$; a <de Sitter scale factor in flat slicing> with $H^2=V(\phi_0)/(3M_{\rm Pl}^2)$ satisfies the homogeneous <Friedmann equation> and <Friedmann acceleration equation>, but not the scalar equation. Varying the <inflaton action in an expanding universe> supplies the missing equation also for a frozen field, requiring $V'(\phi_0)=0$.

= Exact hyperbolic-sine scalar-field cosmology
{parent=Inflaton}

In a flat universe containing a homogeneous canonical <scalar field>, the ansatz
$$
\phi(t)=\phi_0\log\tanh(\lambda t)
$$
has the exact solution
$$
H=\lambda\frac{\phi_0^2}{M_{\rm Pl}^2}\coth(2\lambda t),
\qquad
a=a_0[\sinh(2\lambda t)]^{\phi_0^2/(2M_{\rm Pl}^2)}.
$$
It interpolates between decelerating power-law expansion at early times and exponential accelerated expansion at late times when $2/3<\phi_0^2/M_{\rm Pl}^2<2$.

= Inflaton action in an expanding universe
{parent=Inflaton}

For a canonical scalar field in a spatially flat expanding universe,
$$
S[\phi]=\int d^3x\,dt\,a^3
\left[
\frac12\dot\phi^2
-\frac{c^2}{2a^2}|\nabla\phi|^2
-V(\phi)
\right].
$$
The field <Euler-Lagrange equation> is
$$
\ddot\phi+3H\dot\phi-\frac{c^2}{a^2}\nabla^2\phi+V'(\phi)=0.
$$

= Inflationary scalar Fourier mode
{parent=Inflaton action in an expanding universe}

For nearly constant inflaton potential and constant $H_{\rm inf}$, the spatial <Fourier transform> obeys
$$
\ddot{\widehat\phi}_{\mathbf k}
+3H_{\rm inf}\dot{\widehat\phi}_{\mathbf k}
+\frac{c^2k^2}{a^2}\widehat\phi_{\mathbf k}=0.
$$

= Canonically rescaled de Sitter scalar mode
{c}
{parent=Inflationary scalar Fourier mode}

For $a=-1/(H_{\rm inf}\tau)$, the rescaled mode
$$
\widetilde\phi_{\mathbf k}=a\widehat\phi_{\mathbf k}
$$
satisfies
$$
\widetilde\phi_{\mathbf k}''
+\left(c^2k^2-\frac2{\tau^2}\right)
\widetilde\phi_{\mathbf k}=0.
$$

= Conformal-time quadratic action for an inflaton perturbation
{title2=$S^{(2)}=\tfrac12\int d\tau\,d^3x\{(f')^2-(\nabla f)^2+(a''/a-a^2V'')f^2\}$}
{parent=Canonically rescaled de Sitter scalar mode}

Neglecting metric perturbations, the rescaling $f=a\delta\phi$ removes the expansion friction from the scalar perturbation action after a boundary term is discarded. Its canonical momentum is $f'$, and each real Fourier oscillator has time-dependent squared frequency $k^2+a^2V''-a''/a$. The oscillator can become inverted on <superhorizon scales>; the canonical commutation relations still apply.

= Bunch-Davies vacuum
{c}
{parent=Canonically rescaled de Sitter scalar mode}
{wiki=Bunch–Davies_vacuum}

The Bunch-Davies vacuum selects the mode that approaches the positive-frequency ground-state mode of angular frequency $ck$ in the far past $\tau\to-\infty$.

= Cosmological horizon exit
{parent=Bunch-Davies vacuum}

A Fourier mode exits the Hubble horizon during inflation when $k=aH$. On superhorizon scales $k\ll aH$, the growing mode of a sufficiently light scalar perturbation approaches a constant.

= Superhorizon scale
{parent=Cosmological horizon exit}

= Super-Hubble scale
{synonym}

A cosmological mode is on a superhorizon scale when its physical wavenumber is smaller than the Hubble expansion rate, $k/a\ll H$, equivalently $k\ll aH$.

= Scale-invariant inflationary power spectrum
{parent=Bunch-Davies vacuum}

A Fourier-space variance $P_k$ in three dimensions is scale invariant when the dimensionless power per logarithmic wavenumber interval,
$$
\Delta^2(k)=\frac{k^3P_k}{2\pi^2},
$$
is independent of $k$. For a massless inflaton mode at late times,
$$
P_k\longrightarrow\frac{\hbar H_{\rm inf}^2}{2c^3k^3},
$$
so $\Delta^2(k)$ is constant.

= Infrared qualification of a scale-invariant covariance
{parent=Scale-invariant inflationary power spectrum}
{title2=$C(r)-C(r_0)=-\frac{A}{2\pi^2}\log(r/r_0)$}

An exact $P(k)=A/k^3$ has divergent unsubtracted point <variance>. Its finite isotropic <covariance> difference is the displayed logarithm, unchanged by simultaneous dilation of $r,r_0$. Thus the nonzero-mode scaling statement has a natural subtraction interpretation. Exact dilation invariance of a continuous finite-variance isotropic <covariance> itself would instead force a constant <covariance>, corresponding only to a zero-mode spectral measure. Cutoffs or a zero-mode subtraction must accompany the nontrivial spectrum.

= Equal-time two-point function of a de Sitter scalar
{title2=$P_{\delta\phi}(k,\tau)=H^2(1+k^2\tau^2)/(2k^3)$}
{parent=Scale-invariant inflationary power spectrum}

For a light scalar in the <Bunch-Davies vacuum> with metric perturbations neglected, the mode $e^{-ik\tau}(1-i/(k\tau))/\sqrt{2k}$ gives the displayed equal-time spectrum. The dimensionless power tends to $H^2/(4\pi^2)$ on <superhorizon scales>. The exact massless infinite-volume correlation has an infrared divergence; a finite-volume or finite-duration infrared regulator, and smearing for coincident products, makes the correlators well defined.

= Primordial scalar amplitude
{title2=$A_s$}
{parent=Scale-invariant inflationary power spectrum}

At leading slow-roll order, the dimensionless primordial curvature-perturbation amplitude at horizon exit is
$$
A_s=\frac{H^2}{8\pi^2\epsilon_VM_{\rm Pl}^2}
=\frac{V}{24\pi^2M_{\rm Pl}^4\epsilon_V}.
$$

= Scalar spectral index
{title2=$n_s$}
{parent=Primordial scalar amplitude}
{wiki}

= Spectral tilt
{synonym}

The scalar spectral index describes the departure of the primordial scalar spectrum from scale invariance. To first order in potential slow-roll parameters,
$$
n_s-1\simeq-6\epsilon_V+2\eta_V.
$$

= Scalar spectral index in potential slow-roll parameters
{parent=Scalar spectral index}
{title2=$n_s\simeq1-6\epsilon_V+2\eta_V$}

The leading scalar spectrum is proportional to $V^3/V'^2$. Along a slow-roll trajectory, $d\phi/d\ln a\simeq-M_{\rm pl}^2V'/V$ and $d\ln k/d\ln a=1-\epsilon_H$. Differentiating at horizon exit and keeping first order gives $n_s-1=-6\epsilon_V+2\eta_V$. The derivative-factor correction contributes at second order.

= Slow-roll approximation
{parent=Cosmic inflation}
{wiki}

= Slow-roll
{synonym}

= Slow-roll inflation
{synonym}

= Slow-roll equations
{synonym}

For a canonical scalar field with potential $V(\phi)$, slow roll reduces the field and Friedmann equations to
$$
H^2\simeq\frac{V}{3M_{\rm Pl}^2},
\qquad
3H\dot\phi\simeq-V'.
$$

= Single-field slow-roll inflation
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

Single-field slow-roll inflation uses one canonical scalar clock whose potential evolves slowly enough for the slow-roll parameters to remain small. Its adiabatic long mode produces the <single-field inflation squeezed-limit consistency relation>.

= Starobinsky inflation
{c}
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}
{wiki}

Starobinsky inflation is an inflationary model whose Einstein-frame scalar potential can be written
$$
V(\phi)=V_0\left(1-e^{-\sqrt{2/3}\,\phi/M_{\rm Pl}}\right)^2.
$$
The exponentially flat large-field plateau supports <slow-roll inflation>, while the potential is quadratic near its minimum.

= Hubble slow-roll parameter
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

A Hubble slow-roll parameter measures the fractional change of the Hubble rate or of a preceding slow-roll parameter per e-fold. Small values make the inflationary background evolve adiabatically.

= Potential and Hubble slow-roll parameter relation
{parent=Hubble slow-roll parameter}
{title2=$\eta\simeq4\epsilon_V-2\eta_V$}

For a canonical <inflaton> under the <slow-roll approximation>, $\dot\phi/H\simeq-M_{\rm Pl}^2V'/V$ and $\epsilon\simeq\epsilon_V=(M_{\rm Pl}^2/2)(V'/V)^2$. Differentiating $\log\epsilon_V$ along this trajectory gives
$$
\eta\simeq2\left(\frac{V''}{V'}-\frac{V'}V\right)\frac{\dot\phi}{H}=4\epsilon_V-2\eta_V.
$$
Here $\eta=d\log\epsilon/d\log a$, not the alternate convention $-\ddot\phi/(H\dot\phi)$. The logarithmic definition requires $\epsilon>0$; exact de Sitter expansion is treated as a limit.

= First Hubble slow-roll parameter
{title2=$\epsilon$}
{parent=Hubble slow-roll parameter}

The first Hubble slow-roll parameter is
$$
\epsilon=-\frac{\dot H}{H^2}.
$$
For a canonical inflaton, $\epsilon=\dot\phi^2/(2M_{\rm Pl}^2H^2)\geq0$.

= Second Hubble slow-roll parameter
{title2=$\eta$}
{parent=Hubble slow-roll parameter}

Using the e-fold variable $N=\log a$, define
$$
\eta=\frac{d\log\epsilon}{dN}.
$$

= Comoving curvature perturbation
{title2=$\mathcal R$}
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

The comoving curvature perturbation is the scalar curvature perturbation on hypersurfaces orthogonal to the total matter flow. For single-field adiabatic inflation it is conserved on superhorizon scales.

= Mukhanov-Sasaki variable
{c}
{parent=Comoving curvature perturbation}
{title2=$v=z\mathcal R$}

= Canonical curvature perturbation
{synonym}

For single-field adiabatic inflation, the Mukhanov-Sasaki variable rescales the <comoving curvature perturbation> by $z=a\sqrt{2\epsilon}M_{\rm Pl}$, where $M_{\rm Pl}$ is the <reduced Planck mass>. Its quadratic bulk <action> is $\tfrac12\int[(v')^2-(\nabla v)^2+(z''/z)v^2]$, after a boundary term is removed. A <Fourier transform> mode obeys $v_k''+(k^2-z''/z)v_k=0$ and has canonical <Wronskian normalization>. The mass scale and sound-speed factors depend on the underlying scalar theory; this definition assumes the canonical single-field action and positive slow-roll kinetic coefficient.

= Superhorizon conservation of single-field comoving curvature
{parent=Comoving curvature perturbation}
{title2=$\dot{\mathcal R}\simeq0\quad(k\ll aH)$}

For an adiabatic single-field attractor, gradient terms are negligible well outside the <Hubble radius> and the growing comoving-curvature mode is constant. The separate-universe interpretation identifies it with the difference of local expansion histories. The conservation statement can fail for a non-attractor phase or an independent entropy mode.

= Inflaton clock-shift origin of curvature perturbations
{parent=Comoving curvature perturbation}
{title2=$\mathcal R=H\delta\phi_{\rm flat}/\dot\phi$}

On a single-field attractor, a field fluctuation changes the local point along the background trajectory. The corresponding time shift has magnitude $|\delta\phi/\dot\phi|$, and local expansion converts it into curvature of magnitude $H|\delta\phi/\dot\phi|$. On spatially flat slices a standard sign convention gives $\mathcal R=H\delta\phi_{\rm flat}/\dot\phi$. The vacuum mode amplitude $H/(2\pi)$ then determines the scalar curvature spectrum.

= De Sitter curvature mode function
{parent=Comoving curvature perturbation}
{c}
{title2=$u_k(\tau)$}

For approximately constant $H$ and $\epsilon$, a vacuum-normalized curvature mode is $u_k(\tau)=H(1+ik\tau)e^{-ik\tau}/\sqrt{4\epsilon M_{\mathrm{Pl}}^2k^3}$. Its derivative is $u_k^\prime=Hk^2\tau e^{-ik\tau}/\sqrt{4\epsilon M_{\mathrm{Pl}}^2k^3}$. Ordered unequal-time <Wick contractions> use the complex conjugate at the earlier vertex, consistently with the <vacuum prescription for inflationary in-in integrals>.

= Non-adiabatic pressure perturbation
{title2=$\delta P_{\rm nad}$}
{parent=Comoving curvature perturbation}

The non-adiabatic pressure perturbation is
$$
\delta P_{\rm nad}=\delta P-\frac{\dot{\bar P}}{\dot{\bar\rho}}\delta\rho.
$$
On <superhorizon scales> it sources the evolution of the <comoving curvature perturbation> through a convention-dependent signed relation proportional to $H\delta P_{\rm nad}/(\bar\rho+\bar P)$.

= Cosmological entropy perturbation
{parent=Non-adiabatic pressure perturbation}

= Isocurvature perturbation
{synonym}

A cosmological entropy perturbation changes the relative composition of cosmic fluids while leaving the total curvature initially unchanged. For <cold dark matter> and photons, a common relative entropy variable is
$$
S_{c\gamma}=\delta_c-\frac34\delta_\gamma.
$$

= Slow-roll curvature power spectrum
{title2=$\Delta_{\mathcal R}^2$}
{parent=Comoving curvature perturbation}

At leading order in canonical single-field slow roll and evaluated at horizon exit $k=aH$,
$$
\Delta_{\mathcal R}^2(k)=\frac1{2\epsilon M_{\rm Pl}^2}
\left(\frac{H}{2\pi}\right)^2.
$$

= Inflationary potential normalization and matter power
{parent=Slow-roll curvature power spectrum}
{title2=$V\mapsto A V\ \Longrightarrow\ P_m\mapsto A P_m$}

At a fixed horizon-exit e-fold count and with the later cosmology fixed, rescaling a canonical single-field inflationary potential by a positive constant leaves its potential slow-roll parameters and field solution unchanged. The <slow-roll curvature power spectrum> is proportional to $V/\epsilon_V$, so the <matter power spectrum> scales by the same factor while its leading tilt and transfer-function shape remain unchanged. A changed specified reheating history can also shift the horizon-exit count assigned to a fixed physical wavenumber.

= Scalar spectral index in Hubble slow-roll parameters
{title2=$n_s-1=-2\epsilon-\eta$}
{parent=Slow-roll curvature power spectrum}

Differentiating the curvature spectrum at horizon exit gives
$$
n_s-1=\frac{d\log\Delta_{\mathcal R}^2}{d\log k}
=-2\epsilon-\eta
$$
to first order in Hubble slow-roll parameters.

= Primordial tensor power spectrum
{title2=$\Delta_t^2$}
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

For the two graviton polarizations in canonical single-field inflation,
$$
\Delta_t^2(k)=\frac8{M_{\rm Pl}^2}
\left(\frac{H}{2\pi}\right)^2
$$
at horizon exit.

= Graviton power spectrum
{title2=$P_\gamma(k)$}
{parent=Primordial tensor power spectrum}

The graviton power spectrum is the two-point spectrum of a tensor metric perturbation. A convention may quote it per polarization or summed over both polarizations, so that choice must accompany its normalization.

= Adiabatic tensor mode
{title2=$\gamma_{ij}^{L}$}
{parent=Primordial tensor power spectrum}

An adiabatic tensor mode is a long-wavelength transverse-traceless metric perturbation whose leading local effect can be produced by a <large spatial diffeomorphism>. Its effect on short modes is therefore constrained by a Ward identity.

= Cosmological soft-graviton consistency relation
{parent=Adiabatic tensor mode}

For a single adiabatic long tensor mode, the squeezed scalar-scalar-graviton bispectrum is the anisotropic momentum response of the scalar power spectrum:
$$
\lim_{q\to0}\frac{\langle\gamma^s_{\mathbf q}\varphi_{\mathbf k}
\varphi_{-\mathbf k-\mathbf q}\rangle'}{P_\gamma(q)}
=-\frac12\epsilon_{ij}^s k_i\partial_{k_j}P_\varphi(k).
$$

= Tensor spectral index
{title2=$n_T$}
{parent=Primordial tensor power spectrum}
{wiki}

The tensor spectral index is the logarithmic slope $n_T=d\log\Delta_t^2/d\log k$. Canonical slow-roll inflation gives $n_T=-2\epsilon$ to first order.

= Tensor-to-scalar ratio
{title2=$r$}
{parent=Primordial tensor power spectrum}
{wiki}

The tensor-to-scalar ratio is $r=\Delta_t^2/\Delta_{\mathcal R}^2$. Canonical single-field slow roll gives $r=16\epsilon$.

= Single-field inflation consistency relation
{title2=$r=-8n_T$}
{parent=Tensor-to-scalar ratio}

Combining $r=16\epsilon$ with $n_T=-2\epsilon$ gives the leading-order consistency relation
$$
r=-8n_T.
$$

= Blue primordial spectrum
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

A primordial power spectrum is blue when its dimensionless power increases with wavenumber. Canonical single-field slow roll cannot produce a blue tensor spectrum because $\epsilon\geq0$ implies $n_T\leq0$, but its scalar spectrum can be blue when $\eta<-2\epsilon$.

= Quadratic-potential slow-roll solution
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

For $V(\phi)=m^2\phi^2$ in units where $3H^2\simeq V$ and for positive $\phi$,
$$
\phi(t)=\phi_i-\frac{2m}{\sqrt3}t,
$$
and
$$
a(t)=a_i\exp\left(
\frac{m\phi_i}{\sqrt3}t-\frac{m^2t^2}{3}
\right)
=a_i\exp\left(\frac{\phi_i^2-\phi(t)^2}{4}\right).
$$

= Quadratic-inflation field interval from a wavenumber interval
{parent=Quadratic-potential slow-roll solution}
{title2=$\Delta\Phi/M_{\rm Pl}\simeq2\Delta\log k/\sqrt{4N+2}$}

For a quadratic <inflaton> potential ending at the leading estimate $\epsilon_V=1$, $\Phi/M_{\rm Pl}=\sqrt{4N+2}$. <Cosmological horizon exit> gives $d\log k/dN=-1+\epsilon_H$. A narrow logarithmic interval therefore probes a field span $2\Delta\log k/(\sqrt{4N+2}(1-\epsilon_H))$, to the accuracy of the <slow-roll approximation>. At sixty e-folds, five logarithmic wavenumber units probe about $0.64M_{\rm Pl}$ around a background field near $15.6M_{\rm Pl}$.

= Quadratic-inflation tilt-tensor relation
{parent=Quadratic-potential slow-roll solution}
{title2=$r=4(1-n_s)$}

The quadratic potential has equal <potential slow-roll parameters>, $\epsilon_V=\eta_V=1/(2N+1)$. Consequently $n_s=1-4/(2N+1)$ and $r=16/(2N+1)$ at leading order. The relation $r=4(1-n_s)$ is independent of the <inflaton> mass, so a satisfactory scalar tilt does not by itself guarantee a satisfactory tensor prediction.

= Quadratic-inflation amplitude calibration
{parent=Quadratic-potential slow-roll solution}
{title2=$m/M_{\rm pl}=\sqrt{24\pi^2A_s}/(2N+1)$}

For a quadratic potential with the end set by $\epsilon_V=1$, a mode leaving $N$ e-folds earlier has $\phi_*^2=(4N+2)M_{\rm pl}^2$. Its curvature amplitude is $(m/M_{\rm pl})^2(2N+1)^2/(24\pi^2)$. Thus the scalar amplitude fixes the <inflaton> mass while the tilt and tensor ratio depend only on the exit e-fold count at this order.

= Quartic-potential slow-roll solution
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

For $V(\phi)=\lambda\phi^4/4$ with
$H^2\simeq8\pi GV/(3c^2)$ and positive $\phi$,
$$
\phi(t)=\phi_0
\exp\left[-c\sqrt{\frac{\lambda}{6\pi G}}(t-t_0)\right],
$$
and
$$
a(\phi)=a_0
\exp\left[\frac{\pi G}{c^2}(\phi_0^2-\phi^2)\right].
$$

= Potential slow-roll parameter
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

The potential slow-roll parameter is
$$
\epsilon_V=\frac{M_{\rm Pl}^2}{2}
\left(\frac{V'}V\right)^2.
$$
Under the slow-roll equations, $\dot\phi^2/(2V)\simeq\epsilon_V/3$.

= Second potential slow-roll parameter
{title2=$\eta_V$}
{parent=Potential slow-roll parameter}

The second potential slow-roll parameter is
$$
\eta_V=M_{\rm Pl}^2\frac{V''}{V}.
$$
Slow roll usually requires both $\epsilon_V\ll1$ and $|\eta_V|\ll1$.

= Effective equation of state of an oscillating scalar field
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

Suppose a homogeneous scalar field oscillates much faster than the <Hubble parameter> in a monomial potential $V(\phi)=\lambda|\phi|^n$. The <virial theorem> averaged over one oscillation gives $\langle\dot\phi^2\rangle=n\langle V\rangle$, and hence
$$
\langle w\rangle=\frac{\langle P\rangle}{\langle\rho\rangle}=\frac{n-2}{n+2},
\qquad
\rho\propto a^{-6n/(n+2)}.
$$
A quadratic minimum therefore behaves like <pressureless matter>, whereas a quartic minimum behaves like <radiation in cosmology>.

= Hilltop inflation
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}
{wiki}

Hilltop inflation begins near a local maximum of the inflaton potential. For
$$
V(\phi)=\Lambda^4-\frac12m^2\phi^2,
$$
a positive field rolls away from the maximum at $\phi=0$.

= Quadratic hilltop slow-roll prediction
{parent=Hilltop inflation}

For $V(\phi)=V_0-m^2\phi^2/2$ with $V_0$ dominant,
$$
\eta_V\simeq-\frac{m^2M_{\rm Pl}^2}{V_0},
\qquad
\epsilon_V\simeq\frac12\eta_V^2\frac{\phi^2}{M_{\rm Pl}^2}.
$$
If $\widetilde N$ e-folds remain and $n_s-1\simeq2\eta_V$, then
$$
\phi=\phi_e e^{(n_s-1)\widetilde N/2},
\qquad
r\simeq2(n_s-1)^2\left(\frac{\phi_e}{M_{\rm Pl}}\right)^2e^{(n_s-1)\widetilde N}.
$$

= Monomial inflation potential
{title2=$V(\phi)=\lambda M_{\rm Pl}^4(\phi/M_{\rm Pl})^\alpha$}
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

A monomial inflation potential is a positive power of the inflaton. Its potential slow-roll parameter is $\epsilon_V=\alpha^2M_{\rm Pl}^2/(2\phi^2)$.

= Monomial-inflation curvature amplitude
{parent=Monomial inflation potential}
{title2=$\mathcal P_{\mathcal R}=V/(24\pi^2M_{\rm Pl}^4\epsilon_V)$}

For $V=C_p(\Phi/M_{\rm Pl})^p$ and the leading endpoint estimate $\epsilon_V=1$, the <slow-roll approximation> gives $\Phi_N^2/M_{\rm Pl}^2=2pN+p^2/2$ and $\epsilon_V=p/(4N+p)$. Thus $\mathcal P_{\mathcal R}=C_p(2pN+p^2/2)^{p/2}(4N+p)/(24\pi^2M_{\rm Pl}^4p)$ and the <scalar spectral index> obeys $n_s-1=-2(p+2)/(4N+p)$. The coefficient sets the amplitude, whereas the monomial power and exit e-fold count set the leading tilt. Changing the power without specifying the coefficient leaves the overall normalization undetermined.

= Monomial inflation with a prescribed endpoint
{parent=Monomial inflation potential}
{title2=$\phi_N^2=\phi_e^2+2nNM_{\rm Pl}^2$}

For $V=\lambda\phi^n$ on $\phi>0$, <slow-roll approximation> gives $\epsilon\simeq n^2M_{\rm Pl}^2/(2\phi^2)$ and $\eta\simeq2nM_{\rm Pl}^2/\phi^2$. The <slow-roll e-fold count> integrates to $N\simeq(\phi_N^2-\phi_e^2)/(2nM_{\rm Pl}^2)$. For $n=2,3,4$, the loose order-one conditions $\epsilon\leq1$ and $|\eta|\leq1$ imply $\phi^2\geq2nM_{\rm Pl}^2$ in this approximation. Genuine slow roll requires a much stronger hierarchy. A prescribed endpoint $\phi_e^2=2nM_{\rm Pl}^2$ gives $\phi_N^2=2n(N+1)M_{\rm Pl}^2$ and
$$
n_s\simeq1-\frac{n+2}{2(N+1)},\qquad r\simeq\frac{4n}{N+1}.
$$
The actual end criterion for accelerated expansion is $\epsilon=1$, whose leading potential estimate instead gives $\phi_e^2\simeq n^2M_{\rm Pl}^2/2$. The prescribed endpoint is a convention, not an exact slow-roll or inflation boundary.

= Quartic-inflation tilt-tensor relation
{title2=$n_s-1=-3r/16$}
{parent=Monomial inflation potential}

For canonical slow-roll inflation with $V(\phi)=\lambda\phi^4$, the leading <scalar spectral index> and <tensor-to-scalar ratio> obey
$$
n_s-1=-\frac3{16}r.
$$
It is a parameter-free consistency check of the quartic potential at leading slow-roll order.

= Slow-roll e-fold count
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}

The number of e-folds between field values $\phi_i$ and $\phi_f$ is
$$
N\simeq\frac1{\sqrt2M_{\rm Pl}}
\int_{\phi_f}^{\phi_i}\frac{d\phi}{\sqrt{\epsilon_V(\phi)}}
$$
when the field rolls monotonically down the potential.

= Monomial slow-roll e-fold count
{parent=Slow-roll e-fold count}

For $V(\phi)=\lambda\phi^n/n$ under the stated Cambridge Tripos normalization,
$$
N_e=\frac{4\pi G}{c^4n}
(\phi_i^2-\phi_f^2).
$$
If slow roll ends by kinetic-potential equality, then
$$
\phi_f^2\simeq\frac{n^2c^4}{48\pi G},
\qquad
N_e\simeq\frac n{12}
\left[\left(\frac{\phi_i}{\phi_f}\right)^2-1\right].
$$

= Natural inflation
{parent=Slow-roll approximation}
{wiki}

Natural inflation uses a periodic potential
$$
V(\phi)=V_0[1+\cos(\phi/f)].
$$
Its potential slow-roll parameter obeys
$$
\sqrt{\epsilon_V}=\frac{M_{\rm Pl}}{\sqrt2f}
\tan\frac{\phi}{2f}
$$
on the branch where the tangent is positive.

= E-fold count for the natural-inflation cosine potential
{parent=Natural inflation}

For the cosine potential,
$$
N=\frac{2f^2}{M_{\rm Pl}^2}
\left[
\log\sin\frac{\phi_i}{2f}
-\log\sin\frac{\phi_f}{2f}
\right].
$$

= Ultra-slow-roll inflation
{parent=Cosmic inflation}

Ultra-slow-roll inflation occurs on an exceptionally flat potential. The homogeneous field velocity decays as $\dot\phi\propto a^{-3}$, so $\epsilon\propto a^{-6}$ and the second Hubble slow-roll parameter approaches $-6$.

= Growing curvature perturbation in ultra-slow-roll inflation
{parent=Ultra-slow-roll inflation}

In ultra-slow-roll inflation, $z=a\sqrt{2\epsilon}\propto a^{-2}\propto\eta^2$. The comoving curvature mode equation is
$$
\mathcal R_k''+\frac4\eta\mathcal R_k'+k^2\mathcal R_k=0,
$$
and its Bunch-Davies mode is proportional to $(1+ik\eta)e^{-ik\eta}/\eta^3$. Unlike ordinary slow roll, the superhorizon curvature perturbation grows as $a^3$.

= Classicality parameter of a cosmological perturbation
{parent=Growing curvature perturbation in ultra-slow-roll inflation}

For a perturbation $X$, a normalized commutator
$$
\mathcal C=
\frac{|\langle[X,\dot X]\rangle|}
{\sqrt{\langle XX\rangle\langle\dot X\dot X\rangle}}
$$
compares its quantum commutator with its root-mean-square phase-space fluctuations. A limit $\mathcal C\to0$ signals effective classicality.

= Curvaton
{parent=Cosmic inflation}
{wiki}

A curvaton is a light spectator scalar field whose energy density is negligible during inflation but whose fluctuations can later generate the primordial curvature perturbation.

= Curvaton mechanism
{parent=Curvaton}
{wiki}

In the curvaton mechanism, the curvaton's relative energy density grows after inflation and its initially isocurvature fluctuation becomes an adiabatic curvature fluctuation when the curvaton decays.

= Linear cosmological density perturbation
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki=Structure_formation}

For pressureless subhorizon matter perturbations, a Fourier mode in conformal time obeys
$$\delta''+\mathcal H\delta'-\frac32\Omega_M\mathcal H^2\delta=0,$$
where $\mathcal H=a'/a$.

= Linear density modes with polytropic pressure
{parent=Linear cosmological density perturbation}

In a flat matter-dominated universe with $a\propto t^{2/3}$ and $P\propto\rho^{4/3}$, the squared <sound speed> scales as $t^{-2/3}$. Each comoving Fourier mode then satisfies an <Euler-Cauchy equation>, with gravitational growth at long wavelengths and logarithmic-time acoustic oscillations at short wavelengths.

= Repeated-root cosmological density mode
{parent=Linear density modes with polytropic pressure}
{title2=$t^{-1/6}(A+B\log t)$}

When the characteristic exponents of the density-mode <Euler-Cauchy equation> coincide, the second independent solution gains a logarithm. In the polytropic matter-era example the complete solution at this threshold is $t^{-1/6}(A+B\log t)$.

= Peculiar gravitational potential
{title2=$\Phi$}
{parent=Linear cosmological density perturbation}

The peculiar gravitational potential is the Newtonian potential after subtracting the potential of the homogeneous expanding background. In comoving coordinates it obeys a Poisson equation sourced by the <density contrast>, so its gradient accelerates matter relative to the <Hubble flow>.

= Density contrast
{title2=$\delta$}
{parent=Linear cosmological density perturbation}
{wiki=Density_contrast}

The density contrast is the fractional perturbation $\delta=(\rho-\bar\rho)/\bar\rho$.

= Newtonian-gauge matter density from a constant gravitational potential
{c}
{parent=Density contrast}
{title2=$\delta_c=-2\Phi-2k^2\Phi/(3\mathcal H^2)$}

In <matter domination> with the constant growing potential, the full Newtonian-gauge energy constraint gives the stated <density contrast>. The second term dominates inside the <Hubble radius> and grows as $a$, because $\mathcal H^2\propto a^{-1}$. Outside the <Hubble radius>, the constant $-2\Phi$ term dominates and is gauge dependent. The comoving density combination $\Delta_c=\delta_c+3\mathcal H\theta_c/k^2$ cancels it for $\theta_c=2k^2\Phi/(3\mathcal H)$, leaving the <cosmological Poisson equation> relation. Matter-spectrum power laws must specify whether they refer to this comoving density or to subhorizon Newtonian-gauge density.

= Smoothed matter density variance
{parent=Density contrast}
{title2=$\sigma^2(M,z)=\frac1{2\pi^2}\int_0^\infty k^2P(k,z)|W(kR)|^2\,dk$}

= Cosmological mass variance
{synonym}

The <smoothed matter density variance> measures the root-mean-square linear <density contrast> after applying a window corresponding to a Lagrangian mass scale. For a homogeneous isotropic field it is $\sigma^2=(1/(2\pi^2))\int k^2P(k)|W(kR)|^2dk$. The variance convention, growth epoch and smoothing mass-density relation must agree with the collapse barrier used in a halo abundance calculation.

= Spherical top-hat window function
{title2=$W_R$}
{parent=Density contrast}

A spherical top-hat window function averages a field uniformly inside a <sphere> of radius $R$ and gives zero weight outside it. Its normalized real-space kernel is $W_R(\mathbf x)=3/(4\pi R^3)$ for $|\mathbf x|\leq R$, and its <Fourier transform> is
$$
\widetilde W_R(k)=3\frac{\sin(kR)-kR\cos(kR)}{(kR)^3}.
$$

= Linear growth factor
{title2=$D(t)$}
{parent=Density contrast}
{wiki}

The linear growth factor multiplies the growing mode of a linear cosmological density perturbation, so $\delta(\mathbf x,t)=D(t)\delta(\mathbf x,t_0)/D(t_0)$ when scale-independent growth applies.

= Matter-spectrum response to extra dark energy
{parent=Linear growth factor}
{title2=$P_m^{\rm new}/P_m^{\rm old}\simeq(D_{\rm new}/D_{\rm old})^2$}

With physical matter and radiation densities, primordial amplitudes and spatial flatness fixed, extra smooth late <dark energy> increases $H_0$ but keeps $\Omega_mH_0^2$ fixed. Provided the <dark energy> is negligible at equality, the early <cosmological transfer function> and equality wavenumber in physical units are unchanged. Earlier accelerated expansion suppresses the <linear growth factor>, lowering present dimensional matter power by its squared growth ratio. Units involving $h$ can additionally relabel the horizontal and vertical axes.

= Linear matter perturbation growth equation
{parent=Linear growth factor}
{title2=$\ddot\delta+2H\dot\delta-4\pi G\bar\rho_m\delta=0$}

For pressureless sub-Hubble perturbations, combine the <linearized cosmological continuity equation>, <cosmological Euler equation> and gravitational <Poisson equation> with matter as its source. The expansion contributes a $2H$ friction term and matter self-gravity contributes the negative coefficient of $\delta$. The background expansion can include radiation or dark energy; treating their perturbations as additional sources requires a more complete system.

= Linear growth equation
{parent=Linear growth factor}

For pressureless matter on subhorizon scales, the <linear growth factor> obeys
$$
\ddot D+2H\dot D-4\pi G\bar\rho_mD=0.
$$
The second term is dilution by <cosmic expansion>, while the final term drives <Jeans instability>.

= Reduced gravitational clustering strength
{parent=Linear growth equation}

Replacing the self-gravity term in the matter growth equation by $(1-f)4\pi G\bar\rho_mD$, with $0<1-f<1$, weakens clustering while leaving the background expansion unchanged.

= Matter-era growth with reduced gravitational clustering strength
{title2=$s(f)=(-1+\sqrt{25-24f})/6$}
{parent=Reduced gravitational clustering strength}

During matter domination, the growing mode under a reduced clustering strength $1-f$ is
$$
D(t)\propto t^{s(f)},
\qquad
s(f)=\frac{-1+\sqrt{25-24f}}6.
$$
It grows more slowly than the standard $t^{2/3}$ mode whenever $0<f<1$.

= Matter-dominated density-perturbation modes
{parent=Linear growth factor}

In a matter-dominated universe, the long-wavelength pressureless growth equation has modes
$$
\delta_+\propto a,
\qquad
\delta_-\propto a^{-3/2}.
$$

= Logarithmic growth of matter perturbations during radiation domination
{parent=Linear growth factor}

When matter self-gravity is negligible during radiation domination, $a\propto t^{1/2}$ and $\ddot\delta+2H\dot\delta=0$ gives $\delta=C_1+C_2\log a$.

= Radiation-background Bessel basis for matter perturbations
{parent=Logarithmic growth of matter perturbations during radiation domination}
{title2=$\delta_+=I_0(\sqrt{6a/a_{\rm eq}}),\quad\delta_-=K_0(\sqrt{6a/a_{\rm eq}})$}

In a radiation-dominated background with matter self-gravity retained, $\delta_{aa}+\delta_a/a-3\delta/(2aa_{\rm eq})=0$. A change to $x=\sqrt{6a/a_{\rm eq}}$ gives the <modified Bessel differential equation> of order zero. The <modified Bessel function of the first kind> increases as $1+3a/(2a_{\rm eq})+\cdots$; the <modified Bessel function of the second kind> decreases with a leading negative logarithm. The early solution space has the constant/logarithmic behaviors, not the $a$ growth of <matter domination>. This background approximation must not be extrapolated through equality.

= Freezing of matter perturbations during curvature domination
{parent=Linear growth factor}

During curvature domination $a\propto t$, so a pressureless perturbation without a self-gravity term has modes $1$ and $a^{-1}$. The surviving mode is constant.

= Linearized cosmological continuity equation
{parent=Density contrast}

For a pressureless fluid with
$$
\rho=\bar\rho+\epsilon\,\delta\rho,
\qquad
\mathbf v=\epsilon\,\delta\mathbf v,
$$
the first-order <density contrast> $\delta=\delta\rho/\bar\rho$ obeys
$$
\dot\delta=-\frac1a\nabla\cdot\delta\mathbf v.
$$

= Jeans wavenumber
{title2=$k_J$}
{parent=Linear cosmological density perturbation}
{c}

The Jeans wavenumber separates pressure-supported modes from gravitationally unstable density modes.

= Jeans instability
{c}
{parent=Jeans wavenumber}
{wiki}

Jeans instability is the gravitational growth of density perturbations whose self-gravity overcomes pressure support. For physical sound speed $c_s$, the comoving threshold is
$$
k_J=\frac{a\sqrt{4\pi G\bar\rho}}{c_s}.
$$
Modes with $k\ll k_J$ grow gravitationally, whereas modes with $k\gg k_J$ undergo pressure-supported acoustic oscillations.

= Jeans instability with Yukawa gravity
{parent=Jeans instability}
{c}
{title2=$k^2<4\pi G\rho_0/v_s^2-\alpha^2$}

For the screened attraction $-Gm e^{-\alpha r}/r$ with $\alpha\geq0$, $(\nabla^2-\alpha^2)\phi=4\pi G\rho$. A static homogeneous barotropic medium with <sound speed> $v_s>0$ has $\omega^2=k^2[v_s^2-4\pi G\rho_0/(k^2+\alpha^2)]$. A growing mode exists iff $4\pi G\rho_0>v_s^2\alpha^2$, with $0<k<\sqrt{4\pi G\rho_0/v_s^2-\alpha^2}$. For nonzero screening the growth rate tends to zero as $k\to0$; the homogeneous mode is not an exponentially growing density fluctuation. Finite interaction range can therefore remove the instability entirely, while all sufficiently small nonzero wavenumbers remain unstable when the threshold is exceeded.

= Jeans swindle
{parent=Jeans instability}
{c}
{title2=$\nabla^2\delta\phi=4\pi G\delta\rho$}

The <Jeans swindle> is the removal of the unperturbed uniform density's gravitational field when treating an infinite homogeneous Newtonian medium as static. Without such subtraction or an external supporting prescription, a nonzero constant density and zero background gravitational <acceleration> cannot satisfy Poisson's equation. Linear perturbations then obey $\nabla^2\delta\phi=4\pi G\delta\rho$. This is a specified idealization, not an exact isolated infinite equilibrium.

= Static-universe Jeans modes
{c}
{parent=Jeans instability}

In a static homogeneous universe, a density mode obeys
$$
\ddot\delta_k+c_s^2(k^2-k_J^2)\delta_k=0.
$$
Modes with $k>k_J$ oscillate as sound waves, while modes with $k<k_J$ have one exponentially growing and one exponentially decaying solution.

= Comoving Jeans length
{title2=$\lambda_{J,\mathrm{com}}$}
{parent=Jeans instability}

Up to a convention-dependent numerical factor, a collisional fluid has comoving Jeans length $\lambda_{J,\mathrm{com}}=(c_s/a)\sqrt{\pi/(G\bar\rho)}$.

= Baryon Jeans length across recombination
{parent=Comoving Jeans length}

Before <cosmological recombination>, <photon-baryon sound speed> controls the baryonic <comoving Jeans length>. The tightly coupled <photon-baryon fluid> has $c_s^2=c^2/[3(1+R_b)]$, where $R_b=3\bar\rho_b/(4\bar\rho_\gamma)\propto a$. Using total background density in the instantaneous dynamical Jeans estimate, the scale grows as $a$ during <radiation domination>, approaches a plateau in strongly baryon-loaded <matter domination>, and drops when <photon decoupling> removes photon pressure support. Subsequently, adiabatic monatomic-gas cooling gives $T_b\propto a^{-2}$ and a <comoving Jeans length> proportional to $a^{-1/2}$. Residual <Compton scattering> can delay that cooling law.

= Collisionless Jeans length
{parent=Comoving Jeans length}

For collisionless matter, velocity dispersion replaces sound speed in the Jeans estimate. After free decoupling, a nonrelativistic thermal velocity dispersion redshifts as $a^{-1}$.

= Cold-dark-matter kinetic Jeans scale
{parent=Collisionless Jeans length}

An instantaneous kinetic suppression estimate replaces <sound speed> by the <velocity dispersion> of <cold dark matter>. Nonrelativistic particles in thermal contact with <photons> have $\sigma\propto a^{-1/2}$; after <kinetic decoupling>, their momenta redshift and $\sigma\propto a^{-1}$. A background dynamical estimate using total density is then constant during <radiation domination> and decreases as $a^{-1/2}$ during <matter domination>. Using only the species' own matter density instead makes the self-gravitating <collisionless Jeans length> decrease as $a^{-1/2}$ throughout the decoupled nonrelativistic era. Neither instantaneous estimate is the accumulated distance traveled by <collisionless free streaming>.

= Collisionless free streaming
{parent=Collisionless Jeans length}
{wiki=Free_streaming_(particle_physics)}

Collisionless free streaming is the ballistic motion of particles out of an overdensity. It erases perturbations below the comoving distance travelled by particles while their thermal velocities are appreciable.

= Cosmological sound speed
{title2=$c_s^2$}
{parent=Linear cosmological density perturbation}

For a barotropic fluid, the squared sound speed is $c_s^2=\partial P/\partial\rho$. A Fourier density mode obeys
$$
\ddot\delta+2H\dot\delta
+\left(\frac{c_s^2k^2}{a^2}-4\pi G\bar\rho\right)\delta=0
$$
in linear Newtonian perturbation theory.

= Cosmological adiabatic sound speed
{parent=Cosmological sound speed}
{title2=$c_a^2=\bar P'/\bar\rho'$}

The squared adiabatic sound speed is the ratio of background pressure and energy-density time derivatives. For a <barotropic equation of state> it is $dP/d\rho$. The <non-adiabatic pressure perturbation> is the difference $\delta P-c_a^2\delta\rho$; in a general field or multicomponent model this background quantity need not equal the rest-frame propagation speed.

= Suppression of matter growth by smooth accelerated expansion
{parent=Linear cosmological density perturbation}

If a smooth component dominates the expansion but does not cluster, pressureless matter still satisfies
$$
\ddot\delta+2H\dot\delta-4\pi G\bar\rho_m\delta=0.
$$
As $\bar\rho_m$ becomes negligible, Hubble friction leaves a constant mode and a decaying mode, so structure growth freezes.

= Matter density modes during cosmological-constant domination
{parent=Suppression of matter growth by smooth accelerated expansion}

When $H$ is constant and the matter source is negligible,
$$
\ddot\delta_m+2H\dot\delta_m=0,
$$
so $\delta_m=C_1+C_2a^{-2}$. The growing matter-era mode therefore approaches a constant after cosmological-constant domination begins.

= Matter growth equation as a function of scale factor
{parent=Suppression of matter growth by smooth accelerated expansion}

In a matter-plus-cosmological-constant universe, changing the independent variable from cosmic time to $a$ gives
$$
\frac{d^2\delta_m}{da^2}
+\left(\frac{d\log H}{da}+\frac3a\right)\frac{d\delta_m}{da}
-\frac{3\Omega_{m,0}H_0^2}{2a^5H^2}\delta_m=0.
$$

= Integral linear growth factor in a matter-Lambda universe
{title2=$D_+(a)$}
{parent=Matter growth equation as a function of scale factor}

The growing solution of the matter perturbation equation in a spatially flat matter-plus-cosmological-constant universe is
$$
D_+(a)=\frac52\Omega_{m,0}H_0^2H(a)
\int_0^a\frac{da'}{a'^3H(a')^3},
$$
normalized so that $D_+(a)\sim a$ during early matter domination.

= Freezing of linear growth under a positive cosmological constant
{parent=Integral linear growth factor in a matter-Lambda universe}
{title2=$D_+(a)\to D_\infty$}

When $H\to H_\Lambda>0$, the growth <integral> has an integrable $a^{-3}$ tail. Its product with $H$ tends to a constant, with leading correction proportional to $a^{-2}$ in a matter-plus-Lambda background. Both displayed basis functions can tend to constants; a linear combination cancels that constant and isolates the truly decaying late-time $a^{-2}$ behavior.

= Finite lower limit in the integral matter growth solution
{parent=Integral linear growth factor in a matter-Lambda universe}

Changing the lower limit in $H(a)\int_{a_i}^{a}d\widetilde a/(\widetilde aH)^3$ adds a constant multiple of $H(a)$, the independent matter-era decaying solution. Thus a finite lower limit defines a useful independent solution but can include a decaying admixture. A pure growing normalization is selected by an appropriate linear combination.

= Galaxy-formation bound on the cosmological constant
{parent=Integral linear growth factor in a matter-Lambda universe}

If a perturbation has amplitude $A\ll1$ at decoupling, it needs a growth factor of at least $A^{-1}$ before cosmological-constant domination freezes its growth. The order-of-magnitude requirement is
$$
\rho_\Lambda\lesssim A^3\rho_m(t_{\rm dec}),
$$
up to the collapse threshold and the gradual transition encoded by the exact growth factor.

= Radiation domination
{parent=Linear cosmological density perturbation}

During radiation domination in a flat expanding universe, $a(t)\propto t^{1/2}$ and $H=1/(2t)$.

= Mészáros effect
{c}
{parent=Radiation domination}
{wiki}

The Mészáros effect is the suppression of subhorizon cold-dark-matter growth during radiation domination. The growing solution is only logarithmic until matter-radiation equality.

= Mészáros equation
{c}
{parent=Mészáros effect}
{title2=$\delta_{yy}+\frac{2+3y}{2y(1+y)}\delta_y-\frac3{2y(1+y)}\delta=0$}

In a flat universe containing <pressureless matter> and smooth <radiation in cosmology>, put $y=a/a_{\rm eq}$ and $C=H_0^2\Omega_{m,0}^2/\Omega_{r,0}$. The <Friedmann equation> gives $\mathcal H^2=C(1+y)/y^2$, so $y'^2=C(1+y)$ and $y''=C/2$. The subhorizon <cosmological Poisson equation> gives $\delta''+\mathcal H\delta'=3C\delta/(2y)$. The <chain rule> produces the displayed equation. Smoothing radiation neglects its rapid acoustic forcing after horizon entry; it is not an exact treatment of all radiation perturbations.

= Mészáros equation solution basis
{c}
{parent=Mészáros equation}
{title2=$D_+(y),D_-(y)$}

A <basis> of the <solution space of a homogeneous linear differential equation> for the <Mészáros equation> on $y>0$ is
$$
D_+=1+\frac32y,\qquad
D_-=\left(1+\frac32y\right)\log\frac{\sqrt{1+y}+1}{\sqrt{1+y}-1}-3\sqrt{1+y}.
$$
Substitution verifies both solutions; their <Wronskian> is $-1/[y\sqrt{1+y}]$, so they are independent. At small $y$, $D_+=1+O(y)$ and $D_-=\log(4/y)-3+O(y\log y)$. At large $y$, $D_+\sim3y/2$ and $D_-\sim4/(15y^{3/2})$. Matching the actual early-time radiation forcing determines which constant and logarithmic combination is excited.

= Matter domination
{parent=Linear cosmological density perturbation}

During matter domination in a spatially flat universe, pressureless matter controls the expansion, $a(t)\propto t^{2/3}$, and the growing linear density mode is proportional to $a$.

= Matter-era growing and decaying density modes
{parent=Linear cosmological density perturbation}

In a flat matter-dominated era with $a\propto\tau^2$, one has $\mathcal H=2/\tau$ and
$$\delta=A\tau^2+B\tau^{-3}.$$
The growing mode is proportional to the scale factor.

= Cosmic-time matter density modes
{parent=Matter-era growing and decaying density modes}

In cosmic time during matter domination, $a(t)\propto t^{2/3}$ and the pressureless density contrast obeys
$$
\ddot\delta+\frac4{3t}\dot\delta-\frac2{3t^2}\delta=0.
$$
Its growing and decaying solutions are $t^{2/3}$ and $t^{-1}$.

= Matter-era linear growth factor
{parent=Matter-era growing and decaying density modes}

After neglecting the decaying mode, the linear growth between conformal times $\tau_1$ and $\tau_2$ is
$$D(\tau_2,\tau_1)=\frac{a(\tau_2)}{a(\tau_1)}
=\left(\frac{\tau_2}{\tau_1}\right)^2.$$

= Cosmological horizon crossing
{parent=Linear cosmological density perturbation}

A mode of comoving wavenumber $k$ and physical wavelength $2\pi a/k$ crosses a conformal horizon of physical size $ac\tau$ when
$$\tau_H=\frac{2\pi}{kc}.$$

= Horizon-crossing time across matter-radiation equality
{parent=Cosmological horizon crossing}

With $a(t_0)=1$, $k_0=2\pi/(ct_0)$, and $1+z_{\rm eq}=(t_0/t_{\rm eq})^{2/3}$,
$$
\frac{t_H}{t_0}\simeq
\begin{cases}
(k_0/k)^3,&t_H>t_{\rm eq},\\
(1+z_{\rm eq})^{-1/2}(k_0/k)^2,&t_H<t_{\rm eq}.
\end{cases}
$$

= Matter-era transfer of a horizon-crossing amplitude
{parent=Cosmological horizon crossing}

A mode crossing during matter domination acquires the growth factor $(\tau_0/\tau_H)^2$. An initial amplitude proportional to $\tau_H^2k^{1/2}$ therefore becomes proportional to $k^{1/2}$ today.

= Cosmological density power spectrum
{parent=Linear cosmological density perturbation}
{wiki=Matter_power_spectrum}

= Matter power spectrum
{synonym}

The density power spectrum records the squared Fourier-mode amplitude, up to the chosen statistical normalization. Scale dependence acquired from primordial amplitudes and subsequent growth determines its spectral shape.

= Broken matter power spectrum from horizon entry
{parent=Cosmological density power spectrum}

If primordial horizon-crossing perturbations satisfy $V\langle|\delta_k|^2\rangle=C/k^3$, remain frozen during radiation domination, and grow as $a$ during matter domination, then
$$
P(k)=
\begin{cases}
Ck/k_0^4,&k<k_{\rm eq},\\
Ck_{\rm eq}^4/(k^3k_0^4),&k>k_{\rm eq}.
\end{cases}
$$

= Neutrino free streaming
{parent=Linear cosmological density perturbation}
{wiki}

Neutrino free streaming transports neutrinos out of small-scale overdensities. Massive neutrinos therefore contribute to the homogeneous matter density while clustering inefficiently below their free-streaming scale, slowing the growth of cold-matter perturbations.

= Neutrino Boltzmann hierarchy
{parent=Neutrino free streaming}
{title2=$\Theta_\ell$}

For massless collisionless <neutrinos> in <Newtonian gauge in cosmology>, expand their temperature fluctuation as $\Theta(\mu)=\sum_{\ell\ge0}(-i)^\ell\Theta_\ell P_\ell(\mu)$, without a $(2\ell+1)$ factor. The <Legendre polynomial recurrence relation> gives
$$
\dot\Theta_\ell+k\left(\frac{\ell+1}{2\ell+3}\Theta_{\ell+1}-\frac{\ell}{2\ell-1}\Theta_{\ell-1}\right)=\delta_{\ell0}\dot\phi+\delta_{\ell1}k\psi.
$$
The lower-neighbour term is absent at $\ell=0$. Multipoles with the more usual $(2\ell+1)$ weighting equal $\Theta_\ell/(2\ell+1)$.

= Cosmological recombination
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki=Recombination_(cosmology)}

Cosmological recombination is the epoch in which cooling allowed free electrons and ions to form neutral atoms, sharply reducing the free-electron density.

= Residual electron freeze-out
{parent=Cosmological recombination}
{title2=$\alpha_{\rm eff}n_e\lesssim H$}

= Electron freeze-out
{synonym}

When the effective recombination rate per <electron> becomes smaller than the expansion rate, the small remaining ionized fraction can no longer track <chemical equilibrium>. A nonzero <electron> fraction remains, exceeding the exponentially small <Saha equation> prediction. The effective rate includes atomic radiative bottlenecks; chemical freeze-out is distinct from <photon decoupling> by scattering.

= Hydrogen ionization fraction
{parent=Cosmological recombination}
{title2=$X_e=n_e/n_b$}

In hydrogen-only cosmology, charge neutrality gives $n_e=n_p$ and baryon conservation gives $n_b=n_p+n_H$. The fraction $X_e$ lies between zero and one, with $n_H=(1-X_e)n_b$. With helium present, <electron> abundance per <hydrogen> nucleus and per total baryon are different conventions and must be specified. The <hydrogen-only Saha equation> gives the equilibrium fraction.

= Photon decoupling
{parent=Cosmological recombination}

Photon decoupling occurs when the Thomson-scattering rate drops below the Hubble expansion rate. Photons then free stream and form the cosmic microwave background.

= Thomson decoupling rate criterion
{c}
{parent=Photon decoupling}
{title2=$n_e\sigma_T\simeq H$}

The physical <Thomson scattering> rate equals the inverse <photon> mean scattering time. Comparing it with the <Hubble parameter> gives a local approximate decoupling criterion. A more precise last-scattering <temperature> is obtained from optical depth and the visibility maximum; these conventions need not give exactly the same <temperature>.

= Recombination temperature
{title2=$T_{\rm rec}$}
{parent=Cosmological recombination}

The recombination temperature is the photon temperature at which the free-electron fraction falls sharply as neutral atoms form. For hydrogen it is about $0.3\,\mathrm{eV}$ because the photon-to-baryon ratio is very large.

= Saha half-ionization temperature
{c}
{parent=Recombination temperature}
{title2=$y-\tfrac32\ln y=\ln[2/(3\times10^{-16})]$}

With the rounded <hydrogen> <Saha equation> coefficient, the condition $X_e=1/2$ gives the displayed equation for $y=\mathcal B/T$. Its low-temperature root is about $42$, hence $T\simeq0.32$ eV for <hydrogen> binding energy $13.6$ eV. Dropping the slowly varying logarithm gives the rough leading-log estimate $T\sim0.4$ eV. This equilibrium convention is distinct from <photon decoupling> and from a full kinetic recombination calculation.

= Saha ionization equation
{parent=Cosmological recombination}
{c}
{wiki=Saha_ionization_equation}

= Saha equation
{c}
{synonym}

Chemical equilibrium for $A^++e^-\leftrightarrow A^0+\gamma$ gives
$$
\frac{n_en_{A^+}}{n_{A^0}}
=\frac{g_eg_{A^+}}{g_{A^0}}
\left(\frac{2\pi m_ek_BT}{h^2}\right)^{3/2}
e^{-I_A/(k_BT)},
$$
after neglecting the ion--atom mass difference in the translational prefactor.

= Chemical-potential balance for ionization
{parent=Saha ionization equation}

Because equilibrium reactions balance chemical potentials and photons have zero chemical potential,
$$\mu_{A^+}+\mu_e=\mu_{A^0}.$$
The rest-mass difference in the Maxwell--Boltzmann densities then supplies the Boltzmann factor $e^{-I_A/(k_BT)}$.

= Hydrogen binding energy
{title2=$E_{\mathrm{bind}}$}
{parent=Saha ionization equation}
{c}

For $p+e^-\leftrightarrow H+\gamma$, the hydrogen binding energy is the rest-energy defect
$$
E_{\mathrm{bind}}=(m_p+m_e-m_H)c^2.
$$

= Free-electron fraction during hydrogen--helium recombination
{parent=Cosmological recombination}

With singly ionized helium and helium mass fraction $Y_p$, charge neutrality gives
$$
\frac{n_e}{n_B}
=(1-Y_p)X_{H^+}+\frac{Y_p}{4}X_{He^+}.
$$

= Coupled hydrogen--helium Saha equations
{parent=Free-electron fraction during hydrogen--helium recombination}

Writing $\mathcal F=n_e/n_B$ couples the two equilibrium equations:
$$
\mathcal F\frac{X_{H^+}}{1-X_{H^+}}=K(T)e^{-I_H/(k_BT)},\qquad
\mathcal F\frac{X_{He^+}}{1-X_{He^+}}=2K(T)e^{-I_{He}/(k_BT)}.
$$

= Hydrogen-only Saha equation
{parent=Coupled hydrogen--helium Saha equations}

When $Y_p=0$, $\mathcal F=X_{H^+}$, and
$$
\frac{X_{H^+}^2}{1-X_{H^+}}
=\frac1{n_B}\left(\frac{2\pi m_ek_BT}{h^2}\right)^{3/2}
e^{-I_H/(k_BT)}.
$$

= Friedmann equation
{c}
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki=Friedmann_equations}

$H=\dot a/a$, a fluid with $P=w\rho$ has $\rho\propto a^{-3(1+w)}$, and $\rho_{\rm crit}=3c^2H^2/(8\pi G)$.

= Closed radiation-dominated Friedmann solution
{parent=Friedmann equation}
{title2=$a=A\sin\tau,\quad t=A(1-\cos\tau)$}

With positive curvature, zero <cosmological constant> and radiation density $\rho_0a^{-4}$ in units $8\pi G=1$, $A=\sqrt{\rho_0/3}$. The <scale factor> is positive for $0<\tau<\pi$, reaches its maximum at $\pi/2$, and has a <Big Crunch> at <conformal time> $\pi$ or <proper time> $2A$. This complements the <closed matter-dominated Friedmann solution>.

= Friedmann effective potential for a constant-equation-of-state fluid
{c}
{parent=Friedmann equation}
{title2=$V(a)=-\rho_0a^{-(1+3w)}/6+k/2-\Lambda a^2/6$}

= Friedmann mechanical analogy
{c}
{synonym}

For a <perfect fluid in general relativity> of constant <equation of state> $P=w\rho$, conservation gives $\rho=\rho_0a^{-3(1+w)}$. In units $8\pi G=1$, the <Friedmann equation> becomes $\dot a^2/2+V(a)=0$. Allowed scales have $V\leq0$, and the <Friedmann acceleration equation> is $\ddot a=-V'(a)$. The curvature term shifts the potential vertically; positive <cosmological constant> bends it downward and negative <cosmological constant> bends it upward.

= Radiation-matter scale factor in conformal time
{parent=Friedmann equation}
{title2=$a(\tau)=H_0\sqrt{\Omega_{r,0}}\tau+\tfrac14H_0^2\Omega_{m,0}\tau^2$}

In a flat universe containing separately conserved <radiation in cosmology> and <pressureless matter>, the <Friedmann equation> becomes $(a')^2=H_0^2(\Omega_{r,0}+\Omega_{m,0}a)$ in <conformal time>. Setting $a=0$ at $\tau=0$ gives the stated quadratic-plus-linear <scale factor>. The linear term controls <radiation domination> and the quadratic term controls <matter domination>. The formula presumes $a_0=1$, $\Omega_{r,0}+\Omega_{m,0}=1$ and no <cosmological constant>.

= Flat matter-coasting-fluid Friedmann solution
{parent=Friedmann equation}
{title2=$a=\frac{\Omega_m}{2(1-\Omega_m)}[\cosh(\alpha\tau)-1]$}

A flat universe with separately conserved <pressureless matter> and a <coasting fluid> satisfies $a'^2=H_0^2[\Omega_m a+(1-\Omega_m)a^2]$. For both densities positive, choosing the <Big Bang> at zero <conformal time> gives $a=\Omega_m[\cosh(\alpha\tau)-1]/[2(1-\Omega_m)]$, with $\alpha=H_0\sqrt{1-\Omega_m}$. Physical time follows by integrating $dt=a\,d\tau$. The hyperbolic parametrization is formally like the open dust solution, but the spatial geometry here is flat.

= Conformal Riccati equation for matter and a coasting fluid
{parent=Flat matter-coasting-fluid Friedmann solution}
{title2=$2\mathcal H'+\mathcal H^2=H_0^2(1-\Omega_m)$}

The conformal <Friedmann equation> is $\mathcal H^2=H_0^2[\Omega_m/a+1-\Omega_m]$. Differentiating and using $a'=a\mathcal H$ eliminates the matter term and yields a first-order <Riccati equation>. The expanding Big-Bang branch for $0<\Omega_m<1$ has $\mathcal H=\alpha\coth(\alpha\tau/2)$.

= Open matter-dominated Friedmann solution
{parent=Friedmann equation}
{title2=$a\propto\cosh\eta-1,\quad t\propto\sinh\eta-\eta$}

A negatively curved universe with <pressureless matter> and no dark energy expands forever. With $a_0=1$ and $0<\Omega_{m,0}<1$, its <scale factor> is $a=\Omega_{m,0}(\cosh\eta-1)/[2(1-\Omega_{m,0})]$ and its <cosmic time> is $t=\Omega_{m,0}(\sinh\eta-\eta)/[2H_0(1-\Omega_{m,0})^{3/2}]$. The parameter is <conformal time> scaled by $\sqrt{-K}$ and measured from the <Big Bang>.

= Friedmann effective potential for pressureless matter
{title2=$V(a)=-\mu/a-\Lambda a^2/3$}
{c}
{parent=Friedmann equation}

For pressureless matter normalized by $\rho=3\mu/(8\pi a^3)$ in units $G=c=1$, the <Friedmann equation> is
$$
\dot a^2+V(a)=-k,
\qquad
V(a)=-\frac{\mu}{a}-\frac{\Lambda a^2}{3}.
$$
Allowed scale factors satisfy $V(a)\leq-k$, and equality marks a <turning point>.

= Critical closed matter-cosmological-constant universe
{parent=Friedmann effective potential for pressureless matter}

For positive spatial curvature and positive <cosmological constant>, the effective potential has its maximum at
$$
a_E=\left(\frac{3\mu}{2\Lambda}\right)^{1/3}.
$$
The critical case $V(a_E)=-1$ has
$$
\Lambda=\frac{4}{9\mu^2},
\qquad
a_E=\frac{3\mu}{2}=\Lambda^{-1/2},
$$
and contains the <Einstein static universe>.

= Friedmann bounce for pressureless matter and positive cosmological constant
{c}
{parent=Friedmann effective potential for pressureless matter}

When the energy line $-k$ lies below the maximum of the pressureless-matter effective potential, its large-$a$ allowed region is separated from the Big-Bang region. A contracting large universe reaches the outer turning point at positive scale factor and re-expands, producing a nonsingular bounce.

= Newtonian derivation of the flat Friedmann equation
{c}
{parent=Friedmann equation}

For a test mass on the surface of a uniform sphere of radius $R$ and mass $M=4\pi\rho R^3/3$, zero Newtonian specific energy gives
$$
\frac12\dot R^2-\frac{GM}{R}=0.
$$
With $R\propto a$, this becomes the spatially flat <Friedmann equation> $H^2=8\pi G\rho/3$.

= First integral of the Friedmann acceleration equation
{parent=Friedmann equation}
{c}

Combining the perfect-fluid continuity equation with
$$
\frac{\ddot a}{a}=-\frac{4\pi G}{3c^2}(\rho+3P)
$$
shows that
$$
\frac{8\pi G}{3c^2}\rho a^2-\dot a^2
$$
is constant in time. This constant is the spatial-curvature integration constant in the first Friedmann equation.

= Friedmann acceleration equation
{parent=Friedmann equation}
{c}

= Raychaudhuri equation
{c}
{synonym}

For energy density $\rho$, pressure $P$, and cosmological constant $\Lambda$,
$$
\frac{\ddot a}{a}
=-\frac{4\pi G}{3c^2}(\rho+3P)
+\frac{\Lambda c^2}{3}.
$$

= Newtonian fluid derivation of the Raychaudhuri equation
{c}
{parent=Friedmann acceleration equation}

For a homogeneous pressureless fluid in comoving coordinates, the <Euler equations for an inviscid fluid>[Euler equation] gives
$$
\nabla\bar\Phi=-a\ddot a\,\mathbf x,
$$
while the <Poisson equation> gives
$$
\nabla^2\bar\Phi=\frac{4\pi G}{c^2}\bar\rho a^2.
$$
Taking the <divergence> of the first relation and comparing them yields
$$
\frac{\ddot a}{a}=-\frac{4\pi G}{3c^2}\bar\rho.
$$
This local fluid derivation describes a homogeneous infinite universe without choosing a physical centre of expansion.

= Recollapse of a closed Friedmann universe with nonnegative pressure
{parent=Friedmann acceleration equation}

For positive spatial curvature, zero cosmological constant, and $\rho,P\geq0$, continuity makes $\rho a^3$ nonincreasing during expansion. The negative curvature term then prevents unbounded growth of $a$, while the acceleration equation forces $\dot a$ to reach zero in finite time.

= Strong energy condition in a Friedmann universe
{parent=Friedmann acceleration equation}

With zero cosmological constant, the condition $\rho+3P\geq0$ implies $\ddot a\leq0$ and
$$
\dot H\leq-H^2,
\qquad
\frac d{dt}(H^{-1})\geq1
$$
wherever $H\ne0$.

= Finite-time Friedmann singularity under the strong energy condition
{parent=Strong energy condition in a Friedmann universe}

If $H>0$ at one time, the inequality $d(H^{-1})/dt\geq1$ forces $H\to+\infty$ and $a\to0$ within finite time to the past. If $H<0$, it forces $H\to-\infty$ and $a\to0$ within finite time to the future.

= Radiation-to-cosmological-constant transition
{parent=Friedmann acceleration equation}

In a flat radiation-plus-$\Lambda$ universe,
$$
a(t)=\left(\frac{\Omega_{R0}}{1-\Omega_{R0}}\right)^{1/4}
\left[\sinh\!\left(2H_0\sqrt{1-\Omega_{R0}}\,t\right)\right]^{1/2}.
$$
The expansion changes from deceleration to acceleration when the radiation and cosmological-constant terms are equal, at
$$
t_\Lambda=\frac{\operatorname{arsinh}1}
{2H_0\sqrt{1-\Omega_{R0}}}.
$$

= Closed radiation--cosmological-constant turning polynomial
{parent=Friedmann equation}

If the present radiation and cosmological-constant densities are respectively $\beta$ and $1$ times the present critical density, while $a_0=1$, positive curvature gives
$$
H^2=\frac{H_0^2}{a^4}(a^4-\beta a^2+\beta).
$$
Turning points are therefore the positive roots of $x^2-\beta x+\beta$ with $x=a^2$.

= Supercritical closed radiation--cosmological-constant recollapse
{parent=Closed radiation--cosmological-constant turning polynomial}

For $\beta>4$, the turning polynomial has two positive roots and its smaller root lies below $2$. A universe expanding from $a=0$ reaches that root with $H=0$ and $\dot H<0$, then contracts to a finite-time Big Crunch.

= Critical closed radiation--cosmological-constant solution
{parent=Closed radiation--cosmological-constant turning polynomial}

For $\beta=4$ and $a(0)=0$, the expanding branch is
$$
a(t)=\sqrt{2(1-e^{-2H_0t})}.
$$
It grows as $2\sqrt{H_0t}$ near zero and approaches $\sqrt2$ exponentially at late times.

= Closed matter-dominated Friedmann solution
{parent=Friedmann equation}

A positively curved pressureless Friedmann universe has the cycloidal parametrization $a=A(1-\cos\theta)$ and $t=B(\theta-\sin\theta)$, with $B=A/\sqrt{k}$. It expands from a Big Bang, reaches maximum size at $\theta=\pi$, and recollapses at $\theta=2\pi$.

= Phantom energy
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki}

A fluid with $w<-1$ grows in density during expansion and can produce a finite-time <Big Rip> singularity.

= Big Rip
{c}
{parent=Phantom energy}
{wiki}

A Big Rip is a finite-future-time cosmological singularity at which the <scale factor>, <Hubble parameter>, and <phantom energy> density diverge.

= Big Bang
{c}
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki}

The Big Bang is the hot, dense early state from which the observable universe expanded. In classical Friedmann solutions its idealized initial boundary is often a singularity where the <scale factor> vanishes.

= Hot Big Bang model
{parent=Big Bang}

The hot Big Bang model describes an initially hot expanding universe whose standard radiation- and matter-dominated eras are governed by decelerating Friedmann expansion. Without an earlier inflationary era it exhibits the <horizon problem> and <flatness problem>.

= Scale factor
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki=Scale_factor_(cosmology)}

The cosmological scale factor $a(t)$ converts comoving separations into physical separations in an FLRW universe.

= Cosmic expansion
{parent=Scale factor}
{wiki=Expansion_of_the_universe}

Cosmic expansion is the increase of physical distances between comoving observers as the <scale factor> grows. Its instantaneous fractional rate is the <Hubble parameter>.

= Hubble parameter
{parent=Cosmic expansion}
{c}
{wiki}

The Hubble parameter is the fractional expansion rate
$$
H=\frac{\dot a}{a},
$$
and satisfies $\dot H+H^2=\ddot a/a$.

= Local volume Hubble parameter
{parent=Hubble parameter}
{title2=$H_{\mathrm{loc}}=-K/3$}

For the normal flow with zero <shift vector>, $a_{\mathrm{loc}}=(\det h)^{1/6}$ obeys $H_{\mathrm{loc}}=N^{-1}\partial_t\log a_{\mathrm{loc}}=-K/3$ in the negative <extrinsic curvature of a spatial hypersurface> convention. The <expansion scalar> is $\theta=-K=3H_{\mathrm{loc}}$.

= Conformal Hubble parameter
{parent=Hubble parameter}
{title2=$\mathcal H=a'/a=aH$}

For <conformal time> $d\tau=dt/a$, the conformal Hubble parameter is $\mathcal H=a'/a=aH=\dot a$, where primes and dots denote conformal and <cosmic time> derivatives respectively. Its inverse, in units $c=1$, is the <comoving Hubble radius>. It differs from the ordinary <Hubble parameter> by one factor of the <scale factor>.

= Hubble radius
{c}
{parent=Hubble parameter}
{title2=$c/H$}

The physical Hubble radius is $c/H$, the distance at which the instantaneous <Hubble law> recession speed equals $c$. Its comoving counterpart is $c/(aH)$. Neither radius is generally the same as a <particle horizon> or a causal boundary: those depend on integrals of the past or future expansion history.

= Hubble constant
{title2=$H_0$}
{c}
{parent=Hubble parameter}

The Hubble constant is the present value $H_0$ of the <Hubble parameter>. It sets the local proportionality between recession velocity and distance in the <Hubble law>.

= Hubble parameter identity
{c}
{parent=Hubble parameter}

Differentiating $H=\dot a/a$ gives
$$
\dot H=\frac{\ddot a}{a}-H^2.
$$

= Comoving Hubble radius
{title2=$(aH)^{-1}$}
{parent=Hubble parameter}
{wiki}

The comoving Hubble radius is $(aH)^{-1}$. It grows during ordinary decelerating expansion and shrinks during sufficiently accelerated inflation, allowing modes to cross from sub-Hubble to super-Hubble scales.

= Cosmic-time derivative of the comoving Hubble radius
{parent=Comoving Hubble radius}
{title2=$d[(aH)^{-1}]/dt$}

With $\mathcal H=aH=\dot a$, the <Friedmann acceleration equation> gives
$$
\frac d{dt}\frac1{\mathcal H}=-\frac{\ddot a}{\dot a^2}=\frac\Omega{2a}(1+3w).
$$
The coefficient is positive when $a>0$, $\rho>0$ and $H\ne0$. Thus growth of the <comoving Hubble radius> and linear growth of departures from flatness have the same sign criterion in an expanding universe. This local criterion is distinct from a global <particle horizon>, which depends on the whole past history.

= Hubble parameter for constant-equation-of-state components
{parent=Hubble parameter}

In a spatially flat universe containing components with constant $w_i$,
$$
H(z)=H_0\left[\sum_i\Omega_{i,0}(1+z)^{3(1+w_i)}\right]^{1/2}.
$$

= Hubble parameter for matter and dynamical dark energy
{parent=Hubble parameter}

For pressureless matter and dark energy with equation of state $w(z)$,
$$
H(z)=H_0\left[\Omega_{m,0}(1+z)^3
+\Omega_{{\rm DE},0}(1+z)^3
\exp\left(3\int_0^z\frac{w(z')}{1+z'}\,dz'\right)
\right]^{1/2}.
$$

= Hubble friction
{c}
{parent=Hubble parameter}

Hubble friction is the damping term proportional to the <Hubble parameter> in a field or fluid equation in an expanding universe. A homogeneous scalar field, for example, has the term $3H\dot\phi$ in its equation of motion.

= Hubble flow
{c}
{parent=Hubble parameter}
{wiki=Hubble's_law}

= Hubble law
{c}
{synonym}

The homogeneous expansion velocity is proportional to physical position:
$$
\mathbf u=H\mathbf r=aH\mathbf x=\dot a\,\mathbf x.
$$

= Comoving coordinate
{parent=Scale factor}
{wiki=Comoving_and_proper_distances}

A comoving coordinate remains fixed for an observer following the homogeneous cosmological expansion.

= Comoving observer
{parent=Comoving coordinate}

A comoving observer has constant <comoving coordinates> in a homogeneous expanding universe. Its physical separation from another comoving observer grows in proportion to the <scale factor>, giving relative recession governed by the <Hubble parameter>. Light exchanged between these observers undergoes <cosmological redshift>.

= Comoving volume
{parent=Comoving coordinate}
{title2=$V=a^3V_c$}

A comoving volume has fixed boundaries in <comoving coordinates>. Its physical volume grows as the cube of the <scale factor>. In a homogeneous adiabatic gas, <cosmological entropy conservation> makes $sV$ constant.

= Comoving number density
{parent=Comoving volume}
{title2=$n_p=(1+z)^3n_c$}

<Comoving number density> is object count per <comoving volume>. With scale factor normalized to unity today, proper density is $n_p=a^{-3}n_c$. A conserved population moving with homogeneous expansion has constant $n_c$ despite declining proper density. Formation, mergers or changing sample selection can make $n_c$ evolve.

= Peculiar velocity
{parent=Comoving coordinate}
{wiki}

Peculiar velocity is motion relative to the homogeneous Hubble flow. In comoving coordinates,
$$
\mathbf u=aH\mathbf x+\mathbf v,
$$
where $\mathbf u$ is physical velocity and $\mathbf v$ is peculiar velocity.

= Peculiar-velocity equation
{parent=Peculiar velocity}

For a fluid in an expanding universe, the peculiar-velocity equation is the <Euler equations for an inviscid fluid> after decomposing physical velocity into <Hubble flow> and <peculiar velocity>. It contains the Hubble-drag term $H\mathbf v$ and force $-\nabla\Phi/a$ from the <peculiar gravitational potential>.

= Peculiar-velocity divergence
{title2=$\theta$}
{parent=Peculiar velocity}

The peculiar-velocity divergence is $\theta=\nabla\mathbin\cdot\mathbf v$. For irrotational linear perturbations it captures the scalar part of the peculiar velocity field.

= Cosmological redshift
{parent=Scale factor}
{wiki}

Light emitted at $t_e$ and observed at $t_0$ has $1+z=a(t_0)/a(t_e)$.

= Cosmological wavelength-redshift relation
{parent=Cosmological redshift}
{title2=$1+z=\lambda_{\rm obs}/\lambda_{\rm rest}$}

Cosmic expansion stretches an emitted or absorbed spectral feature's wavelength by $1+z$. A small observed wavelength interval at fixed transition wavelength gives a redshift interval by division by the rest wavelength. Cosmological radial distances then require the <Hubble parameter> and, for proper distance at that epoch, an extra factor $1/(1+z)$.

= Proper absorber separation from a wavelength interval
{parent=Cosmological wavelength-redshift relation}
{title2=$\ell\simeq c\Delta\lambda/[H(z)(1+z)\lambda_{\rm rest}]$}

A local redshift spacing between absorbers corresponds to proper path length $c\Delta z/[H(z)(1+z)]$. Using the same rest-frame transition for all lines gives $\Delta z=\Delta\lambda/\lambda_{\rm rest}$. This conversion assumes <cosmological redshift> dominates the line spacing and uses a local Hubble-rate approximation.

= Redshift drift
{title2=$\dot z$}
{parent=Cosmological redshift}
{wiki}

Redshift drift is the change in a comoving source's cosmological redshift with observer time. It directly probes $\dot z=(1+z)H_0-H(z)$.

= Redshift-time relation
{parent=Cosmological redshift}

Differentiating $1+z=a_0/a(t)$ gives
$$
\frac{dz}{dt}=-(1+z)H(z).
$$

= Cosmic chronometer
{parent=Redshift-time relation}

Cosmic chronometers infer the Hubble parameter from differential ages of passively evolving galaxies:
$$
H(z)=-\frac1{1+z}\frac{dz}{dt}.
$$
They probe $H(z)$ locally in redshift rather than through a distance integral.

= Cosmological time dilation
{parent=Cosmological redshift}

Observed time intervals from a comoving source are stretched by the same factor $1+z$ as photon wavelengths.

= Redshift preservation of a thermal photon spectrum
{parent=Cosmological redshift}

After photon decoupling in an expanding universe, every frequency scales as $\nu\propto a^{-1}$. Therefore a Planck occupation factor retains its thermal form when its temperature is assigned the same scaling,
$$
T(t)=\frac{a(t_{\rm dec})}{a(t)}T_{\rm dec}.
$$

= Luminosity distance
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki}

Luminosity distance is defined by $F=L/(4\pi d_L^2)$ and includes geometric dilution, photon redshift, and arrival-rate dilation.

= Type Ia supernova cosmology
{c}
{parent=Luminosity distance}

Type Ia supernova cosmology uses standardized supernova luminosities to measure the shape of the luminosity-distance--redshift relation. In a spatially flat universe,
$$
d_L(z)=(1+z)c\int_0^z\frac{dz'}{H(z')}.
$$
An unknown common absolute luminosity is degenerate with the overall distance scale but does not erase the redshift dependence.

= Absolute magnitude–Hubble constant degeneracy
{parent=Type Ia supernova cosmology}

In <Type Ia supernova cosmology>, the predicted <apparent magnitude> depends on the common <absolute magnitude> $M_0$ and the <Hubble constant> only through $\beta=M_0-5\log_{10}(H_0/H_{\rm ref})$, after choosing a fixed reference $H_{\rm ref}>0$. A flat <improper prior> on $M_0$ makes the likelihood integrated over $M_0$ independent of $H_0$, by translation of the integration variable. If the other prior factors are independent and the posterior is proper, the marginal posterior of $H_0$ equals its prior. An external calibration of $M_0$ can break this lack of <identifiability>.

= Cosmological flux dimming
{parent=Luminosity distance}

Expansion reduces bolometric flux by one factor of $1+z$ from photon energy and one from arrival rate, in addition to inverse-area dilution.

= Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric
{parent=Cosmology}
{c}
{wiki=Friedmann–Lemaître–Robertson–Walker_metric}

= FRW metric
{c}
{synonym}

= FLRW metric
{c}
{synonym}

The FLRW metric is the homogeneous and isotropic spacetime metric with scale factor $a(t)$ and constant spatial curvature $k$.

= Spherical spatial slices of a closed FLRW universe
{parent=Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric}

A round <three-sphere> of radius $R$ embedded by $|\mathbf x|^2+u^2=R^2$ has metric $R^2\{dr^2/(1-r^2)+r^2d\Omega^2\}$ for $r=|\mathbf x|/R$. Multiplying by $b(t)^2$ gives a <scale factor> $a=Rb$ and normalized positive <spatial curvature of an FLRW universe> $K=1$. This radial chart covers a hemisphere; $r=\sin\chi$ gives the global polar description.

= Cosmic time
{parent=Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric}

Cosmic time is the <proper time> measured by observers comoving with the homogeneous cosmological fluid. Constant-cosmic-time hypersurfaces are the homogeneous spatial slices of an FLRW spacetime.

= Milne universe
{c}
{parent=Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric}
{wiki=Milne_model}

The Milne universe is the empty $k=-1$, $\Lambda=0$ FLRW model with scale factor proportional to proper time. It is a hyperbolic-coordinate description of the interior of a future light cone in <Minkowski spacetime>.

= Spatially flat FLRW metric
{parent=Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric}
{c}

In Cartesian comoving coordinates and signature $(-,+,+,+)$, a spatially flat FLRW universe has
$$
ds^2=-c^2dt^2+a^2(t)(dx^2+dy^2+dz^2).
$$

= Spatial curvature of an FLRW universe
{parent=Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric}

The cases $k>0$, $k=0$, and $k<0$ give spherical, Euclidean, and hyperbolic constant-curvature spatial slices.

= Proper distance in cosmology
{parent=Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric}

Proper distance is the spatial metric length measured along a fixed-cosmic-time slice.

= Conformal time
{parent=Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric}
{wiki}

Conformal time satisfies $d\tau=dt/a(t)$ and factors the FLRW metric into $a^2(\tau)$ times a static conformal metric.

= Closed constant-equation-of-state conformal lifetime
{parent=Conformal time}
{title2=$\Delta\tau=2\pi/(1+3w)$}

For positive curvature and zero <cosmological constant>, the single-fluid Friedmann solution has a bang-to-crunch <conformal time> interval $2\pi/(1+3w)$ for $w\geq0$. A <radial null geodesic in FLRW spacetime> travels that same great-circle comoving distance. Relative to a three-sphere circumference $2\pi$, it is $1/(1+3w)$ circuits: one for dust and one half for radiation, with the final point reached only in the singular-endpoint limit.

= Conformal time during de Sitter expansion
{c}
{parent=Conformal time}

For the <de Sitter scale factor in flat slicing>
$$
a(t)=a_0e^{H_{\rm inf}t},
$$
choose the additive constant so that
$$
\tau=-\frac1{a_0H_{\rm inf}}e^{-H_{\rm inf}t}
=-\frac1{aH_{\rm inf}}.
$$
An eternal expanding patch has $-\infty<\tau<0$.

= Comoving radial distance
{parent=Conformal time}
{wiki=Comoving_and_proper_distances}

Comoving radial distance uses the coordinate $\chi=\int dr/\sqrt{1-kr^2}$ and removes the radial curvature factor.

= Radial null geodesic in FLRW spacetime
{parent=Conformal time}

In conformal coordinates a radial light ray obeys $d\chi/d\tau=\pm c$, so the cosmological light cone is at forty-five degrees after setting $c=1$.

= Cosmological continuity equation
{parent=Cosmology}

Energy conservation in FLRW gives $\dot\rho=-3H(\rho+P)$ when $\rho$ and $P$ are energy density and pressure.

= Equation-of-state parameter
{parent=Cosmological continuity equation}

A cosmological fluid with constant equation-of-state parameter $w$ has $P=w\rho$ and $\rho\propto a^{-3(1+w)}$.

= Quintessence
{parent=Equation-of-state parameter}
{wiki=Quintessence_(physics)}

Quintessence is dynamical dark energy with negative pressure, conventionally $-1<w<-1/3$.

= Quintessence scale-factor solution
{parent=Quintessence}

For $w=-2/3$, zero cosmological constant, and negative curvature, $a(t)=\sqrt\beta\,t+\gamma t^2/4$ with density and curvature parameters $\gamma,\beta>0$.

= Quintessence-dominated universe
{parent=Quintessence}

For $w=-2/3$, a quintessence-dominated scale factor grows quadratically and $tH$ approaches two.

= Age of an FLRW universe
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki=Age_of_the_universe}

The age is the proper cosmic time elapsed from the scale-factor singularity $a=0$ to the present epoch.

= Age of a flat matter-coasting-fluid universe
{parent=Age of an FLRW universe}
{title2=$H_0t_0=\frac1b-\frac{\Omega_m}{b^{3/2}}\operatorname{arsinh}\sqrt{\frac b{\Omega_m}}$}

Integrating $dt=da/(aH)$ gives $H_0t_0=\int_0^1\sqrt a\,da/\sqrt{\Omega_m+(1-\Omega_m)a}$. For $b=1-\Omega_m$ in the interior of the physical density range, this is $b^{-1}-\Omega_m b^{-3/2}\operatorname{arsinh}\sqrt{b/\Omega_m}$. The pure matter and pure coasting endpoints have ages $2/(3H_0)$ and $1/H_0$.

= Coasting-fluid age bound
{parent=Age of a flat matter-coasting-fluid universe}
{title2=$\frac23\leq H_0t_0\leq1$}

For nonnegative matter and coasting-fluid densities, $0\leq\Omega_m\leq1$. Since $a\leq\Omega_m+(1-\Omega_m)a\leq1$ on the normalized integration interval, the age integrand lies between $\sqrt a$ and one. The <age of a flat matter-coasting-fluid universe> therefore decreases monotonically with the matter fraction and stays between the pure-fluid endpoint ages.

= Hubble time
{parent=Age of an FLRW universe}
{c}
{wiki}

The Hubble time $H_0^{-1}$ is the expansion timescale inferred from the present Hubble parameter.

= Curvature-dominated universe
{parent=Cosmology}

In a curvature-dominated open FLRW regime, the scale factor is approximately linear in cosmic time and $tH$ approaches one.

= Deceleration parameter
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki}

The deceleration parameter is $q=-a\ddot a/\dot a^2$; negative values describe accelerated expansion.

= Radiation relation between deceleration and density
{parent=Deceleration parameter}
{title2=$q=\Omega$}

For <radiation in cosmology> with zero <cosmological constant>, $p=\rho/3$. The <Friedmann acceleration equation> gives $\ddot a/a=-8\pi G\rho/3$, so the <deceleration parameter> equals the <cosmological density parameter>, regardless of spatial curvature. This is a relation for a pure radiation fluid, not a general identity for a mixture.

= Accelerating universe
{parent=Deceleration parameter}
{wiki=Accelerating_expansion_of_the_universe}

= Accelerating expansion
{synonym}

An accelerating universe has $\ddot a>0$, equivalently $q<0$ while expansion continues.

= Accelerated contraction
{parent=Deceleration parameter}

Accelerated contraction has $H<0$ and $\ddot a<0$, so the magnitude $|\dot a|$ increases. Since $\ddot a/a=H^2(1-\epsilon)$, it corresponds to a first Hubble slow-roll parameter $\epsilon>1$.

= Big Bang nucleosynthesis
{parent=Cosmology}
{c}
{wiki}

Big Bang nucleosynthesis formed the light nuclei during the first minutes of cosmic expansion after weak freeze-out and the deuterium bottleneck.

= Neutron-proton chemical equilibrium
{parent=Big Bang nucleosynthesis}

With negligible lepton chemical potentials, weak equilibrium gives $n_n/n_p\simeq e^{-Q/(k_BT)}$, where $Q=(m_n-m_p)c^2$.

= Neutron-to-proton ratio
{parent=Neutron-proton chemical equilibrium}
{title2=$n_n/n_p$}

Neglecting chemical potentials, the equilibrium abundance ratio is $n_n/n_p\simeq\exp[-(m_n-m_p)c^2/(k_BT)]$. Weak freeze-out fixes an approximate starting ratio, which is then reduced by <free-neutron decay after freeze-out> before most remaining neutrons bind into helium.

= Cosmological weak freeze-out
{parent=Big Bang nucleosynthesis}

Weak neutron--proton conversion freezes out when its interaction rate falls below the Hubble expansion rate.

= Freeze-out temperature and interaction strength
{parent=Cosmological weak freeze-out}

If an interaction-to-expansion ratio scales as $(T/\kappa)^3$, increasing $\kappa$ raises the temperature at which the ratio drops through one.

= Deuterium bottleneck
{parent=Big Bang nucleosynthesis}
{wiki}

Light nuclei cannot accumulate until cooling makes deuterium sufficiently resistant to photodissociation, delaying nucleosynthesis after weak freeze-out.

= Deuterium equilibrium abundance
{parent=Deuterium bottleneck}

For chemical equilibrium $D\leftrightarrow n+p$, Maxwell--Boltzmann densities and $\mu_D=\mu_n+\mu_p$ give, after taking $m_n\simeq m_p$ and $m_D\simeq2m_p$,
$$
\frac{n_D}{n_pn_n}
\simeq
\left(\frac{\pi m_pk_BT}{h^2}\right)^{-3/2}
 e^{B_D/(k_BT)}.
$$
Writing $X_i=n_i/n_B$, $n_B=\eta n_\gamma$, and $n_\gamma=16\pi\zeta(3)(k_BT)^3/(hc)^3$ gives
$$
\frac{X_D}{X_pX_n}
\simeq\frac{16\zeta(3)}{\sqrt\pi}\eta
\left(\frac{k_BT}{m_pc^2}\right)^{3/2}
 e^{B_D/(k_BT)}.
$$
The very small baryon-to-photon ratio suppresses deuterium until $T$ is far below the binding-energy scale.

= Baryon-density effect on primordial helium
{parent=Deuterium equilibrium abundance}

Increasing the baryon-to-photon ratio raises the equilibrium deuterium abundance at fixed temperature, so the deuterium bottleneck ends earlier. Fewer neutrons decay before nuclear burning, and neutron-limited helium synthesis produces a larger primordial helium mass fraction if weak freeze-out is unchanged.

= Free-neutron decay after freeze-out
{parent=Deuterium bottleneck}

Between weak freeze-out and deuterium formation, beta decay lowers the surviving free-neutron fraction.

= Primordial helium mass fraction
{parent=Big Bang nucleosynthesis}

If almost all neutrons enter helium-4 and $r=n_n/n_p$, its primordial mass fraction is $Y_p\simeq2r/(1+r)$.

= Primordial helium response to extra relativistic species
{parent=Primordial helium mass fraction}
{title2=$T_f\propto g_*^{1/6}$}

With a weak conversion time proportional to $T^{-5}$ and a radiation-era age proportional to $g_*^{-1/2}T^{-2}$, <cosmological weak freeze-out> occurs at $T_f\propto g_*^{1/6}$. Extra radiation therefore raises the freeze-out temperature and the <neutron-to-proton ratio> $r_f=e^{-Q/T_f}$. Before decay, the <primordial helium mass fraction> is $Y_f=2r_f/(1+r_f)$, giving $\delta Y_f/Y_f=Q\delta\log T_f/[T_f(1+r_f)]$. A shorter delay through the <deuterium bottleneck> also leaves more neutrons. After freeze-out, conserve baryon number and evolve the neutron fraction to obtain $Y_p\simeq Y_f e^{-(t_N-t_f)/\tau_n}$. A precise abundance requires the weak rates and nuclear-burning history, not merely the number of relativistic species.

= Primordial helium response to electron-neutrino degeneracy
{parent=Primordial helium mass fraction}
{title2=$n_n/n_p\simeq e^{-(m_n-m_p)/T-\xi_{\nu_e}}$}

Chemical equilibrium of $n+\nu_e\leftrightarrow p+e^-$, with negligible electron <chemical potential>, gives the stated <neutron-to-proton ratio>. Positive electron-neutrino degeneracy reduces the neutron fraction, whereas negative degeneracy increases it. The <primordial helium mass fraction> then responds through <neutron-limited helium synthesis> while protons outnumber neutrons; a sufficiently neutron-rich mixture instead becomes proton limited, so that trend must not be extrapolated indefinitely. Extra <neutrino> <energy density> also accelerates <cosmic expansion>, changing weak freeze-out and the time available for neutron decay. These effects can compete; a quantitative <Big Bang nucleosynthesis> calculation must account for the altered reaction rates as well as the altered expansion rate.

= Neutron-limited helium synthesis
{parent=Primordial helium mass fraction}

When protons outnumber neutrons, helium-4 production is neutron-limited and produces about one helium nucleus per two neutrons.

= Primordial composition and stellar lifetime
{parent=Primordial helium mass fraction}

Increasing primordial helium at the expense of hydrogen reduces fuel for long-lived main-sequence hydrogen burning and changes subsequent stellar evolution.

= Baryon-to-photon ratio
{title2=$\eta$}
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki}

The baryon-to-photon ratio is the number density of baryons divided by the number density of photons. In the present universe it is of order $10^{-9}$.

= Baryon-to-photon dilution by entropy injection
{title2=$\eta^+/\eta^-=(1+\rho_\chi/\rho_\gamma)^{-3/4}$}
{parent=Baryon-to-photon ratio}

Thermal <photon number density> is proportional to $T^3$. A sudden photon heating factor $(1+r)^{1/4}$ therefore raises photon number by $(1+r)^{3/4}$ and dilutes the <baryon-to-photon ratio> by its inverse when baryon number is conserved. Comparing <Big Bang nucleosynthesis> and <cosmic microwave background> baryon-density determinations can test entropy injection between the two epochs, subject to changes in the expansion rate and nuclear abundances.

= Cosmological entropy conservation
{parent=Cosmology}

For an adiabatically expanding homogeneous universe, the <entropy density> satisfies
$$
\nabla_\mu(su^\mu)=0,
\qquad
\dot s+3Hs=0,
$$
so the entropy $sa^3$ in a comoving volume is constant.

= Separately conserved visible and dark entropy
{parent=Cosmological entropy conservation}
{title2=$\xi=s_{\rm SM}/s_\phi$}

After <thermal decoupling in cosmology>, two homogeneous sectors that evolve adiabatically without entropy injection satisfy $s_{\rm SM}a^3=\mathrm{constant}$ and $s_\phi a^3=\mathrm{constant}$, hence their <entropy density> ratio $\xi$ is fixed. At a common relativistic <temperature> before decoupling, $\xi=g_{*s}^{\rm SM}/g_{*s}^\phi$. Internal annihilations can change each sector's <temperature> and effective entropy degrees of freedom without changing its total comoving <entropy>.

= Instantaneous photon heating by a decaying relic
{title2=$T_\gamma^+/T_\gamma^-=(1+\rho_\chi/\rho_\gamma)^{1/4}$}
{parent=Cosmological entropy conservation}

If a nonrelativistic relic decays entirely into promptly thermalized photons, energy conservation at fixed <scale factor> gives $\rho_\gamma^+=\rho_\gamma^-+\rho_\chi^-$. Since thermal photons have $\rho_\gamma\propto T_\gamma^4$, their temperature rises by the displayed factor while an already decoupled <cosmic neutrino background> is not heated. Before decay the relic-to-photon density ratio grows as $a$.

= Effective number of relativistic degrees of freedom
{title2=$g_{*s}$}
{parent=Cosmological entropy conservation}

= Effective number of relativistic entropy degrees of freedom
{synonym}

The effective number of relativistic entropy degrees of freedom is defined by
$$
s=\frac{2\pi^2}{45}g_{*s}T^3.
$$
A relativistic bosonic degree of freedom contributes one, while a relativistic fermionic degree of freedom contributes $7/8$ when all species share a temperature.

= Thermal decoupling in cosmology
{parent=Cosmological entropy conservation}

A particle species thermally decouples when its interaction rate falls below the <Hubble parameter>. Its collisionless relativistic distribution subsequently redshifts with temperature proportional to $a^{-1}$, while annihilations among still-coupled species can heat the remaining plasma.

= Conserved cosmological relic abundance
{parent=Thermal decoupling in cosmology}
{title2=$Y=n/s$}

If relic particle number and reference-gas <entropy> are separately conserved in a <comoving volume>, their ratio $Y=n/s$ is constant. A decay or <entropy> injection can change it. The reference entropy is that of the specified thermal sector, not necessarily the entropy of the decoupled relic itself.

= Density bound on a relativistically decoupled massive relic
{parent=Conserved cosmological relic abundance}
{title2=$m_x\lesssim\frac{\Omega_{x,\max}}{\Omega_\gamma}\frac{\langle E_\gamma\rangle}{n_x/n_\gamma}$}

A species freezing out while relativistic retains its thermal comoving number. If it is nonrelativistic today, $\rho_x\simeq m_xn_x$. Comparing with <photons> gives $\Omega_x/\Omega_\gamma=m_x(n_x/n_\gamma)/\langle E_\gamma\rangle$. An upper density fraction therefore bounds its mass inversely with its relic number density. The relation does not apply unchanged to a species whose abundance is depleted by nonrelativistic annihilation.

= Cannibal phase of a decoupled sector
{parent=Thermal decoupling in cosmology}

A massive decoupled sector enters a cannibal phase when its particles are nonrelativistic but rapid number-changing reactions maintain <chemical equilibrium> and <zero chemical potential from number-changing reactions>. Rest-mass energy released by $3\to2$ reactions heats the remaining particles, so the sector cools much more slowly than an isolated number-conserving nonrelativistic gas. The temperature laws follow directly from <cosmological entropy conservation>. An example is discussed in https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.04219[Cannibal Dark Matter].

= Logarithmic cooling during cannibalism
{parent=Cannibal phase of a decoupled sector}
{title2=$T_\phi\sim m/[3\log a]$}

The <nonrelativistic entropy at zero chemical potential> and $s_\phi a^3=\mathrm{constant}$ give $x+\frac12\log x=3\log a+\mathrm{constant}$. Using this leading nonrelativistic approximation, differentiation yields
$$
\frac{d\log T_\phi}{d\log a}\simeq-\frac3{x+1/2}.
$$
This is much smaller in magnitude than the relativistic-bath cooling rate $-1$ when $x\gg1$ and its entropy degrees of freedom are fixed. Thus <cannibal phase of a decoupled sector> cooling is logarithmic. Once number-changing reactions freeze out, a number-conserving nonrelativistic gas instead cools as $a^{-2}$ during adiabatic expansion.

= Cannibal-sector temperature ratio
{parent=Cannibal phase of a decoupled sector}
{title2=$T_\gamma/T_\phi$}

For a massive real scalar with one internal degree of freedom with $x=m/T_\phi\gg1$, fixed <separately conserved visible and dark entropy> ratio $\xi$, and relativistic visible bath with entropy degrees of freedom $g_{*s}^{\rm SM}$,
$$
\frac{T_\gamma}{T_\phi}\sim\left[\frac{45}{2\pi^2(2\pi)^{3/2}}\right]^{1/3}\left(\frac\xi{g_{*s}^{\rm SM}}\right)^{1/3}x^{5/6}e^{-x/3}.
$$
Indeed, equate $s_{\rm SM}=(2\pi^2/45)g_{*s}^{\rm SM}T_\gamma^3$ to $\xi s_\phi$ and use <nonrelativistic entropy at zero chemical potential>. For $g$ internal states the right side gains $g^{1/3}$. The nonrelativistic species cannot be assigned a constant relativistic entropy count.

= Kinetic decoupling
{parent=Thermal decoupling in cosmology}

Kinetic decoupling occurs when momentum-exchange collisions cease to maintain the <temperature> and <velocity dispersion> of a particle species in equilibrium with a surrounding bath. Afterwards, a freely moving nonrelativistic particle has momentum proportional to $a^{-1}$ and effective kinetic <temperature> proportional to $a^{-2}$, where $a$ is the <scale factor>.

= Temperature of a decoupled relativistic relic
{parent=Thermal decoupling in cosmology}

If a relativistic species decouples when the coupled plasma has $g_{*s,\mathrm{dec}}$ entropy degrees of freedom, then after the plasma reaches $g_{*s}$ degrees of freedom,
$$
\frac{T_{\mathrm{relic}}}{T}
=\left(\frac{g_{*s}}{g_{*s,\mathrm{dec}}}\right)^{1/3}.
$$
The ratio follows from separate redshifting of the decoupled species and <cosmological entropy conservation> in the coupled plasma.

= Two-stage entropy dilution of an early-decoupled neutrino
{parent=Temperature of a decoupled relativistic relic}
{title2=$T_\nu/T_\gamma=[43/(11g_d)]^{1/3}$}

An extra relativistic <neutrino> that decouples before ordinary <neutrinos> misses two stages of plasma heating. Until ordinary decoupling, entropy conservation gives a temperature ratio $(43/(4g_d))^{1/3}$. Electron-positron annihilation then heats <photons> but neither <neutrino> population, multiplying the ratio by $(4/11)^{1/3}$. Here $g_d$ counts the coupled bath at the extra species' decoupling, with no subsequent exotic entropy injection.

= Cosmic neutrino background
{c}
{parent=Thermal decoupling in cosmology}
{wiki}

The cosmic neutrino background is the relic population of neutrinos that thermally decoupled before electron-positron annihilation. Entropy transfer to photons gives
$$
\frac{T_\nu}{T_\gamma}=\left(\frac4{11}\right)^{1/3}.
$$

= Number density of a relativistic neutrino relative to photons
{parent=Cosmic neutrino background}
{title2=$n_\nu/n_\gamma=\tfrac{3g_\nu}{8}(T_\nu/T_\gamma)^3$}

= Relativistic-neutrino number-density ratio
{synonym}

With negligible <chemical potential>, the <Fermi-Dirac distribution> has $3/4$ of the boson number density per degree of freedom at equal temperature. A species with $g_\nu$ populated states therefore has $n_\nu/n_\gamma=(3g_\nu/8)(T_\nu/T_\gamma)^3$, since <photons> have two states. This remains the number-density ratio after the <neutrino> becomes massive if its comoving particle number is conserved.

= Relic-neutrino energy density
{parent=Cosmic neutrino background}

For one relic-neutrino species with two internal states, physical momentum $p$, comoving temperature $T_\nu$, and mass $m_\nu$,
$$
\rho_\nu=\frac1{\pi^2}\int_0^\infty
\frac{p^2\sqrt{p^2+m_\nu^2}}{e^{ap/T_\nu}+1}\,dp.
$$
It approaches $7\pi^2(T_\nu/a)^4/120$ while relativistic and $n_\nu m_\nu$ after becoming nonrelativistic.

= Late-time relic-neutrino speed
{parent=Relic-neutrino energy density}

Expanding the <relic-neutrino energy density> at $T_\nu/a\ll m_\nu$ gives
$$
\langle v^2\rangle^{1/2}\simeq
\sqrt{\frac{15\zeta(5)}{\zeta(3)}}\frac{T_\nu}{am_\nu}.
$$
The thermal speed decreases as $a^{-1}$ after the neutrinos become nonrelativistic.

= Cosmic neutrino background last-scattering surface
{parent=Cosmic neutrino background}

The cosmic neutrino background last-scattering surface is the locus from which relic neutrinos that reach an observer underwent their final appreciable scattering. Although <neutrino decoupling> occurs before <photon decoupling>, a massive neutrino travels slower than light after becoming nonrelativistic, so its last-scattering surface need not be farther away than the <cosmic microwave background> last-scattering surface.

= Neutrino decoupling
{parent=Cosmic neutrino background}
{wiki}

Neutrino decoupling occurs when weak interactions become slower than cosmic expansion. The neutrino distribution then free streams and cools as $T_\nu\propto a^{-1}$.

= Electron-positron annihilation in cosmology
{parent=Neutrino decoupling}

After neutrino decoupling, electron-positron annihilation transfers entropy to photons but not to neutrinos. Comparing the electromagnetic entropy degrees of freedom before and after annihilation gives $T_\nu/T_\gamma=(4/11)^{1/3}$.

= Electron-positron equilibrium pair abundance below the electron mass
{parent=Electron-positron annihilation in cosmology}

For $T\ll m_e$ and negligible chemical potentials, the combined equilibrium density of electrons and positrons divided by the <photon number density> is
$$
\frac{n_{e^-}+n_{e^+}}{n_\gamma}
=\frac{2\pi^2}{\zeta(3)}
\left(\frac{m_e}{2\pi T}\right)^{3/2}e^{-m_e/T}.
$$
The exponential suppression eventually makes pair equilibrium incompatible with the residual electrons required by <charge neutrality>.

= Neutrino degeneracy parameter
{title2=$\xi_\nu$}
{parent=Cosmic neutrino background}

The neutrino degeneracy parameter is the dimensionless chemical potential
$$
\xi_\nu=\frac{\mu_\nu}{T_\nu}.
$$
In thermal equilibrium the corresponding antineutrino has chemical potential $-\mu_\nu$.

= Critical-density bound on massless neutrino degeneracy
{parent=Neutrino degeneracy parameter}
{title2=$|\xi_\nu|\lesssim[8\pi^2\rho_{\rm crit}/T_{\nu,0}^4]^{1/4}$}

Combining the <degenerate neutrino and antineutrino energy density> with a <critical density> bound constrains the <neutrino degeneracy parameter> through the fourth root of the available energy budget. The numerical bound depends on $T_{\nu,0}/T_{\gamma,0}$ and the number of degenerate flavors. The standard ratio $(4/11)^{1/3}$ assumes <neutrino decoupling> before <electron-positron annihilation in cosmology>; it should be stated as a thermal-history assumption if the degeneracy is very large. The massless formula is inappropriate for a species that is nonrelativistic today.

= Degenerate neutrino and antineutrino energy density
{parent=Neutrino degeneracy parameter}
{title2=$\rho_\nu+\rho_{\bar\nu}\simeq|\mu_\nu|^4/(8\pi^2)$}

For one massless <neutrino> flavor with one helicity per member and $|\mu_\nu|\gg T_\nu$, the <Fermi-Dirac distribution> fills momentum states up to the positive <chemical potential>. If $\mu_\nu>0$, the <neutrino> density is $\mu_\nu^4/(8\pi^2)$ and the <antineutrino> contribution is approximately $3T_\nu^4e^{-\mu_\nu/T_\nu}/\pi^2$. For negative <chemical potential> the roles reverse. Thus the leading total <energy density> is even in $\mu_\nu$ and has no factor of two from the two species. Extra populated internal degrees of freedom do multiply it. This distinction prevents using a positive-degeneracy approximation for an exponentially dilute negative-chemical-potential distribution.

= Degenerate relic neutrino
{parent=Neutrino degeneracy parameter}

A degenerate relic neutrino has $\xi_\nu\gg1$. In the ultrarelativistic limit its <Fermi-Dirac distribution> approaches a filled momentum sphere, giving for one internal state
$$
\rho_\nu\simeq\frac{\mu_\nu^4}{8\pi^2},
$$
while the antineutrino density is exponentially suppressed.

= Cosmological lepton asymmetry
{parent=Neutrino degeneracy parameter}

A nonzero neutrino chemical potential produces unequal neutrino and antineutrino densities and hence a cosmological lepton asymmetry. It changes the radiation density, and an electron-neutrino asymmetry also changes neutron--proton weak equilibrium during <Big Bang nucleosynthesis>.

= Effective number of neutrino species
{title2=$N_{\rm eff}$}
{parent=Cosmic neutrino background}
{wiki}

The effective number of neutrino species parametrizes radiation beyond photons through
$$
\rho_r=\frac{\pi^2}{30}\left[2+\frac78\,2
\left(\frac4{11}\right)^{4/3}N_{\rm eff}\right]T_\gamma^4.
$$

= Contribution of a decoupled real scalar to Neff
{title2=$\Delta N_{\rm eff}$}
{parent=Effective number of neutrino species}

If a real relativistic scalar decouples when the other coupled particles have entropy degrees of freedom $g_{*s}(T_d)$, then
$$
\Delta N_{\rm eff}=\frac47
\left(\frac{43}{4g_{*s}(T_d)}\right)^{4/3}.
$$
Earlier decoupling and the subsequent annihilation of more coupled species make the scalar colder and suppress its contribution.

= Linear cosmological perturbation theory
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki=Cosmological_perturbation_theory}

Linear cosmological perturbation theory expands the metric and matter fields to first order about a homogeneous and isotropic <Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric>.

= Gauge transformation in cosmological perturbation theory
{parent=Linear cosmological perturbation theory}
{title2=$\widetilde x^\mu=x^\mu+\xi^\mu$}

= Cosmological gauge transformation
{synonym}

A cosmological gauge transformation changes the identification of a perturbed spacetime with its homogeneous background. Individual field perturbations can change without changing physical observables. At first order the metric change is the <linear metric gauge-transformation law>; matter scalars obey the corresponding background-shift rule.

= Density perturbation gauge transformation
{parent=Gauge transformation in cosmological perturbation theory}
{title2=$\widetilde{\delta\rho}=\delta\rho-\bar\rho'T$}

Density is a <scalar field>. Under a first-order time shift $\widetilde\tau=\tau+T$, its background changes by $\bar\rho'T$, so its perturbation changes by the negative of that amount. Spatial shifts act only at second order on a homogeneous background density.

= Linear metric gauge-transformation law
{parent=Gauge transformation in cosmological perturbation theory}
{title2=$\widetilde{\delta g}=\delta g-\mathcal L_\xi\bar g$}

For the passive convention $\widetilde x=x+\xi$, expanding the tensor coordinate-transformation law around a fixed background gives this <Lie derivative> formula. The background time shift and Jacobian terms must both be retained. On an <FLRW metric>, the scalar lapse, shift and spatial potentials transform under two scalar coordinate functions.

= Vector cosmological perturbation
{parent=Linear cosmological perturbation theory}

A vector cosmological perturbation is the transverse-vector sector of linear metric and matter perturbations. For a <Fourier mode> with nonzero wavevector $\mathbf k$, transversality removes the component parallel to $\mathbf k$, leaving two <helicity> components. In a gauge with $ds^2=a^2[-d\eta^2+2B_i d\eta dx^i+\delta_{ij}dx^i dx^j]$, $\partial_iB_i=0$ describes the vector metric perturbation.

= Photon energy redshift from a vector metric perturbation
{parent=Vector cosmological perturbation}
{title2=$\frac{d\ln\epsilon}{d\eta}=-e^i\dot B_i$}

For the metric in <vector cosmological perturbation>, an <orthonormal tetrad> is $E_0=a^{-1}\partial_\eta$, $E_i=a^{-1}(B_i\partial_\eta+\partial_i)$ to first order. A <photon> has $p^0=(\epsilon/a^2)(1+B_ie^i)$ and $p^i=(\epsilon/a^2)e^i$, so the covariant component is $p_0=-\epsilon+O(B^2)$. The covariant <geodesic equation> gives $dp_0/d\eta=(2p^0)^{-1}\partial_\eta g_{\alpha\beta}p^\alpha p^\beta=\epsilon e^i\dot B_i$. The <derivative> of the <scale factor> cancels by the <null vector> condition, proving the redshift formula.

= Adiabatic mode
{parent=Linear cosmological perturbation theory}

An adiabatic mode is a long-wavelength physical perturbation whose leading local field configuration is generated by a residual gauge transformation. Its action on shorter modes produces a cosmological soft theorem or consistency relation.

= Superhorizon growing density mode of adiabatic matter and radiation
{parent=Adiabatic mode}
{title2=$\Delta_c\propto\tau^2$}

An adiabatic perturbation obeys $\Delta_r=(4/3)\Delta_c$. In a radiation background this makes the cold-matter density equation $\Delta_c''+\Delta_c'/\tau=4\Delta_c/\tau^2$, whose growing solution is $\tau^2$. In a matter background it instead becomes $\Delta_c''+2\Delta_c'/\tau=6\Delta_c/\tau^2$, with the same growing conformal-time exponent and a different scale-factor dependence.

= Tensor cosmological perturbation
{title2=$h_{ij}$}
{parent=Linear cosmological perturbation theory}

A tensor cosmological perturbation is the transverse, trace-free part of a metric perturbation about a <Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric>. In conformal coordinates, its spatial metric has the form $g_{ij}=a^2(\delta_{ij}+h_{ij})$, with $\partial_i h_{ij}=0$ and $h_{ii}=0$. Its two independent polarizations describe a <gravitational wave>. <Neutrino tensor anisotropic stress> can damp its evolution through <neutrino free streaming>.

= Helicity selection for tensor CMB multipoles
{parent=Tensor cosmological perturbation}
{title2=$m=2\quad\text{for the specified complex helicity mode}$}

A complex helicity-$+2$ plane wave along the $z$ axis has polarization contraction $\sin^2\theta e^{2i\varphi}$. Its remaining <tensor CMB line-of-sight source> depends only on $\cos\theta$. Orthogonality of the azimuthal factors of <spherical harmonics> therefore restricts its temperature coefficients to $m=2$. A real perturbation also includes the conjugate mode.

= Long-wavelength tensor CMB quadrupole and octupole
{parent=Helicity selection for tensor CMB multipoles}
{title2=$\Theta_{32}/\Theta_{22}=-ik\eta_0/(3\sqrt7)$}

For a matter-era superhorizon tensor with $h=h_{\mathrm{prim}}[1-(k\eta)^2/10+\cdots]$, the <tensor CMB line-of-sight source> gives $\Theta_{22}\propto h_{\mathrm{prim}}(k\eta_0)^2$ and $\Theta_{32}/\Theta_{22}=-ik\eta_0/(3\sqrt7)$ when $\eta_*\ll\eta_0$. The extra spatial phase makes the octupole one order smaller.

= Photon energy redshift from a tensor metric perturbation
{parent=Tensor cosmological perturbation}
{title2=$d\log\epsilon/d\eta=-\dot h_{ij}e^ie^j/2$}

For a <tensor cosmological perturbation> $h_{ij}$, the time <geodesic equation> in a local <orthonormal tetrad> gives $d\log\epsilon/d\eta=-\dot h_{ij}e^ie^j/2$. The photon comoving energy removes the background redshift; the dot is a partial time derivative, whereas the energy derivative follows the ray.

= Neutrino tensor anisotropic stress
{title2=$\pi_\nu^{ij}$}
{parent=Tensor cosmological perturbation}

For massless <neutrinos> with a temperature perturbation $\Theta$ and background <energy density> $\bar\rho_\nu$, the physical trace-free spatial <stress-energy tensor> is
$$
\pi_\nu^{ij}=4\bar\rho_\nu\int\frac{d\Omega_e}{4\pi}
\Theta\left(e^ie^j-\frac13\delta^{ij}\right).
$$
Some conventions define $\Pi^{ij}=-\pi_\nu^{ij}$ and correspondingly reverse the sign of the stress source in the gravitational-wave equation.

= Neutrino tensor free-streaming kernel
{title2=$K(x)=j_2(x)/x^2$}
{parent=Neutrino tensor anisotropic stress}

Projecting free-streaming <neutrinos> onto the tensor <spherical harmonic> gives
$$
K(x)=\frac1{16}\int_{-1}^{1}(1-\mu^2)^2e^{-ix\mu}\,d\mu
=\frac{j_2(x)}{x^2},\qquad K(0)=\frac1{15}.
$$
Here $j_2$ is a <spherical Bessel function>. The continuous value at zero follows by integrating $(1-\mu^2)^2$, whose integral is $16/15$.

= Gravitational-wave damping by free-streaming neutrinos
{parent=Neutrino tensor anisotropic stress}

For either tensor <helicity>, <neutrino free streaming> produces the memory equation
$$
\ddot h+2\mathcal H\dot h+k^2h
=-24\mathcal H^2f_\nu\int_0^\eta
K(k(\eta-\eta'))\dot h(\eta')\,d\eta',
\qquad
f_\nu=\frac{\bar\rho_\nu}{\bar\rho_{\rm tot}}.
$$
The <neutrino tensor free-streaming kernel> encodes the angular propagation of the perturbation. Energy transferred to neutrino directional anisotropy damps the oscillation amplitude of a <gravitational wave> after horizon entry. This effect was calculated by https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0306304[Weinberg].

= Scalar cosmological perturbation
{parent=Linear cosmological perturbation theory}

A scalar cosmological perturbation transforms as a scalar under spatial rotations and can be represented using scalar metric potentials, density and pressure perturbations, and the longitudinal part of the fluid velocity.

= Weyl lensing potential
{parent=Scalar cosmological perturbation}
{c}
{title2=$\Psi=(\phi+\psi)/2$}

The average of the two <conformal Newtonian gauge> potentials controls scalar <gravitational lensing>. A conformal rescaling makes the spatial optical metric flat and its time component $1+4\Psi$ to first order. Vanishing <scalar anisotropic stress> makes $\phi=\psi$ in general relativity, but lensing depends on their sum even when they differ.

= Scalar photon energy redshift
{parent=Scalar cosmological perturbation}
{title2=$d\ln\epsilon/d\eta=\phi^{\prime}-\mathbf e\cdot\nabla\psi$}

For the <Newtonian gauge in cosmology> with potentials $\psi,\phi$, photon comoving momentum $\epsilon$ obeys $d\ln\epsilon/d\eta=\phi'-\mathbf e\cdot\nabla\psi=-d\psi/d\eta+\phi'+\psi'$. The homogeneous physical-energy redshift is separated by $E=\epsilon/a$. The total derivative follows the unperturbed photon path at linear order.

= Perturbed Klein-Gordon equation with background lapse
{parent=Scalar cosmological perturbation}
{title2=$\delta\Pi=\mathcal D\delta\phi-\bar\Pi\Psi$}

Let $\mathcal D=\bar N^{-1}\partial_t$ and $\bar\Pi=\mathcal D\bar\phi$. For negative-expansion <extrinsic curvature of a spatial hypersurface>, its trace perturbation is $\kappa$. With $\delta\Pi=\mathcal D\delta\phi-\bar\Pi\Psi$, scalar evolution is $\mathcal D\delta\Pi-\Psi\mathcal D\bar\Pi+3H\delta\Pi-\kappa\bar\Pi-\Delta\delta\phi+V_{,\phi\phi}\delta\phi=0$. Combining background acceleration terms produces the standard lapse source $-\bar\Pi(\kappa+\mathcal D\Psi-3H\Psi)+2\Psi V_{,\phi}$.

= Comoving gauge in cosmology
{parent=Scalar cosmological perturbation}
{title2=$\delta q=0$}

A <comoving gauge in cosmology> chooses hypersurfaces with zero scalar momentum density for the specified fluid or total system. For cold <pressureless matter> it can be implemented by taking the matter velocity potential to vanish. The choice of component must be stated in a multi-fluid system; <density contrasts> on this slicing can differ from those in a Newtonian or spatially flat gauge.

= Uniform-density curvature perturbation
{parent=Scalar cosmological perturbation}
{title2=$\zeta$}

= Uniform-density curvature
{synonym}

= Constant-density curvature perturbation
{synonym}

The uniform-density curvature perturbation is the spatial scalar curvature measured on density-defined hypersurfaces, with an explicitly chosen sign convention. If the spatial scalar potentials are $C,E$, one convention is $\zeta=-C+\nabla^2E/3+\mathcal H\delta\rho/\bar\rho'$. Its two time-shift terms cancel under a <cosmological gauge transformation>. The density derivative must be nonzero. This is a different slicing from the <comoving curvature perturbation>, although the variables are related for regular adiabatic long-wavelength modes, with signs depending on convention.

= Uniform-density curvature sign test
{parent=Uniform-density curvature perturbation}
{title2=$\zeta=-\Phi+\delta\rho/[3(\bar\rho+\bar P)]$}

For spatial metric $a^2(1-2\Phi)\delta_{ij}$, the combination $\zeta=-\Phi+\delta\rho/[3(\bar\rho+\bar P)]$ is invariant under a time displacement: $\Phi$ shifts by $\bar NH T$ and $\delta\rho$ by $3\bar NH(\bar\rho+\bar P)T$. The opposite overall sign is also valid. The sum $\Phi+\delta\rho/[3(\bar\rho+\bar P)]$ is not invariant. This is a useful convention check before applying <superhorizon conservation of uniform-density curvature>.

= Superhorizon conservation of uniform-density curvature
{parent=Uniform-density curvature perturbation}
{title2=$\zeta'\simeq0$}

With the convention $\zeta=\Phi-\delta\rho/[3(\bar\rho+\bar P)]$, energy conservation gives $\zeta'=\mathcal H\delta P_{\rm nad}/(\bar\rho+\bar P)+(\nabla\cdot\mathbf v)/3$. The <non-adiabatic pressure perturbation> vanishes for <adiabatic cosmological perturbations>, and regular velocity gradients are suppressed on <superhorizon scales>. Curvature is then conserved to leading gradient order, allowing primordial initial conditions to be propagated between cosmological eras.

= Scalar gauge transformations with a background lapse
{parent=Scalar cosmological perturbation}
{title2=$\delta\widetilde g=\delta g-\mathcal L_\xi\bar g$}

For $N=\bar N(1+\Psi)$, $g_{0i}=a^2\partial_iB$ and spatial scalar convention $h_{ij}=a^2[(1-2\Phi)\delta_{ij}-2\partial_i\partial_jE]$, a passive displacement $(\xi^0,\partial^i\lambda)$ gives $\widetilde\Psi=\Psi-\dot\xi^0-(\dot{\bar N}/\bar N)\xi^0$, $\widetilde B=B+\bar N^2\xi^0/a^2-\dot\lambda$, $\widetilde\Phi=\Phi+(\dot a/a)\xi^0$ and $\widetilde E=E+\lambda$. These follow from subtracting the background metric Lie derivative.

= Scalar shear potential
{parent=Scalar cosmological perturbation}
{title2=$\chi=\frac{a^2}{\bar N}(\dot E-B)$}

For the negative <extrinsic curvature> convention and a <shift vector> entering the line element as $dx^i-N^i dt$, the trace-free scalar curvature perturbation is $\delta K^i{}_j{}_{\rm TF}=-(\partial^i\partial_j-\delta^i_j\Delta/3)\chi$. Here spatial raised derivatives use $a^{-2}\delta^{ij}$, so $\Delta=a^{-2}\nabla^2$. Changing the sign of the metric <shift vector> changes the corresponding definition of $\chi$.

= Scalar expansion perturbation
{parent=Scalar cosmological perturbation}
{title2=$\kappa=\delta K$}

With negative <extrinsic curvature> convention, the background <trace> is $\bar K=-3H$. The scalar expansion perturbation is $\kappa=K+3H$. For <lapse function> $N=\bar N(1+\Psi)$, <induced metric> $h_{ij}=a^2[(1-2\Phi)\delta_{ij}+2E_{,ij}]$ and <shift vector> $N_i=-a^2B_{,i}$, it is $\kappa=3(\dot\Phi/\bar N+H\Psi)-\Delta\chi$, where $\chi$ is the <scalar shear potential>. The expansion of the future normal congruence is $-K$, so its perturbation has the opposite sign.

= Positive-shift scalar shear convention
{parent=Scalar expansion perturbation}
{title2=$\kappa=3(\mathcal D\Phi+H\Psi)+\Delta\chi_+$}

For $N_i=a^2B_{,i}$, $\gamma_{ij}=a^2[(1-2\Phi)\delta_{ij}+2E_{,ij}]$ and $K_{ij}=-(\dot\gamma_{ij}-D_iN_j-D_jN_i)/(2N)$, put $\chi_+=a^2(B-\dot E)/\bar N$. Then $K^i{}_j=-H\delta^i{}_j+(\dot\Phi/\bar N+H\Psi)\delta^i{}_j+\partial^i\partial_j\chi_+$ and $\kappa=3(\dot\Phi/\bar N+H\Psi)+\Delta\chi_+$. Defining $\chi=-\chi_+$ instead makes the trace-free tensor coefficient negative and the trace gradient term $-\Delta\chi$. A sign change must affect both pieces.

= Synchronous gauge in cosmology
{parent=Scalar cosmological perturbation}
{wiki=Synchronous_gauge}

Synchronous gauge sets the lapse and shift perturbations to zero, $h_{0\mu}=0$. Its coordinates follow freely falling observers, but residual coordinate freedom produces gauge modes that must be distinguished from physical perturbations.

= Synchronous perfect-fluid density equation
{parent=Synchronous gauge in cosmology}
{title2=$\delta'=(1+w)(k^2\theta-h'/2)$}

For constant $P=w\rho$ and <velocity potential> $v_i=ik_i\theta$, <stress-energy conservation> gives the displayed density equation in <synchronous gauge in cosmology>. Here $h$ is the trace of the spatial metric perturbation, and primes denote <conformal time>. In particular cold baryons obey $\delta_b'=k^2\theta_b-h'/2$. A positive sign in front of $k^2\theta_b$ on the left contradicts this potential convention.

= Residual synchronous-gauge freedom
{parent=Synchronous gauge in cosmology}
{title2=$\xi^0=C/\bar N,\quad\lambda=C\int\bar N a^{-2}dt+D$}

= Residual gauge freedom in synchronous gauge
{synonym}

Preserving $\Psi=B=0$ under the <scalar gauge transformations with a background lapse> requires $\bar N\xi^0=C(\mathbf x)$ and $\dot\lambda=C(\mathbf x)\bar N/a^2$. Thus arbitrary spatial functions $C,D$ remain after imposing <synchronous gauge in cosmology>. This freedom can generate changes in the remaining scalar metric perturbations.

= Cosmological Euler equation in synchronous gauge
{c}
{parent=Synchronous gauge in cosmology}

For scalar perturbations of a generic fluid in cosmic time, momentum conservation in <synchronous gauge in cosmology> gives
$$
\delta P+\nabla^2\pi^S
+\partial_t[(\bar\rho+\bar P)\delta u]
+3H(\bar\rho+\bar P)\delta u=0.
$$

= Newtonian gauge in cosmology
{c}
{parent=Scalar cosmological perturbation}
{wiki=Newtonian_gauge}

= Conformal Newtonian gauge
{synonym}

= Newtonian gauge
{synonym}
{c}

Newtonian gauge removes scalar shear and writes scalar metric perturbations in terms of gravitational potentials. The fluid Euler equation then contains an explicit gradient of the Newtonian potential.

= Conformal optical metric for scalar perturbations
{parent=Newtonian gauge in cosmology}
{title2=$d\widehat s^2=(1+4\Psi)d\eta^2-\delta_{ij}dx^idx^j$}

Rescale the perturbed cosmological metric by $a^{-2}(1+2\phi)$. To first order, its spatial part is Euclidean and only its lapse is perturbed. The unparameterized <null geodesics> are unchanged. The nonzero linear connection coefficients are $\widehat\Gamma^0_{00}=2\partial_\eta\Psi$, $\widehat\Gamma^0_{0i}=2\partial_i\Psi$ and $\widehat\Gamma^i_{00}=2\partial^i\Psi$. The affine parameter is that of the rescaled metric, not necessarily that of the original spacetime.

= First-order photon trajectory in scalar perturbations
{parent=Conformal optical metric for scalar perturbations}
{title2=$x^i=e^i(\eta-\eta_0)+\delta x^i$}

For reception at the origin with unit propagation direction $e$, the <null geodesic> condition fixes $v(\eta_0)=(1+2\Psi_0)e$. The perturbative spatial acceleration is $x^{i\prime\prime}=2e^i d\Psi/d\eta-2(\delta^{ij}-e^ie^j)\partial_j\Psi$. Its integrated trajectory is $x^i=e^i(\eta-\eta_0)+2e^i\int_{\eta_0}^{\eta}\Psi\,d\eta^{\prime}-2\int_{\eta_0}^{\eta}(\eta-\eta^{\prime})(\delta^{ij}-e^ie^j)\partial_j\Psi\,d\eta^{\prime}$. Potentials in these already first-order integrals can be evaluated on the straight background ray.

= Gravitational potential evolution of a barotropic fluid
{parent=Newtonian gauge in cosmology}
{title2=$\Phi_k''+3(1+w)\mathcal H\Phi_k'+wk^2\Phi_k=0$}

For a flat background dominated by one constant-$w$ <barotropic equation of state>, assume $\delta P=w\delta\rho$ and no <scalar anisotropic stress>. The <Einstein field equations> then reduce the common Newtonian-gauge potential to the stated <Fourier transform> equation, with $\mathcal H=2/[(1+3w)\tau]$. For radiation the regular solution is $3\Phi_0(\sin x-x\cos x)/x^3$, $x=k\tau/\sqrt3$, and oscillates with a decaying envelope after sound-horizon entry. For pressureless matter the two solutions are a constant and $\tau^{-5}$. A non-adiabatic pressure perturbation would supply an additional source, so constant background $w$ alone does not justify the homogeneous equation.

= Cosmological Euler equation
{parent=Newtonian gauge in cosmology}

For pressureless matter in conformal time and Newtonian gauge,
$$
\mathbf v'+\mathcal H\mathbf v+(\mathbf v\mathbin\cdot\nabla)\mathbf v
=-\nabla\phi.
$$
Its linear curl implies that vorticity decays as $a^{-1}$.

= Linear matter density equation in Newtonian gauge
{parent=Cosmological Euler equation}
{title2=$\delta_m''+\mathcal H\delta_m'$}

For <pressureless matter> in <Newtonian gauge in cosmology> with a common metric potential $\Phi$, let $\theta=\nabla\cdot\mathbf v$. The linear equations $\delta_m'=-\theta+3\Phi'$ and $\theta'=-\mathcal H\theta-\nabla^2\Phi$, where primes denote <conformal time>, yield
$$
\delta_m''+\mathcal H\delta_m'=\nabla^2\Phi+3(\Phi''+\mathcal H\Phi').
$$
This follows by differentiating the first equation and eliminating $\theta$. On deeply subhorizon scales with a slowly varying matter potential, the metric time derivatives are small compared with its spatial <Laplacian>.

= Cosmological Poisson equation
{parent=Newtonian gauge in cosmology}

On subhorizon scales, the Newtonian potential sourced by the matter density contrast obeys
$$
\nabla^2\phi=\frac32\mathcal H^2\Omega_m\delta.
$$

= Scalar velocity potential
{title2=$\delta u$}
{parent=Scalar cosmological perturbation}

The longitudinal velocity perturbation of a cosmological fluid can be represented by a scalar velocity potential through $\delta T^0{}_i=(\bar\rho+\bar P)\partial_i\delta u$.

= Scalar anisotropic stress
{title2=$\pi^S$}
{parent=Scalar cosmological perturbation}

Scalar anisotropic stress is the trace-free scalar part of the spatial stress perturbation. Under a common convention it enters as $\delta T^i{}_j=\delta P\,\delta^i_j+\partial^i\partial_j\pi^S$.

= Scalar neutrino anisotropic stress
{parent=Scalar anisotropic stress}
{title2=$\Pi_\nu=-3\Theta_2/5$}

Using the unweighted <neutrino Boltzmann hierarchy> convention and $\Pi_{ij}=-(4\bar\rho_\nu/3)\Pi_\nu(\hat k_i\hat k_j-\delta_{ij}/3)$, the scalar <scalar anisotropic stress> is $\Pi_\nu=-3\Theta_2/5$. Only the quadrupole contributes, since the trace-free tensor $e_ie_j-\delta_{ij}/3$ has angular degree two. Different stress and multipole sign conventions change this displayed coefficient.

= Radiation-era adiabatic potentials with free-streaming neutrinos
{parent=Scalar neutrino anisotropic stress}
{title2=$\phi=(1+2f_\nu/5)\psi$}

For regular <adiabatic initial conditions> on <superhorizon scales> during <radiation domination>, the unweighted <neutrino Boltzmann hierarchy> gives $\Theta_1=\psi k\eta/2$ and $\Theta_2=\psi(k\eta)^2/6$. With $f_\nu=\bar\rho_\nu/(\bar\rho_\nu+\bar\rho_\gamma)$ and neutrinos the only source of <scalar anisotropic stress>, the trace-free <Einstein field equations> gives $\phi=(1+2f_\nu/5)\psi$. For the sign convention $\mathcal R=-\phi-\psi/2$,
$$
\psi=-\frac{10}{15+4f_\nu}\mathcal R,\qquad\phi=-\frac{10+4f_\nu}{15+4f_\nu}\mathcal R.
$$

= Flat gauge in cosmology
{parent=Scalar cosmological perturbation}

Flat gauge sets the scalar perturbation of the spatial metric to zero. Scalar fluctuations then reside in the lapse, shift, and matter fields, making the field fluctuation directly visible while the lapse and shift remain constrained variables.

= Cosmological transfer function
{parent=Linear cosmological perturbation theory}

A cosmological transfer function relates a late perturbation to a specified primordial perturbation mode by mode. It records the scale-dependent evolution caused by horizon entry, pressure, free streaming, and changes in the dominant cosmic component.

= Radiation acoustic transfer function
{parent=Cosmological transfer function}

During matter domination with nearly constant gravitational potential, a radiation density perturbation obeys a forced acoustic equation. The observable <Sachs-Wolfe combination> is the homogeneous oscillatory part.

= Subhorizon radiation acoustic oscillations
{parent=Radiation acoustic transfer function}
{title2=$\Delta_r\sim C\cos(k\tau/\sqrt3+\vartheta)$}

= Radiation acoustic oscillation
{synonym}

Pressure-supported radiation perturbations well inside the horizon oscillate rather than exhibit secular gravitational growth. In the ideal relativistic fluid, $c_s^2=1/3$ and the leading conformal frequency is $k/\sqrt3$. Their rapidly changing source has a small late-time response in the slowly varying cold-matter density mode.

= Suppressed cold-matter forcing by rapid radiation oscillations
{parent=Subhorizon radiation acoustic oscillations}
{title2=$\Delta_{c,\rm osc}=O[\Delta_r/(k\tau)^2]$}

In deep <radiation domination>, an oscillatory radiation density source in the matter equation has order $\Delta_r/\tau^2$ and frequency of order $k$. Its rapidly varying particular response is therefore of order $\Delta_r/(k\tau)^2$. For $k\tau\gg1$ it does not control the secular matter growth, though forcing near horizon entry sets the constant and logarithmic homogeneous amplitudes.

= Cold-dark-matter transfer function
{title2=CDM transfer function}
{parent=Cosmological transfer function}

At fixed time after equality, the comoving cold-dark-matter density contrast per unit primordial curvature grows as $k^2$ for $k\ll k_{\rm eq}$ and only logarithmically for $k\gg k_{\rm eq}$. Small-scale modes entered during radiation domination and could grow only logarithmically until equality.

= Asymptotic cold-dark-matter power spectrum
{parent=Cold-dark-matter transfer function}
{title2=$P_m\propto k^{n_s}T(k)^2$}

The dimensional <matter power spectrum> contains a factor $k^{n_s}$, rather than $k^{n_s+3}$. The <cosmological transfer function> tends to one below the <matter-radiation equality scale> and to $\ln(k/k_{\rm eq})/(k/k_{\rm eq})^2$ above it. The smooth spectrum therefore scales as $k^{n_s}$ at small wavenumber and $k^{n_s-4}\ln^2(k/k_{\rm eq})$ at large wavenumber. The logarithmic factor records slow cold-matter growth during <radiation domination>. Multiplying by $k^3$ instead produces dimensionless matter power.

= Logarithmic small-scale matter transfer
{parent=Cold-dark-matter transfer function}
{title2=$T(k)\propto\log(k/k_{\rm eq})(k_{\rm eq}/k)^2$}

Modes with $k\gg k_{\rm eq}$ enter the horizon during <radiation domination>. Their matter perturbations grow only logarithmically until <matter-radiation equality>, rather than as $a$. Matching the radiation-era logarithmic growth onto the matter-era growing mode gives the large-$k$ <cosmological transfer function> scaling shown, up to constants and the matching scale inside the logarithm. For a primordial <scalar spectral index> $n_s$, the late-time linear <cosmological density power spectrum> consequently changes from $P(k)\propto k^{n_s}$ at small $k$ to $P(k)\propto k^{n_s-4}\log^2(k/k_{\rm eq})$ at large $k$. These scalings omit baryonic acoustic structure and nonlinear evolution.

Here the <cold-dark-matter transfer function> is normalized so that $\delta_m(k,a)=A(a)k^2T(k)\mathcal R(k)$, where $\mathcal R$ is the primordial <comoving curvature perturbation> and $A(a)$ is independent of $k$. During <radiation domination>, horizon entry occurs at $a_{\rm ent}\propto k^{-1}$. An adiabatic entry amplitude of order $\mathcal R$ then acquires a factor $\log(a_{\rm eq}/a_{\rm ent})\sim\log(k/k_{\rm eq})$ by <matter-radiation equality>. Subsequent <matter domination> supplies a common growth factor; division by the conventional $k^2$ prefactor therefore gives $T(k)\propto k^{-2}\log(k/k_{\rm eq})$.

= Logarithmic high-wavenumber density-spectrum transfer
{parent=Logarithmic small-scale matter transfer}
{title2=$\mathscr P_c\propto(\tau/\tau_{\rm eq})^4\ln^2(k\tau_{\rm eq})$}

A scale-invariant curvature seed gives wavenumber-independent dimensionless matter power at radiation-era horizon entry. Horizon entry at $\tau_h\sim1/k$, followed by logarithmic growth to equality, gives a density transfer proportional to $\ln(k\tau_{\rm eq})$. Subsequent matter-era growth is proportional to $\tau^2$, so the dimensionless density power is proportional to $(\tau/\tau_{\rm eq})^4\ln^2(k\tau_{\rm eq})$. This is a high-wavenumber asymptote, not an expression at the transition point.

= Baryon transfer function
{parent=Cosmological transfer function}

The baryon transfer function retains acoustic oscillations generated while baryons belonged to the <photon-baryon fluid>. After recombination, baryons fall into cold-dark-matter potential wells, reducing the relative perturbation while leaving a small oscillatory imprint.

= Baryon--cold-dark-matter relative density mode
{title2=$D=\delta_c-\delta_b$}
{parent=Baryon transfer function}

After recombination and when pressure is negligible, cold dark matter and baryons feel the same gravitational acceleration. Their density-contrast difference therefore obeys
$$
\ddot D+2H\dot D=0.
$$
During matter domination $D=D_0+D_1a^{-1/2}$, whereas the growing total-matter mode is proportional to $a$, so $\delta_c/\delta_b\to1$.

= Field theory in cosmology
{parent=Cosmology}

Field theory in cosmology applies classical and quantum fields to the homogeneous universe and its perturbations.

= Canonical rescaling of a scalar cosmological perturbation
{parent=Field theory in cosmology}
{title2=$v=a\delta\chi$}

Neglecting <metric perturbations>, a free massive <scalar field> fluctuation obeys $\delta\chi''+2\mathcal H\delta\chi'-\nabla^2\delta\chi+a^2m^2\delta\chi=0$. Rescaling it by the <scale factor> removes the conformal-time friction and gives $v''-\nabla^2v+(a^2m^2-a''/a)v=0$. Growth of $v$ need not mean growth of the physical fluctuation $v/a$.

= Massive de Sitter superhorizon scalar mode
{parent=Canonical rescaling of a scalar cosmological perturbation}
{title2=$\nu=\sqrt{9/4-m^2/H^2}$}

In the <de Sitter approximation>, a canonically rescaled scalar has superhorizon powers $(-\tau)^{1/2\pm\nu}$. The physical field has powers $(-\tau)^{3/2\pm\nu}$. For $H^2<m^2<2H^2$, both physical branches decay, while the slower rescaled branch grows. The generic late-time <dimensionless power spectrum> is proportional to $(k/(aH))^{3-2\nu}$.

= P(X, phi) scalar field theory
{title2=$P(X,\phi)$ theory}
{c}
{parent=Field theory in cosmology}
{wiki=K-essence}

A $P(X,\phi)$ scalar theory has Lagrangian density $P$ depending on the field and $X=-g^{\mu\nu}\partial_\mu\phi\partial_\nu\phi/2$. It includes canonical scalar theory and models with noncanonical kinetic interactions.

= Sound speed of a P(X, phi) scalar perturbation
{title2=$c_s^2$}
{c}
{parent=P(X, phi) scalar field theory}

On a homogeneous background with $X=\bar X$, a $P(X,\phi)$ perturbation has sound speed
$$
c_s^2=\frac{P_X}{P_X+2XP_{XX}}.
$$
The denominator is its time-kinetic coefficient and $P_X$ is its spatial-gradient coefficient.

= Noncanonical scalar kinetic stability
{parent=Sound speed of a P(X, phi) scalar perturbation}
{title2=$P_{,X}+2XP_{,XX}>0,\quad P_{,X}>0$}

For $X=\partial_\mu\phi\partial^\mu\phi/2$ in signature $(+---)$, the derivative part of the quadratic action on a timelike background is $\tfrac12(P_{,X}+2XP_{,XX})\delta\dot\phi^2-\tfrac12P_{,X}a^{-2}(\nabla\delta\phi)^2$. Absence of a ghost requires $P_{,X}+2XP_{,XX}>0$; positive gradient energy requires $P_{,X}>0$. The squared sound speed is their ratio $P_{,X}/(P_{,X}+2XP_{,XX})$.

= Cubic curvature action for a P(X, phi) scalar field
{parent=P(X, phi) scalar field theory}

In the limit where <scalar field> fluctuations dominate metric fluctuations, put $\zeta=-H\delta\phi/\dot{\bar\phi}$ and neglect derivatives of slowly varying background coefficients. Splitting $\delta X=\delta X_1+\delta X_2$ gives
$$
\delta X_1=-\frac{2\bar X}{H}\dot\zeta,\qquad
\delta X_2=\frac{\bar X}{H^2}\left(\dot\zeta^2-a^{-2}(\partial_i\zeta)^2\right).
$$
The cubic terms $P_{XX}\delta X_1\delta X_2+P_{XXX}\delta X_1^3/6$ therefore yield
$$
S_3=\int dt\,d^3x\,\frac{a^3\epsilon(1-c_s^2)}{Hc_s^2}
\left[a^{-2}\dot\zeta(\partial_i\zeta)^2+\mathcal A\dot\zeta^3\right],
\qquad
\mathcal A=-1-\frac{2\bar X P_{XXX}}{3P_{XX}}.
$$
The expression before factoring remains well-defined when $P_{XX}=0$. These interactions can generate substantial <primordial non-Gaussianity> when the <sound speed of a P(X, phi) scalar perturbation> is small.

= Derivative-cubic coefficient of a noncanonical scalar
{parent=Cubic curvature action for a P(X, phi) scalar field}
{title2=$C=-\frac{a^3}{H^3}(2\bar X^2P_{,XX}+\frac43\bar X^3P_{,XXX})$}

Neglect metric fluctuations and derivatives of $\dot{\bar\phi}/H$, so $\delta X_1=-2\bar X\dot\zeta/H$ and $\delta X_2=\bar X[\dot\zeta^2-a^{-2}(\nabla\zeta)^2]/H^2$. The coefficient of $\dot\zeta^3$ in the cosmic-time action is $C=-a^3[2\bar X^2P_{,XX}+(4/3)\bar X^3P_{,XXX}]/H^3$. This unfactored form is valid even when $P_{,XX}=0$ and a factored ratio would be undefined.

= ADM momentum constraint for a P(X, phi) scalar field
{title2=ADM momentum constraint for a $P(X,\phi)$ scalar field}
{c}
{parent=P(X, phi) scalar field theory}

Varying the shift in the <3+1 decomposition of spacetime> gives a nondynamical momentum constraint. For a $P(X,\phi)$ field it equates the spatial divergence of $K_i{}^j-\delta_i^jK$ to the scalar momentum density.

= Primordial non-Gaussianity
{parent=Field theory in cosmology}
{wiki=Non-Gaussianity}

Primordial non-Gaussianity is any departure of the primordial perturbations from Gaussian random fields. Cubic interactions generate a nonzero three-point correlation function at leading order.

= Local-type primordial non-Gaussianity
{parent=Primordial non-Gaussianity}
{title2=$f_{\rm NL}$}

A local quadratic departure from a <Gaussian random field> is
$$
\varphi(\mathbf x)=\varphi_G(\mathbf x)+f_{\rm NL}\bigl(\varphi_G(\mathbf x)^2-\langle\varphi_G^2\rangle\bigr).
$$
The subtraction removes the homogeneous mean. At leading order, <Wick theorem> gives the <primordial bispectrum> $B_\varphi=2f_{\rm NL}[P_\varphi(k_1)P_\varphi(k_2)+\text{cyclic terms}]$. The numerical convention depends on which potential or <comoving curvature perturbation> is used.

= Loop correction to the local primordial bispectrum
{parent=Local-type primordial non-Gaussianity}
{title2=$B_{\mathrm{loop}}=O(f_{\mathrm{NL}}^3)$}

The exact <centered quadratic Gaussian transformation> has a connected three-quadratic-vertex contribution $8\alpha^3\int d^3q\,P(q)P(|\mathbf k_1-\mathbf q|)P(|\mathbf k_2+\mathbf q|)/(2\pi)^3$. It is higher order than the usual tree-level local <primordial bispectrum>. Infrared or ultraviolet regularization may be needed.

= Primordial contribution to the matter bispectrum
{parent=Local-type primordial non-Gaussianity}
{title2=$B_m^{\rm prim}$}

If the linear <density contrast> obeys $\delta(\mathbf k)=\alpha(k)\varphi(\mathbf k)$, then <local-type primordial non-Gaussianity> contributes
$$
B_m^{\rm prim}=2f_{\rm NL}\left[\frac{\alpha_3}{\alpha_1\alpha_2}P_m(k_1)P_m(k_2)+\text{cyclic terms}\right].
$$
In a <squeezed bispectrum configuration> and for a nearly scale-invariant potential, the leading term is $4f_{\rm NL}P_m(k_L)P_m(k_S)/\alpha(k_L)$. This excludes the <standard perturbation theory in cosmology> contribution from nonlinear gravity.

= Short-scale variance modulation by local non-Gaussianity
{parent=Local-type primordial non-Gaussianity}

For a long potential approximately constant over the short-scale region, <local-type primordial non-Gaussianity> gives $\varphi_s=(1+2f_{\rm NL}\varphi_{G,L})\varphi_{G,s}$, to first order in the long mode and ignoring short-mode squares. A linear <cosmological Poisson equation> therefore gives
$$
\sigma_s^2=\sigma_{G,s}^2(1+4f_{\rm NL}\varphi_{G,L})
$$
to the same order. The <variance> is conditional on the long mode.

= Primordial bispectrum
{title2=$B(k_1,k_2,k_3)$}
{parent=Primordial non-Gaussianity}
{wiki=Bispectrum}

Statistical homogeneity defines the primordial bispectrum by
$$
\langle\phi_{\mathbf k_1}\phi_{\mathbf k_2}\phi_{\mathbf k_3}\rangle
=(2\pi)^3\delta^{(3)}(\mathbf k_1+\mathbf k_2+\mathbf k_3)
B(k_1,k_2,k_3).
$$

= Spatial-gradient cubic curvature bispectrum
{parent=Primordial bispectrum}
{title2=$H_{\rm int}=g\int d^3x\,a\zeta(\partial\zeta)^2$}

With cosmic-time interaction coefficient $g$, constant $H,\epsilon,c_s$, and modes whose positive-frequency part is $H(1+ikc_s\tau)e^{-ikc_s\tau}/\sqrt{4\epsilon c_sk^3}$ in units $M_p=1$, two <Wick contractions> per differentiated pair and the <late-time scalar gradient vertex integral> give
$$
B=-\frac{gH^4}{16\epsilon^3c_s^2\prod_i k_i^3}\left(\sum_{i<j}\mathbf k_i\cdot\mathbf k_j\right)\left[-K+\frac{s_2}{K}+\frac{s_3}{K^2}\right].
$$
Here $\langle\zeta^3\rangle_c=(2\pi)^3\delta^3(\sum\mathbf k_i)B$. The extra factor $a$ in $dt=a\,d\tau$ must be included. A positive $g$ yields a negative bispectrum in an <equilateral bispectrum configuration>; changing the sign of the interaction changes the sign of $B$.

= Curvature bispectrum from a zeta zeta-prime-squared interaction
{parent=Primordial bispectrum}
{title2=$B_{\zeta\zeta\prime^2}$}

For $H_{\mathrm{int}}=-M_{\mathrm{Pl}}^2\int d^3x\,a^3\epsilon^2\zeta\dot\zeta^2$ and the standard Gaussian de Sitter modes, the tree <in-in formalism> gives $B=H^4\sum_{\mathrm{cyc}}k_2^2k_3^2(K^{-1}+k_1K^{-2})/[16\epsilon M_{\mathrm{Pl}}^4(k_1k_2k_3)^3]$, where $K=\sum_i k_i$. The result includes six connected <Wick contractions> and a vacuum contour.

= Planck normalization of a cubic curvature interaction
{c}
{parent=Curvature bispectrum from a zeta zeta-prime-squared interaction}
{title2=$H_{\mathrm{int}}\propto M_{\mathrm{Pl}}^2a^3\epsilon^2\zeta\dot\zeta^2$}

The <comoving curvature perturbation> is dimensionless. With spatial coordinates and time carrying inverse mass dimensions, the cubic interaction $\int dt\,d^3x\,a^3\epsilon^2\zeta\dot\zeta^2$ needs a mass-squared coefficient. Its standard gravitational normalization is $M_{\mathrm{Pl}}^2$. Six <de Sitter curvature mode functions> supply $M_{\mathrm{Pl}}^{-6}$; this vertex then supplies $M_{\mathrm{Pl}}^2$, leaving the physical $M_{\mathrm{Pl}}^{-4}$ curvature <primordial bispectrum>. The <Wick-pairing multiplicity for a zeta zeta-prime-squared vertex> is independently six: three choices of undifferentiated external leg times two differentiated-leg assignments.

= Slow-roll amplitude of the squeezed zeta zeta-prime-squared bispectrum
{parent=Curvature bispectrum from a zeta zeta-prime-squared interaction}
{title2=$f_{\mathrm{NL}}^{\mathrm{effective}}=5\epsilon/24$}

The undifferentiated soft-leg permutation gives $B(q,k,k)\simeq(\epsilon/2)P(q)P(k)$ as $q/k\to0$. It retains a squeezed $q^{-3}$ enhancement, but corresponds to local amplitude $f_{\mathrm{NL}}^{\mathrm{effective}}=5\epsilon/24$. Soft momentum placed only at derivative slots would miss this dominant term.

= Wick-pairing multiplicity for a zeta zeta-prime-squared vertex
{parent=Curvature bispectrum from a zeta zeta-prime-squared interaction}
{c}
{title2=$3\times2=6$}

Three external fields can be joined to the three cubic vertex fields in six connected <Wick contractions>. There are three choices for the external field at the undifferentiated slot and two exchanges of the differentiated slots. Three cyclic terms therefore require an explicit factor of two. Ordered contractions with an earlier vertex use complex-conjugated mode functions.

= Constant primordial bispectrum
{parent=Primordial bispectrum}
{title2=$B\propto(k_1k_2k_3)^{-2}$}

A constant <primordial bispectrum> means a constant <bispectrum shape function>, not a bispectrum independent of wavenumber. With $P(k)=2\pi^2\Delta_\zeta^2/k^3$ and scale-independent $\Delta_\zeta^2$, one normalization is $B=f_{\rm NL}[P(k_1)P(k_2)P(k_3)]^{2/3}=4\pi^4f_{\rm NL}\Delta_\zeta^4/(k_1k_2k_3)^2$.

= Flattened bispectrum configuration
{parent=Primordial bispectrum}
{title2=$k_3=k_1+k_2$}

= Folded bispectrum configuration
{synonym}

A flattened <primordial bispectrum> configuration lies at the collinear boundary of the allowed momentum-triangle domain. For a <cubic time-derivative curvature bispectrum>, it is finite rather than singular. Its equal-short-side value $k_1=k_2=k_3/2$ gives $k_1k_2k_3/(k_1+k_2+k_3)^3=1/32$, compared with $1/27$ at an <equilateral bispectrum configuration>.

= Equilateral bispectrum configuration
{parent=Primordial bispectrum}
{title2=$k_1=k_2=k_3$}

An equilateral <primordial bispectrum> configuration has equal external wavenumbers. For a <cubic time-derivative curvature bispectrum>, $S\propto k_1k_2k_3/(k_1+k_2+k_3)^3$ is maximized here at fixed perimeter by the <arithmetic-geometric mean inequality>.

= Primordial bispectrum shape function
{parent=Primordial bispectrum}
{title2=$S(k_1,k_2,k_3)\propto(k_1k_2k_3)^2B(k_1,k_2,k_3)$}

= Bispectrum shape function
{synonym}

For a scale-invariant <primordial bispectrum>, the displayed shape function is invariant under common rescaling of all three wavenumbers. It isolates triangle dependence from the overall <power spectrum> amplitude. It indicates intrinsic shape preference but alone does not determine observational signal-to-noise, which also depends on transfer functions, <covariance> and survey weights.

= Cubic time-derivative curvature bispectrum
{parent=Primordial bispectrum}
{title2=$H_{\rm int}\propto\dot\zeta^3$}

Put $C=\epsilon+1-c_s^2$. For constant $H,\epsilon,c_s>0$ and cubic interaction $H_{\rm int}=-M_{\rm Pl}^2\int d^3x\,a^3\epsilon C\dot\zeta^3/(Hc_s^2)$, the <Bunch-Davies vacuum> modes $u_k=H(1+ikc_s\tau)e^{-ikc_s\tau}/\sqrt{4\epsilon M_{\rm Pl}^2c_sk^3}$ give
$$
B(k_1,k_2,k_3)=\frac{3H^4C}{8\epsilon^2M_{\rm Pl}^4c_s^2k_1k_2k_3(k_1+k_2+k_3)^3}.
$$
To derive this <primordial bispectrum>, transform $dt=a\,d\tau$, $\dot\zeta=\zeta'/a$, use the six connected <Wick contractions>, and integrate $\int_{-\infty(1-i0)}^0\tau^2e^{ic_s(k_1+k_2+k_3)\tau}\,d\tau=2i/[c_s(k_1+k_2+k_3)]^3$ in the <in-in formalism>. The normalized equilateral amplitude is $B(k,k,k)/P(k)^2=2C/9$; raw inverse powers of $c_s$ must not be confused with normalized <primordial non-Gaussianity>.

= Unit-speed cubic time-derivative bispectrum
{parent=Cubic time-derivative curvature bispectrum}
{title2=$B=3H^4\mathcal F/(8\epsilon^2k_1k_2k_3K^3)$}

For $H_{\rm int}=-\int d^3x\,a^3\epsilon\mathcal F\dot\zeta^3/H$, $a=-1/(H\tau)$ and stipulated modes $u_k=H(1+ik\tau)e^{-ik\tau}/\sqrt{4\epsilon k^3}$, six connected <Wick contractions> and $\int_{-\infty(1-i0)}^0\tau^2e^{iK\tau}d\tau=2i/K^3$ yield $B=3H^4\mathcal F/(8\epsilon^2k_1k_2k_3K^3)$. Here $\langle\zeta^3\rangle_c=(2\pi)^3\delta(\sum\mathbf k_i)B$. Using unstarred vertex modes with the external-before-vertex prefactor incorrectly reverses the sign. Non-unit sound speed also changes the quadratic modes, so this result is conditional on the specified unit-speed modes.

= Squeezed bispectrum configuration
{parent=Primordial bispectrum}
{title2=$k_L\ll k_S$}

A <primordial bispectrum> is squeezed when one external <Fourier mode> has much smaller <wavenumber> than the other two. Momentum conservation then makes the two short wavevectors almost opposite. This configuration measures the response of short-scale correlations to a long perturbation.

= Bispectrum from a time-derivative cubic scalar interaction
{parent=Primordial bispectrum}

For a massless scalar in <de Sitter spacetime> with interaction $S_{\rm int}=(\lambda/3!)\int dt\,d^3x\,a^3\dot\phi^2\phi$, the tree-level late-time <primordial bispectrum> is
$$
B=\frac{\lambda H^4}{12k_1^3k_2^3k_3^3K^2}
\sum_{\rm cyclic}k_j^2k_l^2(K+k_i),
\qquad K=k_1+k_2+k_3.
$$
It scales as $B(\mu k_1,\mu k_2,\mu k_3)=\mu^{-6}B(k_1,k_2,k_3)$.

= Shift-symmetry soft theorem for a scalar bispectrum
{parent=Primordial bispectrum}

If a scalar vacuum is invariant under an exact <scalar-field shift symmetry>, the Ward identity makes the leading soft bispectrum vanish:
$$
\lim_{q\to0}\frac{B_\phi(q,k,|\mathbf k+\mathbf q|)}{P_\phi(q)}=0.
$$

= Single-field inflation squeezed-limit consistency relation
{parent=Primordial bispectrum}

For a single-clock adiabatic inflationary background, a long <comoving curvature perturbation> acts on short modes as a spatial dilation. The squeezed bispectrum therefore obeys
$$
\lim_{q\to0}B_{\mathcal R}(q,k,|\mathbf k+\mathbf q|)
=-(n_s-1)P_{\mathcal R}(q)P_{\mathcal R}(k)
$$
at leading order in $q/k$.

= Absence of primordial scale-dependent halo bias in single-clock inflation
{parent=Single-field inflation squeezed-limit consistency relation}

In an attractor with one adiabatic clock, the leading squeezed <primordial bispectrum> is the response to a coordinate dilation. Holding a halo's physical mass and smoothing scale fixed cancels this dilation in its abundance response. Thus the consistency-relation term does not generate the physical $k^{-2}$ <scale-dependent halo bias from local non-Gaussianity>. For example, a <top-hat filter> of fixed physical radius has coordinate radius $R_c=e^{-\zeta_L}R_0$, while the short <density contrast> is $\delta_s(\mathbf x)=\delta_s^{(0)}(e^{\zeta_L}\mathbf x)$. The substitution $\mathbf y=e^{\zeta_L}\mathbf x$ shows that its normalized average over $|\mathbf x|<R_c$ equals the original average over $|\mathbf y|<R_0$. Genuine local effects start at two spatial derivatives of the long mode. Non-attractor backgrounds or nonstandard initial states need not satisfy this argument.

= Primordial trispectrum
{title2=$T(\mathbf k_1,\mathbf k_2,\mathbf k_3,\mathbf k_4)$}
{parent=Primordial non-Gaussianity}
{wiki=Trispectrum}

The primordial trispectrum is the connected four-point function with its momentum-conserving delta function removed. Exchange diagrams split naturally into $s$, $t$, and $u$ channels according to which pair of external momenta meets at one cubic vertex.

= Parity-odd primordial trispectrum
{parent=Primordial trispectrum}

A parity-odd primordial trispectrum changes sign when all external momenta are reversed. Rotational invariance allows such a four-point structure because four momentum-conserving vectors can form a nonzero scalar triple product.

= Reality of a momentum-space scalar correlator
{parent=Primordial trispectrum}

For a real scalar field, $\phi(\mathbf k)^*=\phi(-\mathbf k)$. Its momentum-space $n$-point function therefore obeys
$$
\langle\phi(\mathbf k_1)\cdots\phi(\mathbf k_n)\rangle^*
=\langle\phi(-\mathbf k_1)\cdots\phi(-\mathbf k_n)\rangle.
$$
A parity-odd scalar correlator is consequently purely imaginary after removing its momentum-conserving delta function.

= Large-scale structure of the universe
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki}

The large-scale structure of the universe is the cosmic distribution of matter on scales where galaxies and halos form a web of clusters, filaments, sheets, and voids.

= Cosmological structure formation
{parent=Large-scale structure of the universe}
{wiki}

Cosmological structure formation is the growth of primordial density perturbations into sheets, filaments, halos, galaxies, and clusters under gravity.

= Hierarchical galaxy formation
{parent=Cosmological structure formation}
{title2=$\sigma(M)\text{ increases as }M\text{ decreases}$}

In <hierarchical galaxy formation>, low-mass dark-matter structures typically collapse before larger structures because the smoothed linear density variance is larger on smaller mass scales. Accretion and mergers assemble increasingly massive haloes. Cooling, feedback and angular momentum determine how baryons form galaxies within that halo hierarchy; the ordering is statistical rather than an absolute rule for every object.

= Halo baryon-to-star conversion efficiency
{parent=Hierarchical galaxy formation}
{title2=$\epsilon_*=M_*/(f_bM_h)$}

= Halo star-formation efficiency
{synonym}

The integrated <halo star-formation efficiency> is the fraction of a <dark-matter halo>'s reference baryonic mass converted into stars: $\epsilon_*=M_*/(f_bM_h)$, with <cosmic baryon fraction> $f_b$. It is distinct from <star-formation efficiency per free-fall time>. Cooling, gas supply and retention, <stellar feedback> and <active-galactic-nucleus feedback> make it strongly dependent on <dark-matter halo> mass. With stellar <mass-to-light ratio> $\Upsilon_*$, a simple central-galaxy mapping is $L=\epsilon_*f_bM_h/\Upsilon_*$. Changing this efficiency changes both the mapping and the logarithmic Jacobian between the <dark-matter halo> mass function and the <luminosity function>.

= Low-angular-momentum baryonic disk estimate
{parent=Hierarchical galaxy formation}
{title2=$r_d\simeq j_d^2/(GM_b)$}

Selecting a baryonic mass $M_b$ from a normalized cumulative distribution $M_b(<j)\propto j$ fixes a cutoff specific angular momentum $j_d=(M_b/M_{b,\rm tot})j_{\rm vir}$. With circular support and dominant enclosed baryonic gravity, the outer radius is $r_d\simeq j_d^2/(GM_b)$ and speed $v_d\simeq GM_b/j_d$. The mean specific angular momentum is $j_d/2$, which defines a different one-zone radius. The estimate assumes energy dissipation but negligible redistribution of <specific angular momentum>.

= Baryon conversion efficiency of a halo
{parent=Hierarchical galaxy formation}
{title2=$f_*=M_*/(f_bM_h)$}

The <baryon conversion efficiency of a halo> is the fraction of its baryonic mass incorporated into stars. If the halo retains the <cosmic baryon fraction> $f_b$, its stellar-to-total-mass ratio is $f_bf_*$. This efficiency depends on halo mass, epoch, gas supply and feedback and must not be confused with a star-formation efficiency per free-fall time.

= Halo-to-galaxy luminosity mapping
{parent=Baryon conversion efficiency of a halo}
{title2=$\Phi(L)=n_h(M_h)\left|dM_h/dL\right|$}

A monotonic luminosity assignment to a counted halo population converts its differential abundance per mass into a <luminosity function> by a change of variable. If $n_h\propto M_h^{-a}$ and $L\propto M_h^p$, then $\Phi\propto L^{-1-(a-1)/p}$. This simple mapping assumes one counted galaxy per halo, no scatter and a fixed selection. A mass-dependent <baryon conversion efficiency of a halo>, satellites and stellar-population differences modify it.

= Stellar-to-halo mass ratio
{parent=Baryon conversion efficiency of a halo}
{title2=$\epsilon_*=M_*/M_h=f_bf_*$}

The <stellar-to-halo mass ratio> compares a galaxy's <galactic stellar mass> with its host's total halo mass. In a simple baryon-conversion model it is $\epsilon_*=f_bf_*$. Observational comparisons must distinguish this total-mass ratio from the fraction of baryons converted, and must specify whether central, satellite or all stellar populations are included.

= Constant baryon conversion mapping of halo and stellar mass
{parent=Stellar-to-halo mass ratio}
{title2=$M_*=\epsilon_*M_h$}

If every counted halo hosts one counted galaxy and the baryon fraction and conversion efficiency are mass-independent without scatter, <galactic stellar mass> is a constant multiple of halo mass. The galaxy mass function is obtained by changing variable with $dM/dM_*=1/\epsilon_*$. Satellites, efficiency variation and scatter prevent this mapping from being generally exact.

= Cooling criterion for galaxy formation
{parent=Hierarchical galaxy formation}
{title2=$t_{\rm cool}\lesssim t_{\rm dyn}$}

Gas in a collapsing halo is heated toward a <virial temperature>. If its cooling time is short compared with the dynamical time, thermal support is removed and rapid condensation can form a galaxy. If cooling is slower, a hot atmosphere can persist or condense only gradually. This criterion links baryonic galaxy formation to the dark-matter assembly hierarchy without making every halo into one luminous object.

= Atomic and molecular cooling thresholds for galaxy formation
{parent=Cooling criterion for galaxy formation}
{title2=$T_{\rm atomic}\sim10^4\,\mathrm K,\quad T_{\rm molecular}\sim10^2\text{--}10^3\,\mathrm K$}

Primordial <atomic line cooling> becomes effective near the temperature where electronic excitations are accessible, of order ten thousand kelvin. <Molecular hydrogen> has lower-energy transitions and can cool colder gas if it forms and survives. The corresponding <virial temperature> thresholds translate into epoch-dependent halo mass thresholds. Radiation, molecular chemistry, <metal-line cooling> and gas density qualify a single universal minimum galaxy mass.

= Upper galaxy mass from gas cooling
{parent=Cooling criterion for galaxy formation}
{title2=$M_{\rm cool}\text{ of order a galactic mass}$}

In simple hierarchical models, increasing system mass raises the <virial temperature> and can carry primordial gas beyond its efficient atomic line-cooling interval. At assembly densities, the cooling-time constraint then distinguishes galaxy-sized condensations from larger hot groups or clusters. The resulting characteristic mass is approximate, depends on composition and structure, and does not prohibit subsequent mergers of already formed stellar systems.

= Zeldovich approximation
{c}
{parent=Cosmological structure formation}
{wiki}

The Zeldovich approximation maps a fluid element from its <Lagrangian coordinate> $\mathbf q$ to the comoving position $\mathbf x=\mathbf q+D(t)\mathbf s(\mathbf q)$, where $D$ is the <linear growth factor>. It follows the initial gravitational displacement along a straight comoving trajectory until trajectories cross.

= Cosmological Lagrangian displacement
{title2=$\boldsymbol\psi(\mathbf q,t)$}
{parent=Zeldovich approximation}

The cosmological Lagrangian displacement sends an initially labelled matter element from its <Lagrangian coordinate> $\mathbf q$ to $\mathbf x(\mathbf q,t)=\mathbf q+\boldsymbol\psi(\mathbf q,t)$. Its <derivative>[time derivative] determines the <peculiar velocity> through $\mathbf v=a\dot{\boldsymbol\psi}$.

= Deformation tensor in the Zel'dovich approximation
{title2=$d_{ij}$}
{parent=Zeldovich approximation}

For an irrotational displacement $\mathbf s=-\nabla_q\Phi$, the deformation tensor is the symmetric Hessian $d_{ij}=-\partial_i\partial_j\Phi$. Its real eigenvalues determine the principal collapse directions.

= Shell crossing
{parent=Zeldovich approximation}
{wiki}

Shell crossing occurs when the Lagrangian-to-Eulerian map ceases to be one-to-one. Its Jacobian determinant vanishes, the pressureless single-stream density formally diverges, and several streams subsequently occupy the same position.

= Cosmological caustic
{parent=Shell crossing}

A cosmological caustic is the image of a singular point of the Lagrangian map. The ideal collisionless density diverges there, although velocity dispersion and other small-scale physics regularize the singularity.

= Tidal torque theory
{parent=Cosmological structure formation}
{wiki}

Tidal torque theory explains the early <angular momentum> of a protohalo through the misalignment of its <mass second-moment tensor> $I_{ij}$ and the <Hessian matrix> $T_{ij}=\partial_i\partial_j\Phi$ of the surrounding <peculiar gravitational potential>. At leading order,
$$
J_i\propto a^2\dot D\,\epsilon_{ijk}T_{jl}I_{lk}.
$$

= Cosmic web
{parent=Cosmological structure formation}
{wiki}

The cosmic web is the network of matter sheets, filaments, nodes, and intervening voids produced by anisotropic gravitational collapse.

= Collisionless dark-matter Vlasov equation
{c}
{parent=Cosmological structure formation}
{wiki=Vlasov_equation}

For comoving position $\mathbf x$, canonical momentum $\mathbf p=am\mathbf x'$, and Newtonian potential $\phi$, the dark-matter phase-space density obeys
$$
\partial_\eta f+\frac{\mathbf p}{am}\mathbin\cdot\nabla_xf-am\nabla\phi\mathbin\cdot\nabla_pf=0.
$$

= Velocity-dispersion tensor of collisionless matter
{title2=$\sigma^{ij}$}
{parent=Collisionless dark-matter Vlasov equation}

The velocity-dispersion tensor is the second central moment of a phase-space distribution,
$$
\sigma^{ij}=\langle u^iu^j\rangle-v^iv^j.
$$
It contributes the stress-divergence term $\rho^{-1}\partial_j(\rho\sigma^{ij})$ to the Euler equation and vanishes in the ideal single-stream limit.

= Einstein-de Sitter universe
{c}
{parent=Large-scale structure of the universe}
{wiki=Einstein–de_Sitter_universe}

An Einstein-de Sitter universe is spatially flat and contains only pressureless matter. In conformal time, $a\propto\tau^2$ and $\mathcal H=a'/a=2/\tau$.

= Spherical secondary infall
{parent=Einstein-de Sitter universe}

Spherical secondary infall follows concentric collisionless shells around a primordial overdensity. Each shell initially expands with the Hubble flow, turns around, collapses, and subsequently crosses other shells.

= Turnaround radius
{title2=$r_*$}
{parent=Spherical secondary infall}
{wiki}

The turnaround radius of a shell is its maximum physical radius before collapse. At a given cosmic time, the currently turning shell defines a natural similarity length.

= Self-similar secondary infall
{parent=Spherical secondary infall}

If the initial fractional excess mass is a power law in enclosed mass, spherical secondary infall admits a similarity reduction in which radii scale by the current turnaround radius and masses by the mass of the currently turning shell.

= Standard perturbation theory in cosmology
{title2=SPT}
{parent=Large-scale structure of the universe}

Standard perturbation theory expands the density contrast and velocity divergence of a pressureless irrotational fluid in powers of the linear density field.

= Nonlinear wavenumber
{parent=Standard perturbation theory in cosmology}
{title2=$k_{\rm NL}$}

The nonlinear <wavenumber> is the inverse scale at which linear <density contrast> fluctuations become of order unity. For the one-dimensional convention $\langle\delta(k)\delta(k')\rangle=2\pi\delta^{(D)}(k+k')P_L(k)$, a usual estimate is $k_{\rm NL}P_L(k_{\rm NL})/\pi\sim1$. Loop momenta above this scale require the <effective field theory of large-scale structure> even when external modes are perturbative.

= Gaussian initial conditions
{c}
{parent=Standard perturbation theory in cosmology}

Gaussian initial conditions make every connected primordial correlation function above second order vanish. <Wick theorem> then reduces perturbative late-time correlation functions to sums of products of the linear power spectrum.

= Alpha mode-coupling kernel
{title2=$\alpha(\mathbf k_1,\mathbf k_2)$}
{parent=Standard perturbation theory in cosmology}

The continuity-equation kernel is
$$
\alpha(\mathbf k_1,\mathbf k_2)
=\frac{(\mathbf k_1+\mathbf k_2)\mathbin\cdot\mathbf k_1}{k_1^2}.
$$
Its symmetrization is $\alpha_s=1+(\mathbf k_1\mathbin\cdot\mathbf k_2)(k_1^{-2}+k_2^{-2})/2$.

= Beta mode-coupling kernel
{title2=$\beta(\mathbf k_1,\mathbf k_2)$}
{parent=Standard perturbation theory in cosmology}

The Euler-equation kernel is the symmetric function
$$
\beta(\mathbf k_1,\mathbf k_2)
=\frac{|\mathbf k_1+\mathbf k_2|^2
(\mathbf k_1\mathbin\cdot\mathbf k_2)}{2k_1^2k_2^2}.
$$

= Standard perturbation theory density kernel
{title2=$F_n$}
{parent=Standard perturbation theory in cosmology}

The symmetrized kernel $F_n$ convolves $n$ linear density fields to produce the $n$th-order density perturbation. In an Einstein-de Sitter universe,
$$
F_2=\frac57\alpha_s+\frac27\beta.
$$

= One-dimensional cosmological density kernel
{parent=Standard perturbation theory density kernel}
{title2=$F_{n,s}(q_1,\ldots,q_n)$}

For plane-parallel pressureless matter in an <Einstein-de Sitter universe>, before <shell crossing>, the symmetrized growing-mode <standard perturbation theory density kernel> is
$$
F_{n,s}(q_1,\ldots,q_n)=\frac{(q_1+\cdots+q_n)^n}{n!q_1\cdots q_n}.
$$
In particular $F_{2,s}=(q_1+q_2)^2/(2q_1q_2)$. An unsymmetrized representation of the one-dimensional <beta mode-coupling kernel> is $(q_1+q_2)/q_1$; its symmetric part is the expression above.

= Ultraviolet softness of the second-order density kernel
{parent=Standard perturbation theory density kernel}

At fixed external momentum $\mathbf k$, mass and momentum conservation imply
$$
F_2(\mathbf q,\mathbf k-\mathbf q)=O(k^2/q^2)
$$
as $q\to\infty$. In particular, the constant term in the hard-momentum expansion must cancel.

= Unsmoothed matter-density reduced skewness
{title2=$S_3=34/7$}
{parent=Standard perturbation theory density kernel}

For <Gaussian initial conditions> and the Einstein-de Sitter second-order <standard perturbation theory density kernel>, the unsmoothed <density contrast> obeys
$$
\langle\delta^3\rangle=\frac{34}{7}\sigma^4+O(\sigma^6),
\qquad \sigma^2=\langle\delta^2\rangle.
$$
There are three choices of the second-order field and two connected <Wick contractions>; the angular average of $F_2$ is $5/7+(2/7)/3=17/21$. Thus the reduced <skewness> is $6(17/21)=34/7$. Smoothing changes this result, and the unsmoothed calculation requires a finite <variance>.

= Tree-level connected matter five-point function
{parent=Standard perturbation theory density kernel}

For Gaussian initial conditions, the tree-level connected matter five-point function has perturbative-order partitions
$$
4+1+1+1+1,
\qquad 3+2+1+1+1,
\qquad 2+2+2+1+1.
$$
They correspond respectively to a four-valent star, an $F_3$--$F_2$ tree, and a chain of three $F_2$ vertices. Every topology contains four linear power spectra.

= One-loop matter power spectrum
{parent=Standard perturbation theory in cosmology}

For Gaussian linear perturbations, the one-loop matter power spectrum is
$$
P_{\rm 1-loop}=a^2P+a^4(P_{22}+2P_{13}),
$$
where $P_{22}$ correlates two second-order fields and $P_{13}$ correlates a linear field with a third-order field.

= P22 contribution to the one-loop matter power spectrum
{title2=$P_{22}(k)$}
{c}
{parent=One-loop matter power spectrum}

The $P_{22}$ contribution correlates two second-order density fields. For symmetrized kernels,
$$
P_{22}(k)=2\int\frac{d^3q}{(2\pi)^3}F_2(\mathbf q,\mathbf k-\mathbf q)^2P(q)P(|\mathbf k-\mathbf q|).
$$

= P13 contribution to the one-loop matter power spectrum
{title2=$P_{13}(k)$}
{c}
{parent=One-loop matter power spectrum}

The $P_{13}$ contribution correlates a linear density field with a third-order field:
$$
P_{13}(k)=3P(k)\int\frac{d^3q}{(2\pi)^3}F_3(\mathbf k,\mathbf q,-\mathbf q)P(q).
$$
Both orderings contribute $2P_{13}$ to the power spectrum.

= Infrared cancellation in large-scale structure
{parent=One-loop matter power spectrum}

At equal time, leading loop contributions from a very long-wavelength displacement cancel between perturbative diagrams. This is required by the <equivalence principle>, because a coherent long displacement merely translates short-scale structure.

= Scale-free matter power spectrum
{parent=One-loop matter power spectrum}

A scale-free linear matter spectrum has $P(k)=Ak^n$. Dimensional analysis then makes its one-loop $P_{22}$ contribution proportional to $A^2k^{2n+3}$ whenever the loop integral converges.

= Scale-free smoothed density variance
{title2=$\sigma_R\propto R^{-(n+3)/2}$}
{parent=Scale-free matter power spectrum}

For an unsmoothed density spectrum $P(k)=Ak^n$ and a scale-dependent smoothing window $\widetilde W(kR)$, substituting $q=kR$ in the variance integral gives $\sigma_R^2\propto R^{-(n+3)}$, provided $\int_0^\infty q^{n+2}|\widetilde W(q)|^2dq$ converges. For a window tending to one at zero, infrared convergence requires $n>-3$; the ultraviolet condition depends on the window. A real-space spherical top-hat also requires $n<1$.

= Standard perturbation theory velocity kernel
{title2=$G_n$}
{parent=Standard perturbation theory in cosmology}

The symmetrized kernel $G_n$ convolves $n$ linear density fields to produce the $n$th-order peculiar-velocity divergence in cosmological standard perturbation theory.

= One-loop matter bispectrum
{parent=Standard perturbation theory in cosmology}

For Gaussian initial conditions, the matter bispectrum through one loop is the sum of the tree diagram $B_{211}$ and the four one-loop topologies $B_{222}$, $B_{321}^{I}$, $B_{321}^{II}$, and $B_{411}$.

= B411 contribution to the one-loop matter bispectrum
{c}
{parent=One-loop matter bispectrum}
{title2=$B_{411}$}

The connected correlation of one fourth-order and two first-order <density contrasts> contracts a pair of inputs at the fourth-order vertex into a loop. For each choice of the fourth-order external field there are twelve <Wick contractions>. In one dimension,
$$
B_{411}=12P_L(k_1)P_L(k_2)\int\frac{dq}{2\pi}F_4(-k_1,-k_2,q,-q)P_L(q)+\text{two cyclic terms}.
$$
For the <one-dimensional cosmological density kernel>, $F_4=-k_3^4/(24k_1k_2q^2)$ exactly. Its leading cutoff dependence is therefore proportional to $k_3^2F_2(k_1,k_2)P_L(k_1)P_L(k_2)\int dq\,P_L(q)/q^2$.

= B222 contribution to the one-loop matter bispectrum
{c}
{parent=One-loop matter bispectrum}
{title2=$B_{222}$}

The connected correlation of three second-order <density contrasts> forms a triangle of three linear <power spectra>. It has eight <Wick contractions>. In one dimension, with $k_1+k_2+k_3=0$ and $P_L$ the power at the evaluation time,
$$
B_{222}=8\int\frac{dq}{2\pi}F_2(q,k_1-q)F_2(-q,k_2+q)F_2(q-k_1,-k_2-q)P_L(q)P_L(k_1-q)P_L(k_2+q).
$$
For the <one-dimensional cosmological density kernel>, its hard-loop integrand starts as $-k_1^2k_2^2k_3^2P_L(q)^3/q^6$.

= One-loop matter trispectrum
{title2=$T_{\rm 1-loop}$}
{parent=Standard perturbation theory in cosmology}

For Gaussian initial conditions, the one-loop connected matter trispectrum contains the perturbative-order classes $T_{5111}$, $T_{4211}$, $T_{3311}$, $T_{3221}$, and $T_{2222}$. Each class includes every inequivalent external-leg assignment and Wick contraction.

= Effective field theory of large-scale structure
{title2=EFTofLSS}
{parent=Large-scale structure of the universe}
{wiki}

The effective field theory of large-scale structure represents unresolved short-scale dynamics by an effective stress tensor. Its leading deterministic correction is proportional to $k^2\delta^{(1)}$ and renormalizes ultraviolet-sensitive loop contributions.

= Second-order density Laplacian counterterm
{parent=Effective field theory of large-scale structure}
{title2=$C(\Lambda)\partial_x^2\delta^{(2)}$}

For the one-dimensional <B411 contribution to the one-loop matter bispectrum>, contracting two hard inputs in the fourth-order field gives $-(I_\Lambda/2)k^2\delta^{(2)}$, where $I_\Lambda=\int_{q_h<|q|<\Lambda}dq\,P_L(q)/(2\pi q^2)$. A <counterterm> $C(\Lambda)\partial_x^2\delta^{(2)}$ cancels this cutoff-dependent part when $C(\Lambda)=-I_\Lambda/2+C_{\rm finite}$. This sign follows from $\partial_x^2\leftrightarrow-k^2$; the finite coefficient describes unresolved short-scale dynamics.

= Effective sound-speed counterterm in large-scale structure
{title2=$-2c_{\rm eff}^2k^2P(k)$}
{parent=Effective field theory of large-scale structure}

The leading deterministic power-spectrum counterterm in the effective field theory of large-scale structure is proportional to $-2c_{\rm eff}^2k^2P(k)$. Its coefficient depends on the coarse-graining convention and runs so that it cancels the cutoff dependence of the one-loop $P_{13}$ contribution.

= Ultraviolet sensitivity of the one-loop matter power spectrum
{parent=Effective field theory of large-scale structure}

For internal momentum $q\gg k$, the <P22 contribution to the one-loop matter power spectrum> starts at $k^4$, while twice the <P13 contribution to the one-loop matter power spectrum> starts at $k^2P(k)$. The latter has the same momentum dependence as the <effective sound-speed counterterm in large-scale structure>. Short-scale stochastic contributions start at $k^4$ by mass and <momentum conservation>.

= Bias expansion
{parent=Effective field theory of large-scale structure}

A bias expansion expresses a tracer density, such as a galaxy or halo density, as a renormalized sum of local and nonlocal operators built from matter density, tidal fields, derivatives, and stochastic fields. Its coefficients encode unresolved tracer formation physics.

= Galaxy bias
{title2=$b$}
{parent=Bias expansion}
{wiki}

Galaxy bias describes how fluctuations in galaxy number density differ from fluctuations in the underlying matter density. On sufficiently large scales, the leading deterministic relation is $\delta_g=b_1\delta_m$.

= Lagrangian-to-Eulerian linear bias relation
{parent=Bias expansion}

Number conservation under the displacement from Lagrangian coordinates to Eulerian coordinates gives
$$
\boxed{b_1^{(E)}=1+b_1^{(L)}}.
$$
The added one is the linear matter-density response of the coordinate Jacobian.

= Local quadratic galaxy bias
{parent=Bias expansion}

The simplest nonlinear local bias model is
$$
\delta_g=b_1\delta+\frac{b_2}{2}(\delta^2-\langle\delta^2\rangle).
$$
For Gaussian matter fluctuations, its leading galaxy three-point function is proportional to $b_1^2b_2$.

= Galaxy bispectrum
{title2=$B_g(k_1,k_2,k_3)$}
{parent=Local quadratic galaxy bias}

The galaxy bispectrum is the connected momentum-space three-point function of the galaxy density contrast after removing its momentum-conserving delta function.

= Dark-matter halo
{parent=Large-scale structure of the universe}
{wiki=Dark_matter_halo}

= Dark-matter haloes
{synonym}

A dark-matter halo is a gravitationally bound concentration of dark matter that hosts galaxies and larger structures.

= Halo abundance mass-budget bound
{parent=Dark-matter halo}
{title2=$\int M\,n_h(M)\,dM\leq\bar\rho_{m,0}$}

A population of distinct, nonoverlapping <dark-matter halos> cannot contain more total mass per <comoving volume> than the cosmic mean matter density. A narrow mass population therefore obeys $n_cM_h\leq\bar\rho_{m,0}$. Do not sum subhalo masses and their inclusive host masses in the same bound, because that double-counts material. This check can reveal incompatible inferred absorption incidence, object size and halo-mass assumptions.

= Virial radius of a dark-matter halo
{title2=$r_v$}
{parent=Dark-matter halo}

The virial radius of a dark-matter halo is conventionally the radius within which the mean density is a chosen overdensity $v$ times a reference critical or matter density.

= Halo density estimate of formation redshift
{parent=Virial radius of a dark-matter halo}
{title2=$1+z_f\simeq[\rho_h/(\Delta_{\rm vir}\bar\rho_{m,0})]^{1/3}$}

If a halo retains the mean density acquired at collapse, $3M/(4\pi R^3)=\Delta_{\rm vir}\bar\rho_{m,0}(1+z_f)^3$ estimates its formation redshift. In the non-dissipative <spherical-collapse model> during matter domination, $\Delta_{\rm vir}=18\pi^2$. Subsequent mass growth, profile evolution and the reference-density convention limit this inference.

= Virial velocity of a spherical-overdensity halo
{parent=Virial radius of a dark-matter halo}
{title2=$v_{\rm vir}=H(z)R_h\sqrt{\Delta_v/2}$}

= Virial velocity
{synonym}

For halo mean density $\Delta_v$ times the <critical density> at its epoch, the circular virial velocity obeys $v_{\rm vir}^2=GM_h/R_h=(\Delta_v/2)H^2R_h^2$. Thus $v_{\rm vir}=HR_h\sqrt{\Delta_v/2}$. Specifying whether overdensity is relative to mean matter or <critical density> is necessary for numerical inference.

= Halo virial-velocity conversion between epochs
{parent=Virial velocity of a spherical-overdensity halo}
{title2=$v_2/v_1=[\Delta_{c,2}H_2^2/(\Delta_{c,1}H_1^2)]^{1/6}$}

For a fixed mass under a spherical-overdensity definition, $R_h\propto(\Delta_cH^2)^{-1/3}$ and $v_h^2=GM_h/R_h$. Thus equivalent virial mass scales at two epochs have the displayed velocity ratio. With fixed overdensity during matter domination, $v_h\propto(1+z)^{1/2}$. This converts an epoch-specific mass–velocity calibration; it does not require a previously collapsed bound halo to physically expand with the later background density.

= Virial mass of a dark-matter halo
{title2=$M_v$}
{parent=Virial radius of a dark-matter halo}

If the virial radius is defined relative to critical density $\rho_c$, the virial mass is
$$
M_v=\frac{4\pi}{3}v\rho_cr_v^3.
$$

= Concentration of a dark-matter halo
{title2=$c$}
{parent=Dark-matter halo}

= Halo concentration
{synonym}

The concentration of a spherical dark-matter halo compares its outer radius with an inner scale radius. For a <Navarro--Frenk--White profile>, $c=r_v/r_s$.

= Mass-concentration relation of dark-matter haloes
{parent=Concentration of a dark-matter halo}

The mass-concentration relation describes the statistical dependence of halo concentration on virial mass and epoch. In hierarchical cold-dark-matter cosmologies, lower-mass haloes generally formed earlier against a denser background and are therefore more concentrated at fixed epoch.

= Navarro--Frenk--White profile
{title2=NFW profile}
{c}
{parent=Dark-matter halo}
{wiki}

The Navarro--Frenk--White profile is
$$
\rho(r)=\frac{\rho_s}{(r/r_s)(1+r/r_s)^2}.
$$
It has an inner $r^{-1}$ density cusp and an outer $r^{-3}$ tail.

= Scale radius of a Navarro--Frenk--White profile
{title2=$r_s$}
{parent=Navarro--Frenk--White profile}

The NFW scale radius is where the logarithmic density slope equals $-2$.

= Characteristic density of a Navarro--Frenk--White profile
{title2=$\rho_s$}
{parent=Navarro--Frenk--White profile}

The NFW characteristic density fixes the normalization of the halo profile at a given scale radius.

= Cusp--core problem
{parent=Navarro--Frenk--White profile}
{wiki}

The cusp--core problem is the tension between the central cusps of collisionless cold-dark-matter halo profiles and the approximately constant-density cores inferred for many dwarf and low-surface-brightness galaxies.

= Dark-matter halo merger
{parent=Dark-matter halo}

A dark-matter halo merger joins previously distinct <dark-matter halos>. Orbital <angular momentum> can become internal halo spin, while escaping material carries away some mass and <angular momentum>. Halo mergers and accretion are important reasons that early <tidal torque theory> is only an approximate predictor of the spin of a later virialized remnant.

= Top-hat filter
{parent=Dark-matter halo}
{wiki}

A top-hat filter averages a field with uniform weight inside a ball and zero weight outside it.

= Spherical-collapse model
{parent=Dark-matter halo}

= Spherical collapse
{synonym}

The spherical-collapse model applies a spherical <top-hat filter> to an overdense region and predicts collapse when its linearly extrapolated smoothed overdensity exceeds the threshold $\delta_c\simeq1.686$ in an Einstein-de Sitter universe.

= Dimensionless spherical-collapse time normalization
{parent=Spherical-collapse model}
{title2=$\tau\sim(8/9\pi)y^{3/2}$}

For <spherical collapse> in an <Einstein-de Sitter universe>, take $y=R/R_{\rm ta}$ and $\tau=H_{\rm ta}t$, with $\Delta_{\rm ta}=(3\pi/4)^2$. The expanding branch has $\tau=\Delta_{\rm ta}^{-1/2}\int_0^y[u/(1-u)]^{1/2}\,du$. Its small-radius expansion is $\tau=(8/9\pi)y^{3/2}(1+3y/10+O(y^2))$, and its turnaround time is $2/3$. These constants are linked: using another leading coefficient without changing the time normalization no longer gives a <density contrast> approaching zero at early times. The collapsing branch is $4/3-\tau_{\rm expand}(y)$.

= Virial radius from turnaround energy
{parent=Spherical-collapse model}
{title2=$R_{\rm vir}=R_{\rm ta}/2$}

For a homologous, isolated collapsing sphere, write its <Newtonian gravitational potential energy> as $U=-aGM^2/R$. At <spherical-collapse turnaround> the coherent <kinetic energy> vanishes. Conserving total energy and applying the <virial theorem> to the final equilibrium gives $U_{\rm ta}=U_{\rm vir}/2$ and hence $R_{\rm vir}=R_{\rm ta}/2$. If the gravitational structure coefficient changes, the ratio is $a_{\rm vir}/(2a_{\rm ta})$ instead. Mass loss, surface pressure, external tides and energy exchange also change this estimate.

= Turnaround of spherical collapse
{parent=Spherical-collapse model}
{title2=$\theta_{\rm ta}=\pi$}

= Spherical-collapse turnaround
{synonym}

A pressureless bound spherical shell first expands, reaches $\dot R=0$ at its maximum radius, and recollapses. In the cycloidal <spherical-collapse model>, turnaround occurs at $\theta=\pi$, with $R_{\max}=2A$ and $t_{\max}=\pi B$. Its mean density is $9\pi^2/16$ times the background density in an <Einstein-de Sitter universe>.

= Uniform-sphere collapse with a cosmological constant
{parent=Spherical-collapse model}

For fixed mass $M$, a uniform sphere of physical radius $R$ has $W_G=-3GM^2/(5R)$ and $W_\Lambda=-\Lambda MR^2/10$, using the convention in which its outward acceleration is $\Lambda r/3$. The <virial theorem> reads $2T+W_G=2W_\Lambda$. Defining $x=R_f/R_{\rm ta}$ and $\eta=\Lambda/(4\pi G\rho_{\rm ta})$, <conservation of energy> gives $2\eta x^3-(2+\eta)x+1=0$. The collapsing branch has $x=1/2-\eta/8+O(\eta^2)$. For small positive <cosmological constant>, it is more compact at fixed turnaround data, even though the outward force inhibits turnaround and collapse.

= Linear spherical-collapse threshold
{title2=$\delta_c$}
{parent=Spherical-collapse model}

In an Einstein-de Sitter universe, extrapolating the linear growing mode to the time when the spherical top-hat solution collapses gives
$$
\delta_c=\frac3{20}(12\pi)^{2/3}\simeq1.686.
$$

= Present-extrapolated spherical-collapse barrier
{parent=Linear spherical-collapse threshold}
{title2=$\delta_c(z)/\sigma(M,0)=\delta_{\rm sc}/\sigma(M,z)$}

With linear growth normalized to unity today, a collapse threshold $\delta_{\rm sc}$ at epoch $z$ corresponds to a present-extrapolated initial barrier $\delta_c(z)=\delta_{\rm sc}/D(z)$. Comparing this barrier to present variance is equivalent to comparing $\delta_{\rm sc}$ to variance evolved to the collapse epoch. Mixing the two conventions counts growth twice.

= Halo peak height
{title2=$\nu$}
{parent=Spherical-collapse model}

The peak height of a halo of mass $M$ is $\nu=\delta_c/\sigma(M)$, where $\sigma^2(M)$ is the variance of the linear density field smoothed on the corresponding mass scale.

= Press-Schechter formalism
{c}
{parent=Spherical-collapse model}
{wiki=Press–Schechter_formalism}

The Press-Schechter formalism estimates the halo abundance from the Gaussian probability that a smoothed linear overdensity crosses the spherical-collapse threshold, with a factor of two enforcing total mass conservation.

= Excursion-set description of halo formation
{parent=Press-Schechter formalism}

A halo is associated with the first upcrossing of the <linear spherical-collapse threshold> by the smoothed <density contrast> as its variance increases. With a <sharp-k smoothing filter>, the <Brownian reflection principle> makes the crossing probability twice the one-scale Gaussian upper tail. This handles trajectories that crossed earlier but finish below the barrier and leads to the <Press-Schechter halo mass function>.

= Halo first crossing with a finite variance cutoff
{parent=Excursion-set description of halo formation}

A small-scale spectral cutoff bounds the smoothing variance by $S_{\max}$. A sharp-k excursion remains Brownian up to this terminal variance, with total crossed fraction $\operatorname{erfc}(\delta_c/\sqrt{2S_{\max}})<1$. The ordinary first-crossing density remains valid before the cutoff. Below the sharp-filter cutoff radius the variance is constant, so there are no new crossings and the idealized differential halo abundance vanishes. The missing fraction need not be artificially renormalized to one.

= Sharp-k smoothing filter
{title2=$\widetilde W(kR)=\mathbf1_{k<1/R}$}
{parent=Press-Schechter formalism}

= Sharp Fourier-space filter
{synonym}

A sharp-k filter includes every Fourier mode below a cutoff with unit weight and excludes all modes above it. For a <Gaussian random field>, increasing the cutoff adds independent Fourier shells, so the smoothed <density contrast> is a <Brownian motion> when parameterized by its variance. The real-space window is not a localized spherical top-hat, so its halo mass assignment needs a specified convention.

= Press-Schechter halo mass function
{c}
{parent=Press-Schechter formalism}

The Press-Schechter differential abundance is
$$
\frac{dn}{dM}
=-\sqrt{\frac2\pi}\frac{\bar\rho}{M^2}
\nu e^{-\nu^2/2}\frac{d\log\sigma}{d\log M}.
$$

= Scale-free characteristic mass in the Press-Schechter formalism
{parent=Press-Schechter halo mass function}
{title2=$M_*\propto D^{1/s},\quad \sigma(M)\propto M^{-s}$}

If the linearly extrapolated rms amplitude scales as $\sigma(M)\propto M^{-s}$, a fixed peak-height cutoff in the <Press-Schechter halo mass function> has $M_*\propto D^{1/s}$, where $D$ is the <linear growth factor>. The differential prefactor scales as $M^{s-2}$ and the exponential argument as $M^{2s}$. The cutoff convention must be stated when fixing its absolute normalization: $\nu=1$ and $\nu^2/2=1$ select different masses, although their redshift scaling is the same.

= Halo spacing from a differential mass function
{parent=Press-Schechter halo mass function}
{title2=$\bar l=[Mn(M)]^{-1/3}$}

For comoving differential number density $n(M)=dn/dM$, the abundance in an order-unity logarithmic mass bin is approximately $Mn(M)$. A characteristic mean spacing is therefore $[Mn(M)]^{-1/3}$. A cumulative mass fraction cannot replace this number directly; cumulative halo counts require integrating $n(M)$ over mass.

= Press-Schechter abundance growth at fixed mass
{c}
{parent=Press-Schechter halo mass function}
{title2=$\partial_t\ln n\big|_M=(\nu^2-1)\dot D/D$}

For a fixed narrow mass bin with time-independent smoothing slope, the <Press-Schechter halo mass function> has time dependence $n\propto\nu e^{-\nu^2/2}$, with <halo peak height> $\nu\propto D^{-1}$. Differentiating gives the displayed rate. It is positive only for $\nu>1$; the bin can lose objects to higher masses later. Cumulative counts require integrating the differential abundance, and a moving mass boundary adds a selection term.

= Two abundance crossings of the Press-Schechter mass function
{parent=Press-Schechter abundance growth at fixed mass}
{title2=$\mathcal N_{\ln M}\propto\nu e^{-\nu^2/2}$}

At fixed mass, the <Press-Schechter halo mass function> has abundance per logarithmic interval proportional to $\nu e^{-\nu^2/2}$. This increases as $\nu$ falls from infinity to one and then decreases as $\nu$ falls toward zero, reflecting incorporation of small <dark-matter haloes> into larger ones. A target below the maximum therefore has two mathematical crossings if the <linear growth factor> can increase without limit. The large-$\nu$ crossing describes first appearance; the small-$\nu$ crossing is late depletion. Late vacuum domination limits growth, so a formal future crossing need not be reached physically.

= Peak-height calibration from an exponential mass-function cutoff
{parent=Press-Schechter halo mass function}
{title2=$\sigma(M,z)=\frac{\delta_{\rm sc}}{\sqrt2}(M/M_{h,\rm ch})^{-\beta}$}

Defining characteristic mass by an exponential factor $\exp[-(M/M_{h,\rm ch})^{2\beta}]$ fixes its peak height to $\nu=\sqrt2$, rather than one. Hence $\sigma(M_{h,\rm ch},z)=\delta_{\rm sc}/\sqrt2$ and the variance at other masses follows the same power-law scaling. The precise characteristic-mass convention determines this factor.

= Scale-free halo cutoff normalization
{parent=Peak-height calibration from an exponential mass-function cutoff}
{title2=$A=2q\bar\rho_{m,0}M_*^{-q}/\sqrt\pi$}

For $\sigma(M,0)=SM^{-q}$ with $q=(n+3)/6>0$, matching the <Press-Schechter halo mass function> to $AM^{q-2}\exp[-(M/M_*)^{2q}]$ requires $\sigma(M_*,0)=\delta_c(z)/\sqrt2$. The comoving normalization is $A=2q\bar\rho_{m,0}M_*^{-q}/\sqrt\pi$. Consequently both $A$ and $M_*$ evolve with the <linear growth factor>; $M_*\propto D^{1/q}$.

= Power-law Press-Schechter stellar mass function
{parent=Press-Schechter halo mass function}
{title2=$n_*(M_*)\propto M_*^{\beta-2}e^{-(M_*/M_{*,\rm ch})^{2\beta}}$}

For a power-law matter spectrum, smoothed variance scales as $\sigma\propto M^{-\beta}$ with $\beta=(n+3)/6$. Differentiating the Press-Schechter collapsed fraction gives a halo mass function proportional to $M^{\beta-2}\exp[-(M/M_{h,\rm ch})^{2\beta}]$. A constant <stellar-to-halo mass ratio> preserves both exponents and rescales characteristic mass and normalization.

= Peak-background split
{parent=Dark-matter halo}
{wiki}

The peak-background split decomposes density fluctuations into long- and short-wavelength parts. A long overdensity lowers the effective local collapse threshold and thereby changes the abundance of halos.

= Scale-dependent halo bias from local non-Gaussianity
{parent=Peak-background split}
{title2=$\Delta b(k)=2f_{\rm NL}\delta_c b_L/\alpha(k)$}

For a universal abundance depending on <halo peak height>, the <peak-background split> gives a potential-response coefficient $b_\varphi=2f_{\rm NL}\delta_c b_L$, with $b_L$ the <linear Lagrangian halo bias>. If $\delta=\alpha(k)\varphi$, the corresponding deterministic <galaxy bias> correction is $\Delta b=b_\varphi/\alpha(k)$, proportional to $k^{-2}$ on sufficiently large scales. For a physical Eulerian tracer, $b_E=1+b_L$, so $b_\varphi=2f_{\rm NL}\delta_c(b_E-1)$. The deterministic tracer <power spectrum> is $(b_E+\Delta b)^2P_m$; it remains nonnegative even if a negative correction changes the sign of the tracer-matter correlation.

= Linear Lagrangian halo bias
{title2=$b_L(M)$}
{parent=Peak-background split}

Linear Lagrangian halo bias is the fractional response of halo abundance to a long-wavelength overdensity in the initial coordinates. For Press-Schechter halos, $b_L=(\nu^2-1)/\delta_c$.

= Linear Eulerian halo bias
{title2=$b(M)$}
{parent=Linear Lagrangian halo bias}

Mapping matter and halos from their initial positions adds the matter displacement contribution, giving $b=1+b_L$. Mass conservation requires the mass-weighted consistency relation $\int(dn/dM)(M/\bar\rho)b(M)dM=1$.

= Halo model
{parent=Dark-matter halo}
{wiki}

The halo model represents the matter density as a sum of normalized halo profiles and decomposes its power spectrum into same-halo and distinct-halo contributions.

= One-halo term
{title2=$P_{1h}$}
{parent=Halo model}

The one-halo term comes from pairs of mass elements in the same halo:
$$
P_{1h}(k)=\int dM\,\frac{dn}{dM}
\left(\frac{M}{\bar\rho}\right)^2|\widetilde u(k|M)|^2.
$$

= Two-halo term
{title2=$P_{2h}$}
{parent=Halo model}

At linear order in halo correlations,
$$
P_{2h}(k)=
\left[\int dM\,\frac{dn}{dM}\frac{M}{\bar\rho}
b(M)\widetilde u(k|M)\right]^2P_{\rm lin}(k).
$$

= Cosmic microwave background
{title2=CMB}
{c}
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki=Cosmic_microwave_background}

= CMB
{c}
{synonym}

= Reionization
{parent=Cosmic microwave background}
{wiki}

Reionization returns some neutral cosmic gas to an ionized state after <cosmological recombination>. The resulting free electrons increase the <cosmological optical depth> through <Thomson scattering> and create a second contribution to the <cosmological visibility function>.

= Photon-budget reionization threshold
{parent=Reionization}
{title2=$f_{\rm coll}=N_{\rm req}/N_\gamma$}

If each collapsed baryon supplies $N_\gamma$ effective ionizing photons and $N_{\rm req}$ photons per cosmic baryon are required, the threshold collapsed fraction is $f_{\rm coll}=N_{\rm req}/N_\gamma$. The <Press-Schechter formalism> gives $\operatorname{erfc}[\delta_{\rm sc}/(\sqrt2\sigma(M_{\min},z))]=f_{\rm coll}$. Escape losses and recombinations must either be incorporated in the effective yield/requirement or counted separately, not twice.

= Photoevaporation of a dark-matter minihalo
{parent=Reionization}
{title2=$t_{\rm evap}\sim R_h/c_{s,\rm ion}$}

<Photoionization> can heat a shallow halo's gas well above its <virial temperature>. If the heated <sound speed> exceeds the escape-speed scale, the previous <hydrostatic equilibrium> is lost and a pressure-driven outflow removes gas. A crossing time gives a useful estimate, but photon supply, the gas profile, shielding and recombinations can delay the process. This particularly affects haloes whose <virial temperatures> are below the usual atomic cooling threshold and reduces their subsequent <star formation>.

= Reionization damping of cosmic microwave background temperature anisotropy
{parent=Reionization}
{title2=$e^{-\tau_{\rm re}}$}

Ignoring velocities and evolving potentials, a thin <reionization> screen transmits the primary <cosmic microwave background anisotropy> with amplitude $e^{-\tau_{\rm re}}$ and adds a direction-averaged source. A plane wave propagating a distance $\Delta\chi$ from recombination to the screen has monopole factor $j_0(k\Delta\chi)=\sin(k\Delta\chi)/(k\Delta\chi)$. This suppresses the rescattered source at $k\Delta\chi\gg1$, while coherent large-scale modes are unchanged to leading order.

= Primordial-amplitude–optical-depth degeneracy
{parent=Reionization damping of cosmic microwave background temperature anisotropy}
{title2=$A_s e^{-2\tau_{\rm re}}$}

The primary small-scale <cosmic microwave background power spectrum> measures the amplitude $A_s e^{-2\tau_{\rm re}}$, because <reionization> damps each temperature amplitude once. The unaffected large-scale temperature modes are few and limited by <cosmic variance>. Polarization and gravitational lensing provide additional information that partly breaks this degeneracy.

= Cosmic microwave background last-scattering surface
{title2=CMB last-scattering surface}
{c}
{parent=Cosmic microwave background}

The cosmic microwave background last-scattering surface is the shell from which the <photons> observed in the <cosmic microwave background> last scattered around <photon decoupling>.

= Cosmic microwave background anisotropy
{title2=CMB anisotropy}
{c}
{parent=Cosmic microwave background}

Cosmic microwave background anisotropies are direction-dependent perturbations of the relic radiation temperature and polarization.

= Reduced CMB bispectrum
{parent=Cosmic microwave background anisotropy}
{title2=$b_{\ell_1\ell_2\ell_3}$}

A statistically isotropic <cosmic microwave background anisotropy> has $\langle a_{\ell_1m_1}a_{\ell_2m_2}a_{\ell_3m_3}\rangle=\mathcal G^{\ell_1\ell_2\ell_3}_{m_1m_2m_3}b_{\ell_1\ell_2\ell_3}$, where $\mathcal G$ is the integral of the three <spherical harmonics>. The reduced CMB bispectrum removes this angular geometric factor and retains the primordial and transfer-function dependence. A <Sachs-Wolfe projection of a constant bispectrum> is an analytic large-angle example.

= Full-sky cubic bispectrum estimator
{parent=Reduced CMB bispectrum}
{title2=$\hat f_{\mathrm{NL}}=(6F)^{-1}\sum b^1\mathcal G\Theta_1\Theta_2\Theta_3/(C_1C_2C_3)$}

For an isotropic, full-sky temperature map with a fixed bispectrum template $b=f_{\mathrm{NL}}b^1$, the inverse-variance-weighted cubic statistic has normalization $F=\frac16\sum (b^1)^2\mathcal G^2/(C_1C_2C_3)$ over ordered triples. It is unbiased under the linear template relation and has Gaussian <cosmic variance> $1/F$. The factor $1/6$ counts the six cross-triple <Wick contractions>. A removed monopole cancels the nine internal-pairing terms; incomplete sky coverage or anisotropic noise generally require a linear correction.

= Monopole cancellation of internal cubic-estimator contractions
{parent=Full-sky cubic bispectrum estimator}
{title2=$\sum_m(-1)^m\mathcal G^{\ell\ell L}_{m,-m,M}=0\quad(L>0)$}

In a <full-sky cubic bispectrum estimator>, pairwise Gaussian covariance within one triple gives a sum of <Gaunt integrals> with opposite $m$ indices. The <spherical harmonic addition theorem> turns their pair into the constant $(2\ell+1)/(4\pi)$; its integral against $Y_{LM}$ vanishes for $L>0$. A zero temperature monopole removes $L=0$. Thus only the six pairings connecting the two triples survive in the Gaussian variance; isotropic weights are essential for the cancellation.

= Primordial-to-angular bispectrum projection
{parent=Reduced CMB bispectrum}
{title2=$b_{\ell_1\ell_2\ell_3}=(2/\pi)^3\int x^2dx\,\prod_i[k_i^2dk_i\,j_{\ell_i}(k_ix)g_{\ell_i}(k_i)]B$}

Linear cosmological transfer maps the <primordial bispectrum> to the <reduced CMB bispectrum>. A Fourier representation of the momentum delta function and three <Rayleigh plane-wave expansions> separate the radial transfer integrals from a <Gaunt integral>. The six angular factors and three Fourier measures give $(4\pi)^6/(2\pi)^9=(2/\pi)^3$. The observer-position phase cancels by momentum conservation; the remaining radial position is an auxiliary integration variable.

= Sachs-Wolfe projection of a constant bispectrum
{c}
{parent=Reduced CMB bispectrum}
{title2=$b_{\ell_1\ell_2\ell_3}=\frac{4\pi^4f_{\rm NL}\Delta_\zeta^4}{125\prod_i(2\ell_i+1)}\left(\frac1{L+3}+\frac1L\right),\quad L=\sum_i\ell_i$}

For a <constant primordial bispectrum> and large-angle transfer function $\Delta_\ell(k)=j_\ell(kR)/5$, the <spherical Bessel product integral> reduces the radial projection to $\int_0^1r^{L+2}\,dr+\int_1^\infty r^{-L-1}\,dr$. For $L>0$ this equals $1/(L+3)+1/L$, giving the displayed <reduced CMB bispectrum>. All powers of the distance $R$ cancel. Under common large-multipole scaling it behaves as $\ell^{-4}$ at fixed shape; this is angular scale invariance, not a constant angular bispectrum. The all-monopole case $L=0$ has a logarithmically divergent radial tail and is excluded.

= CMB lensing potential
{title2=$\psi$}
{c}
{parent=Cosmic microwave background anisotropy}

The CMB lensing potential is the line-of-sight projection of gravitational potential that remaps observed CMB directions by the deflection angle $\nabla\psi$.

= Cosmic microwave background power spectrum
{title2=CMB power spectrum}
{c}
{parent=Cosmic microwave background anisotropy}

= CMB angular power spectrum
{c}
{synonym}

The CMB angular power spectrum decomposes the variance of temperature or polarization anisotropy by angular multipole. Its large-angle plateau and acoustic peaks encode primordial perturbations and photon-baryon evolution.

= Cosmic variance
{parent=Cosmic microwave background power spectrum}
{wiki}

Cosmic variance is the sampling uncertainty caused by observing only one realization of a cosmological random field. For a Gaussian isotropic full-sky temperature field and an ideal noiseless estimate, $\operatorname{Var}(\widehat C_\ell)=2C_\ell^2/(2\ell+1)$ because there are only $2\ell+1$ independent real angular degrees of freedom.

= Cosmic microwave background polarization
{title2=CMB polarization}
{c}
{parent=Cosmic microwave background anisotropy}
{wiki}

= CMB polarization
{synonym}
{c}

Thomson scattering of a local radiation quadrupole generates linear cosmic microwave background polarization. Its acoustic phase follows the photon velocity and is shifted by one quarter-period relative to the monopole temperature oscillation.

= B-mode polarization
{parent=Cosmic microwave background polarization}
{c}
{title2=$P_B$}

The parity-odd part of linear sky polarization is represented by the pseudoscalar potential $P_B$. At linear order an axisymmetric <scalar cosmological perturbation> gives no physical B mode. Tensor or vector sources and lensing conversion of <E-mode polarization> are outside that scalar-source statement.

= E-mode polarization
{parent=Cosmic microwave background polarization}
{c}
{title2=$P_E$}

The parity-even part of linear sky polarization can be represented by a scalar potential $P_E$ through $Q\pm iU=\eth_{\pm}^2(P_E\pm iP_B)$. It is naturally separated from <B-mode polarization> by the parity of its spherical multipoles. Linear <scalar cosmological perturbations> generate E modes; the potential has an unobservable low-multipole kernel.

= Scalar E-mode radial projection
{parent=E-mode polarization}
{title2=$P_{E,\ell}\propto\Theta_2j_\ell(k\chi_*)/(k\chi_*)^2$}

For a scalar <photon quadrupole> emitted at distance $\chi_*$, the meridian-basis source is $Q\pm iU\propto\Theta_2e^{-ik\chi_*\mu}(1-\mu^2)$. Integrating its scalar-potential equation and removing the <low-multipole kernel of polarization potentials> yields $P_E\propto\sum_{\ell\ge2}(-i)^\ell(2\ell+1)\Theta_2j_\ell(k\chi_*)P_\ell(\mu)/(k\chi_*)^2$, with no scalar $P_B$. The apparent $k^{-2}$ divergence lies in discarded low multipoles; $j_\ell(x)=O(x^\ell)$ makes the physical small-$x$ limit regular.

= Low-multipole kernel of polarization potentials
{parent=E-mode polarization}
{title2=$\ker(\eth^2)=\{\ell=0,1\}$}

Twice-applied spin-raising or lowering annihilates the ordinary spherical-harmonic multipoles $\ell=0,1$. Polarization potentials therefore have a four-dimensional monopole/dipole ambiguity on the sphere. For an axisymmetric mode it is $A+B\cos\theta$. Setting these components to zero defines the observable $\ell\ge2$ potentials without changing $Q$ or $U$.

= Photon temperature multipole
{title2=$\Theta_\ell$}
{parent=Cosmic microwave background anisotropy}

For Fourier wavevector $\mathbf k$ and photon direction $\widehat{\mathbf p}$, the temperature perturbation is expanded as
$$
\Theta(\mu)=\sum_{\ell=0}^{\infty}(-i)^\ell(2\ell+1)
\Theta_\ell P_\ell(\mu),
\qquad
\mu=\widehat{\mathbf k}\mathbin\cdot\widehat{\mathbf p}.
$$

= Photon multipole normalization change
{parent=Photon temperature multipole}

For a fixed angular order $m$, write $\Theta=\sum_\ell C_\ell q_\ell Y_{\ell m}$. Streaming by $ik\mu$ contributes $ik(C_{\ell+1}/C_\ell)a_{\ell+1,m}q_{\ell+1}+ik(C_{\ell-1}/C_\ell)a_{\ell,m}q_{\ell-1}$. Thus rescaling the expansion coefficients also rescales the hierarchy couplings and source amplitudes. Replacing $C_\ell=(-i)^\ell\sqrt{(2\ell+1)/(8\pi)}$ by $\widetilde C_\ell=(-i)^\ell\sqrt{2\pi/(2\ell+1)}$ requires $\widetilde q_\ell=(2\ell+1)q_\ell/(4\pi)$; the temperature field is unchanged. This prevents mixing incompatible <photon temperature multipole> conventions.

= Photon angular temperature moments
{parent=Photon temperature multipole}
{title2=$\delta_\gamma=4\langle\Theta\rangle,\quad v_\gamma^i=3\langle\Theta e^i\rangle$}

Let $\langle F\rangle=(4\pi)^{-1}\int F\,d\Omega$ and $f=\bar f(\epsilon)-\epsilon\bar f'(\epsilon)\Theta(\mathbf e)$ with comoving energy $\epsilon=aE$. If $\epsilon^4\bar f$ vanishes at both integration endpoints, <integration by parts> gives $-\int\epsilon^4\bar f'\,d\epsilon=4\int\epsilon^3\bar f\,d\epsilon$. The <kinetic stress-energy tensor> then gives $\delta_\gamma=4\langle\Theta\rangle$, $v_\gamma^i=3\langle\Theta e^i\rangle$, $\delta P=\bar\rho\delta_\gamma/3$, and $\pi^{ij}=4\bar\rho\langle\Theta(e^ie^j-\delta^{ij}/3)\rangle$. If $\Pi=-\pi$ is used, the last expression changes sign. Frequency independence of $\Theta$ is essential to this common temperature description.

= Photon temperature perturbation
{parent=Photon angular temperature moments}
{title2=$\Theta=\delta T/\bar T$}

A direction-dependent fractional photon temperature change. For a thermal spectrum, $f(\epsilon)=\bar f(\epsilon)-\Theta\epsilon\bar f'(\epsilon)$ is its linear expansion at fixed comoving energy. For a nonthermal spectrum the same ansatz is a brightness dilation, rather than necessarily a thermodynamic temperature. In unweighted <Legendre polynomial> multipoles, $\Theta=\sum_\ell(-i)^\ell\Theta_\ell P_\ell$, the photon density, velocity and <anisotropic stress> are $\delta_\gamma=4\Theta_0$, $v_\gamma=-\Theta_1$ and $\Pi_\gamma=-3\Theta_2/5$.

= Photon monopole
{title2=$\Theta_0$}
{parent=Photon temperature multipole}

The photon monopole is the direction-averaged fractional temperature perturbation. In Newtonian gauge its gravitationally observable effective temperature is the Sachs-Wolfe combination $\Theta_0+\Psi$.

= Sachs-Wolfe combination
{c}
{parent=Photon monopole}

In Newtonian gauge, $\Theta_0+\Psi$ combines the intrinsic photon temperature perturbation with the gravitational redshift at emission. With constant potentials and negligible baryon loading it oscillates as $\cos(kr_s)$ for adiabatic initial conditions.

= Sachs-Wolfe radiation-to-matter matching
{parent=Sachs-Wolfe combination}
{c}
{title2=$(\Theta_0+\psi)_*=-\mathcal R/5$}

For a constant growing adiabatic mode with curvature convention $\mathcal R=-\phi-\mathcal H(\phi'+\mathcal H\phi)/[4\pi Ga^2(\bar\rho+\bar P)]$, $\phi_{\rm rad}=-2\mathcal R/3$ and $\phi_{\rm mat}=-3\mathcal R/5$. The superhorizon <photon continuity equation> conserves $\Theta_0-\phi$. With radiation initial value $\Theta_0=\mathcal R/3$, it gives $\Theta_{0,\rm mat}=2\mathcal R/5$ and $(\Theta_0+\psi)_{\rm mat}=-\mathcal R/5$ when $\psi=\phi$.

= Sachs-Wolfe effect
{c}
{parent=Sachs-Wolfe combination}
{wiki}

The Sachs-Wolfe effect is the large-angle CMB temperature anisotropy produced by intrinsic photon-temperature perturbations and gravitational redshift at last scattering. For an adiabatic mode during matter domination, $\Theta_0+\Psi=\Psi/3$ on superhorizon scales.

= Doppler CMB anisotropy
{c}
{parent=Sachs-Wolfe combination}

The Doppler CMB anisotropy is the line-of-sight velocity contribution from the last-scattering plasma. With the convention used here it contributes $-\widehat{\mathbf n}\mathbin\cdot\mathbf v_e$ to the observed fractional temperature perturbation.

= Integrated Sachs-Wolfe effect
{c}
{parent=Sachs-Wolfe combination}
{wiki}

The integrated Sachs-Wolfe effect is the CMB temperature shift produced when the gravitational potentials traversed by a photon evolve with time. Its line-of-sight source is proportional to $\Phi'+\Psi'$.

= Photon dipole
{title2=$\Theta_1$}
{parent=Photon temperature multipole}

The photon dipole represents the bulk velocity of the radiation. In tight coupling to baryons, $v_b=-3\Theta_1$ under the convention used here.

= Photon quadrupole
{title2=$\Theta_2$}
{parent=Photon temperature multipole}

The photon quadrupole is the leading anisotropy that sources linear polarization through Thomson scattering.

= Photon-baryon fluid
{parent=Cosmic microwave background anisotropy}

Before recombination, frequent Thomson scattering couples photons, electrons, and baryons into an acoustic fluid.

= Baryon Euler equation with Thomson drag
{parent=Photon-baryon fluid}
{title2=$\dot v_b+\mathcal H v_b+k\psi=(\dot\tau/R)(v_b-v_\gamma)$}

For the velocity convention $v_\gamma=-\Theta_1$ and $\dot\tau<0$, momentum-conserving <Thomson scattering> gives $\dot v_b+\mathcal H v_b+k\psi=(\dot\tau/R)(v_b-v_\gamma)$. Here $R$ is the <baryon loading parameter>. Its collision force is opposite to the photon force when both are weighted by their enthalpies.

= Baryon loading parameter
{parent=Photon-baryon fluid}
{title2=$R=3\bar\rho_b/(4\bar\rho_\gamma)$}

The inertia ratio of the <photon-baryon fluid> is $R=3\bar\rho_b/(4\bar\rho_\gamma)$. Photon momentum is weighted by the radiation enthalpy $4\bar\rho_\gamma/3$, so this ratio controls momentum exchange, the common sound speed and the offset of the acoustic oscillator. Background conservation gives $R\propto a$ and $\dot R=\mathcal H R$.

= Thomson scattering
{c}
{parent=Photon-baryon fluid}
{wiki}

Thomson scattering is the low-energy elastic scattering of electromagnetic radiation by a free charged particle. Before cosmological recombination, repeated photon-electron scattering couples photons to the baryon velocity and damps higher photon multipoles.

= Tight-coupling approximation
{parent=Photon-baryon fluid}
{wiki}

= Tight coupling
{synonym}

The tight-coupling approximation expands the photon-baryon Boltzmann equations in powers of $k/\Gamma$ and $\mathcal H/\Gamma$, where $\Gamma$ is the Thomson scattering rate.

= Temperature-only tight-coupling quadrupole
{parent=Tight-coupling approximation}
{title2=$\Theta_2=-20k\Theta_1/(27\tau^{\prime})$}

For $\Theta(\mu)=\sum_\ell(-i)^\ell\Theta_\ell P_\ell(\mu)$ and a Thomson collision operator omitting polarization feedback, the quadrupole relaxes with rate $(9/10)\Gamma_T$, where $\Gamma_T=-\tau'>0$. Balancing its leading streaming source gives $\Theta_2=20k\Theta_1/(27\Gamma_T)$. This differs from <photon quadrupole in tight coupling with polarization>; the two formulas expand different collision operators.

= Photon-baryon acoustic oscillator
{parent=Tight-coupling approximation}
{title2=$\omega^2=k^2/[3(1+R)]$}

With $v_b=v_\gamma$ at leading order and $c_s^2=1/[3(1+R)]$, the <photon continuity equation> and total Euler equation give $\ddot\delta_\gamma+\dot R\dot\delta_\gamma/(1+R)+k^2\delta_\gamma/[3(1+R)]=4\ddot\phi+4\dot R\dot\phi/(1+R)-4k^2\psi/3$. The <baryon loading parameter> changes inertia and shifts the gravitational equilibrium. The leading equations omit diffusion damping.

= Photon-baryon sound speed
{title2=$c_s^2$}
{parent=Tight-coupling approximation}

For baryon-loading ratio $R=3\rho_b/(4\rho_\gamma)$, the photon-baryon sound speed is $c_s^2=1/[3(1+R)]$.

= Photon-baryon velocity slip
{title2=$s=v_\gamma-v_b$}
{parent=Tight-coupling approximation}

The velocity slip is the difference between the bulk velocities of photons and baryons. <Thomson scattering> suppresses it at a rate $\Gamma(1+R^{-1})$, where $R=3\rho_b/(4\rho_\gamma)$ and $\Gamma=-\dot\tau>0$. Its first correction in the <tight-coupling approximation> produces heat-conduction damping of acoustic waves.

= Photon quadrupole in tight coupling with polarization
{parent=Tight-coupling approximation}

For the expansion $\Theta(\mu)=\sum_\ell(-i)^\ell\Theta_\ell P_\ell(\mu)$ without a $(2\ell+1)$ factor, the leading polarization relation is $E_2=-\sqrt6\,\Theta_2/4$. The <photon Boltzmann hierarchy> then gives $\Theta_2=-8k\Theta_1/(9\dot\tau)$. This small <photon quadrupole> supplies the shear-viscosity part of <Silk damping>.

= Photon-baryon diffusion damping equation
{parent=Tight-coupling approximation}

When gravity and expansion can be neglected and $R$ is constant, the <photon monopole> satisfies
$$
\ddot\Theta_0+
\frac{k^2}{3(1+R)\Gamma}
\left(\frac{R^2}{1+R}+\frac{16}{15}\right)\dot\Theta_0+
\frac{k^2}{3(1+R)}\Theta_0=0.
$$
The first damping contribution comes from <photon-baryon velocity slip>; the second comes from the <photon quadrupole>. This is a local form of <Silk damping>.

= Shear-only photon diffusion damping
{parent=Photon-baryon diffusion damping equation}
{title2=$k_D^{-2}=\frac2{15}\int\tau_c\,d\tau$}

For the simplified quadrupole equation $\sigma_\gamma'+4k^2\theta_\gamma/15=-\sigma_\gamma/\tau_c$, neglecting its time derivative and gravitational driving gives $\sigma_\gamma=-\tau_c\delta_\gamma'/5$. A radiation-dominated inertia then obeys $\delta_\gamma''+4\tau_ck^2\delta_\gamma'/15+k^2\delta_\gamma/3=0$. On slowly varying subhorizon scales the amplitude is damped by $\exp[-(2/15)k^2\int\tau_cd\tau]$. This coefficient belongs to that truncated collision model, rather than the polarization-inclusive <Silk damping> equation.

= Sound horizon
{title2=$r_s$}
{parent=Photon-baryon fluid}
{wiki}

The comoving sound horizon is the distance traveled by an acoustic wave,
$$
r_s(\eta)=\int_0^\eta c_s(\eta')\,d\eta'.
$$

= Cosmic microwave background diffusion damping
{title2=CMB diffusion damping}
{c}
{parent=Photon-baryon fluid}

= Silk damping
{c}
{synonym}

Cosmic microwave background diffusion damping is the suppression of small-angular-scale anisotropy as photons random-walk out of overdense regions during the finite-width recombination epoch. It produces an approximately exponential damping tail in the <cosmic microwave background power spectrum> at multipoles $\ell\gtrsim1000$.

= Adiabatic initial conditions
{parent=Cosmic microwave background anisotropy}

= Adiabatic cosmological perturbation
{synonym}

Adiabatic initial conditions perturb every species by the same local time shift, so their relative number-density ratios are initially unperturbed. Photon acoustic modes then begin predominantly as displacement modes with cosine phase.

Equivalently, $\delta\rho_i/\dot{\bar\rho}_i$ is independent of the component $i$. The <non-adiabatic pressure perturbation> then vanishes, and the <comoving curvature perturbation> is conserved on <superhorizon scales> when anisotropic stress and gradient terms can be neglected.

= Cosmic microwave background acoustic peak
{title2=CMB acoustic peak}
{c}
{parent=Cosmic microwave background anisotropy}

Cosmic microwave background acoustic peaks arise from photon-baryon oscillations caught at successive extrema at recombination. Their approximate phase is set by $kr_s=n\pi$ and their angular positions measure the sound horizon relative to the distance to last scattering.

= Adiabatic acoustic-peak spacing
{parent=Cosmic microwave background acoustic peak}
{title2=$\ell_n\simeq n\pi\chi_*/r_s(\eta_*)$}

<Adiabatic initial conditions> select coherent cosine phases of the <photon-baryon acoustic oscillator>. Its extrema at last scattering satisfy $kr_s\simeq n\pi$ and project to $\ell\simeq k\chi_*$. Thus $\ell_n\simeq n\pi\chi_*/r_s(\eta_*)$, with comoving <sound horizon> $r_s=\int d\eta/\sqrt{3(1+R)}$. Projection, driving and finite last-scattering width modify the exact peak locations.

= Free-streaming photon Boltzmann equation
{c}
{parent=Cosmic microwave background anisotropy}

In the absence of collisions, a photon phase-space density is constant along its geodesic. Linearizing this statement gives a first-order transport equation for the direction-dependent temperature perturbation.

= Photon Boltzmann hierarchy
{title2=$\Theta_\ell$ hierarchy}
{parent=Free-streaming photon Boltzmann equation}

Expanding the direction-dependent photon temperature in <Legendre polynomials> turns its transport equation into an infinite hierarchy. Free streaming couples each multipole $\Theta_\ell$ only to its neighbours $\Theta_{\ell-1}$ and $\Theta_{\ell+1}$.

= Vector photon Boltzmann hierarchy
{parent=Photon Boltzmann hierarchy}
{title2=$\Theta_\ell^{(\pm)}$}

With $B_i^{(\pm)}=iB^{(\pm)}m_i^{(\pm)}/\sqrt2$ and the same convention for baryon velocity, choose $\Theta^{(\pm)}=\sum_{\ell\geq1}(-i)^\ell\sqrt{2\pi/(2\ell+1)}\Theta_\ell^{(\pm)}Y_{\ell,\pm1}$. The <spherical-harmonic streaming recurrence> gives
$$
\dot\Theta_\ell^{(\pm)}+k\left[\frac{\sqrt{(\ell+1)^2-1}}{2\ell+3}\Theta_{\ell+1}^{(\pm)}-\frac{\sqrt{\ell^2-1}}{2\ell-1}\Theta_{\ell-1}^{(\pm)}\right]=\dot\tau\left(1-\frac{\delta_{\ell2}}{10}\right)\Theta_\ell^{(\pm)}\mp(\dot B^{(\pm)}+\dot\tau v_b^{(\pm)})\delta_{\ell1}.
$$
This is the temperature-only <Thomson scattering> hierarchy without polarization feedback, with optical depth to the observer satisfying $\dot\tau<0$. A <photon multipole normalization change> changes the denominators and the dipole-source prefactor. The lower coupling vanishes at $\ell=1$, so no vector monopole occurs.

= Photon continuity equation
{parent=Photon Boltzmann hierarchy}
{title2=$\dot\delta_\gamma+\tfrac43\nabla\cdot\mathbf v_\gamma-4\dot\phi=0$}

In <Newtonian gauge in cosmology>, the <free-streaming photon Boltzmann equation> is $\dot\Theta+\mathbf e\cdot\nabla\Theta=\dot\phi-\mathbf e\cdot\nabla\psi$. Angular averaging and the <photon angular temperature moments> give the displayed <continuity equation>. Its dipole yields the <photon Euler equation>, with the sign of the <anisotropic stress> term determined by the stress convention.

= Photon Euler equation
{parent=Photon Boltzmann hierarchy}

The dipole member of the photon Boltzmann hierarchy is the photon Euler equation. In Newtonian gauge and without collisions,
$$
\Theta_1'=\frac{k}{3}(\Theta_0+\Psi-2\Theta_2).
$$

= Line-of-sight solution for free-streaming photons
{parent=Free-streaming photon Boltzmann equation}

The line-of-sight solution integrates the gravitational source along the unperturbed photon trajectory. In Fourier space the integrating factor $e^{ik\mu\eta}$ accounts for free streaming between emission and observation.

= Photon Boltzmann equation with Thomson scattering
{c}
{parent=Free-streaming photon Boltzmann equation}

At linear order in Newtonian gauge, free streaming, gravitational redshift, and Thomson scattering combine into a first-order transport equation for the photon temperature perturbation. The collision term drives the radiation toward its monopole plus the electron bulk-velocity dipole.

= Cosmological optical depth
{title2=$\tau(\eta)$}
{parent=Photon Boltzmann equation with Thomson scattering}

The optical depth from conformal time $\eta$ to observation at $\eta_0$ is
$$
\tau(\eta)=\int_\eta^{\eta_0}\Gamma(\eta')\,d\eta'.
$$
The factor $e^{-\tau(\eta)}$ is the probability that a photon travels from $\eta$ to the observer without another scattering.

= Cosmological visibility function
{title2=$g(\eta)$}
{parent=Cosmological optical depth}
{wiki=Recombination_(cosmology)#Visibility_function}

The visibility function
$$
g(\eta)=\partial_\eta e^{-\tau(\eta)}
=\Gamma(\eta)e^{-\tau(\eta)}
$$
is the probability density for the conformal time of a CMB photon's last scattering.

= Cosmic microwave background line-of-sight solution
{title2=CMB line-of-sight solution}
{c}
{parent=Cosmological visibility function}

The CMB line-of-sight solution separates sharply localized last-scattering sources, weighted by the <cosmological visibility function>, from integrated gravitational sources weighted by $e^{-\tau}$ along the photon trajectory.

= Tensor CMB line-of-sight source
{parent=Cosmic microwave background line-of-sight solution}
{title2=$\Theta=-\tfrac12\int\dot h_{ij}e^ie^j d\eta$}

Ignoring the last-scattering angular source and later scattering, the tensor-induced temperature anisotropy is $\Theta=-\tfrac12\int_{\eta_*}^{\eta_0}\dot h_{ij}(\eta,\mathbf x_0-(\eta_0-\eta)\mathbf e)e^ie^j d\eta$. An instantaneous <cosmological visibility function> alone leaves a scattering source at last scattering; leading tensor <tight coupling> supplies the additional reason to neglect it.

= Cosmological distance measure
{parent=Cosmology}

A cosmological distance measure converts an observed redshift, angle, flux, or travel time into a distance in an expanding spacetime. Different measures answer different operational questions and therefore need not agree numerically.

= Angular diameter distance
{title2=$d_A$}
{parent=Cosmological distance measure}
{wiki}

The angular diameter distance is defined by $d_A=x/\theta$ for a small transverse physical size $x$ subtending angle $\theta$. In a spatially flat <Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric>,
$$
d_A(z)=\frac{\chi(z)}{1+z},
$$
where $\chi$ is the <comoving radial distance>.

= Angular diameter distance turnover
{parent=Angular diameter distance}

In a standard expanding cosmology, $d_A(z)$ initially increases from zero but eventually decreases because the factor $(1+z)^{-1}$ dominates the bounded comoving distance. Beyond its maximum, a fixed physical object subtends a larger angle at a larger redshift.

= Cosmological standard ruler
{parent=Cosmological distance measure}

A cosmological standard ruler is a feature with a fixed known or redshift-independent physical or comoving size. Comparing its observed angular and radial extents constrains the expansion history even when its absolute size is treated as a nuisance parameter.

= Baryon acoustic oscillation
{title2=BAO}
{parent=Cosmological standard ruler}
{wiki}

Baryon acoustic oscillations are the relic spatial scale of sound waves in the pre-recombination <photon-baryon fluid>. The scale appears both as a preferred separation in galaxy clustering and as oscillatory structure in the <cosmological density power spectrum>.

= Transverse baryon acoustic oscillation measurement
{title2=Transverse BAO measurement}
{parent=Baryon acoustic oscillation}

For a comoving BAO scale $\chi_d$ perpendicular to the line of sight in a spatially flat universe,
$$
\Delta\theta=\frac{\chi_d}{\chi(z)}.
$$
Its redshift dependence constrains the shape of the distance--redshift relation.

= Radial baryon acoustic oscillation measurement
{title2=Radial BAO measurement}
{parent=Baryon acoustic oscillation}

For a small comoving BAO separation parallel to the line of sight,
$$
\Delta z=H(z)\chi_d
$$
in units with $c=1$. Combining this with a transverse measurement eliminates the unknown ruler size.

= Baryon acoustic oscillation in the matter power spectrum
{title2=BAO in the matter power spectrum}
{parent=Baryon acoustic oscillation}

The matter power spectrum contains low-amplitude oscillatory features because baryons preserve their pre-recombination acoustic phase and later transfer part of it gravitationally to the combined matter distribution.

= Alcock-Paczyński test
{c}
{parent=Cosmological standard ruler}
{wiki}

The Alcock-Paczyński test compares radial and transverse distance conversion using a feature that is statistically isotropic in comoving space.

= Alcock-Paczyński parameter
{title2=$F_{\rm AP}(z)=H(z)\chi(z)$}
{c}
{parent=Alcock-Paczyński test}

The Alcock-Paczyński parameter compares radial and transverse distance conversion at one redshift. An intrinsically isotropic comoving feature obeys
$$
\frac{\Delta z}{\Delta\theta}=H(z)\chi(z),
$$
so its unknown absolute size cancels.

= Lambda-CDM model
{title2=$\Lambda$CDM model}
{c}
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki}

The Lambda-CDM model combines a <cosmological constant> with <cold dark matter> and the standard baryonic and radiation components in an expanding <Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric>.

= Dark matter
{parent=Cosmology}
{wiki}

Dark matter is matter inferred from its gravitational effects that does not emit, absorb, or scatter enough electromagnetic radiation to account for those effects as ordinary baryonic matter.

= Cold dark matter
{title2=CDM}
{parent=Dark matter}
{wiki}

Cold dark matter is nonrelativistic early enough that its primordial free-streaming length is small compared with the cosmological structures of interest.

= Warm dark matter
{title2=WDM}
{parent=Dark matter}
{wiki}

Warm dark matter has a primordial <collisionless free-streaming> length large enough to suppress the formation of low-mass haloes, while remaining nonrelativistic early enough to cluster on galactic and larger scales.

= Matter-radiation equality
{parent=Cosmology}

Matter-radiation equality is the epoch at which the background matter and radiation densities are equal. Its horizon wavenumber $k_{\rm eq}$ sets the turnover scale of the cold-dark-matter transfer function.

= Matter-radiation equality scale
{title2=$k_{\rm eq}$}
{parent=Matter-radiation equality}

The matter-radiation equality scale is the comoving horizon wavenumber at equality. Modes at larger wavenumber entered during radiation domination and had their matter growth suppressed.
